43f77b" "q-73107/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-73107/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-73107/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-73107/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-73107/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-73107/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-73107/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73107" :question/id #uuid "84a6a876-1976-59db-8f65-20eb371fea76" :question/math-academy-id "q-73107" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/88ea6a8d8a41b75aa433a17a2d861d32cf5f9eeb8e4c0995ef1eca2d12d1107a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. - The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\nII. - The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\nIII. - The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-73107/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73107"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81426/answer/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "4ec58839-1ccd-5c76-8dcf-2d78ccda033c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81426/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b1f5c1c-4daf-5f2e-86d7-96ebd535fe07" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-81426/answer/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer-field/choices ["q-81426/answer/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81426" :question/id #uuid "a9293d48-2b7e-5505-a66c-48465f5a4479" :question/math-academy-id "q-81426" :question/problem "Use the $p$-test to determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{5n}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThis is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $\\frac{3}{5}$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81426/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ead28b84-1f92-510d-87b3-07eb9a394622"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81426"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353317/field-1/0be271702c359235c3e2238f4d79c8c88dc17f9fde52028ad501e4fb60c8259d" :answer/id #uuid "f62e55a8-541f-5460-be29-38471e8e82e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{729}2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353317/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "293eeead-4ae0-5c88-9e68-19a4cafcedc9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353317/field-1/0be271702c359235c3e2238f4d79c8c88dc17f9fde52028ad501e4fb60c8259d" :answer-field/choices ["q-353317/field-1/0be271702c359235c3e2238f4d79c8c88dc17f9fde52028ad501e4fb60c8259d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353317" :question/id #uuid "c5b3ef5f-3bcf-5c98-a527-aed005bdad3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-353317" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a third term equal to $27$ and a sixth term equal to $1.$ Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the terms of this sequence. Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{3}=27$ and ${a}_{6}=1.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=3$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}(r)^{2} \\\\ 27 & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\ 27 & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{9}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =243\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =243(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-353317/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353317"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e96e100f-1eee-5783-967e-3be91b260a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5b5c0b65-3e2c-5da1-9db8-a08c28ba2700" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b44a206e-e61a-52c0-8e31-7eb08be8d568" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "90ce0177-07a5-5771-8645-06d784001a87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "07ad044a-9941-5828-9cc0-10232340ffb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "c0365355-b02b-554c-81ea-383f5d75c51e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "8c3f0dd1-7101-5e8a-8a4d-def94257f103" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "a8a2d0f3-dc8a-5d03-aaa4-9fec605441cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "21f9f03d-b6f6-51d4-ad67-325a813dfd9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143193/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-143193/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-143193/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-143193/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-143193/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-143193/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-143193/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-143193/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-143193/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143193" :question/id #uuid "9fa88825-edb5-50fd-b953-cabd8cb91f63" :question/math-academy-id "q-143193" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=2{n}^{5}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=2{n}^{5}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(n+1)^{5}}{2{n}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{5}}{{n}^{5}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{5} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}^{5} \\\\  & ={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ we have that $\\frac{1}{n}>0.$ Therefore, it follows that\n\n$$\n(1+\\frac{1}{n})>1,\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\nL={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{5}>1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-143193/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90b485f8-a07d-55f4-8cde-f699070eeae7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143193"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7355086d-9d4d-5f5a-b683-0bacd7fb9ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b0427245-ebbd-55ba-988a-40d2853b3993" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "9f7200f1-0aaf-524d-80e2-9d3389d8f571" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b3678b0b-031a-50f1-90bd-96251cf9c108" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "b4d31ee5-71ea-5b18-97d9-6a60494d5200" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "85276abb-4321-52ba-8356-271bb26942b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "c090be31-e0fe-53b2-9454-19182ededf52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "4144a272-db25-51ee-9623-a0f734b10e7e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f94aaaa-9bc1-5a1d-a06c-4a240f08215e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96669/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-96669/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-96669/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-96669/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-96669/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-96669/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-96669/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-96669/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-96669/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96669" :question/id #uuid "e356353f-6fc7-5ad8-bdec-cdea5919a2a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96669" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{‴}+(\\operatorname{sin}x){y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{y}^{''}{y}^{'}+3xy=4{y}^{'}$\nIII. - ${({y}^{'})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=y$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-96669/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96669"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124087/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f7c2455e-888e-590f-95aa-15b1e28f2407" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124087/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "49a5bf3c-9ed0-57ba-9f2d-c99903851ecc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124087/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "cbd839a1-bff7-5b24-8a00-17e92901903a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124087/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00