361c82d3d038fc4d03ac7649411abbb66628aa61700fe97ab48fa8810f" "q-152928/selection/9d869808e53a23386bbcec29e7c70dcf2a3a3621d5ff5dccf72ada0187b39692" "q-152928/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152928" :question/id #uuid "4dfe3453-c9ac-58b3-b12c-8c3fa9577459" :question/math-academy-id "q-152928" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x+12, & x\\ne -2 \\\\ 4, & x=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)$\nIV. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(4x+12) \\\\  & =4(-2)+12 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(4x+12) \\\\  & =4(-2)+12 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=4\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=4=f(-2).$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-152928/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152928"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104159/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "d8503c3b-e02d-5ad3-9012-3f65dcd1c70a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104159/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "04bf3706-2aec-5d95-8407-21ebd5fa2088" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-104159/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-104159/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104159" :question/id #uuid "146303a4-6968-5cfe-bc52-997b521163c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104159" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9,$ then what is $f(\\sqrt{3})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3})={lim}_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104159/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104159"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342250/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "3f3ed0bf-073a-501c-86f6-0f39bd003375" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342250/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ddc09d83-a77a-505c-8bd4-a9a8e6e2d69e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342250/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342250/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342250" :question/id #uuid "c3cbfacf-5614-5092-a752-7217dd8c35fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-342250" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=7,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}{(4{x}^{2}-5f(x))}^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(4{x}^{2}-5f(x))}^{3} & ={(4(3)^{2}-5(7))}^{3} \\\\  & =(36-35)^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342250/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342250"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342129/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "9b7dbfc0-99ea-5a67-8019-85bfc7b6319b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342129/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed926169-2d5e-5d4a-9f49-f2e72c530c72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342129/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-342129/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342129" :question/id #uuid "818caafd-e5ae-5f20-aaeb-0a80ded6953b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342129" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=5,$ then\n\n$\\frac{10-f(0)}{(f(0))^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(0)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=5.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10-f(0)}{(f(0))^{2}} & =\\frac{10-5}{(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342129/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342129"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342268/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "f66fcfca-6e1c-57fe-bd33-f02b0d9f6e94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342268/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "292c1ad5-ee4b-5ef2-9146-3da9b38c1d78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342268/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342268/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342268" :question/id #uuid "41af6c8a-0117-5f6c-bdfe-b0a3b0975805" :question/math-academy-id "q-342268" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=-3,$ then\n\n${[(f(4))^{2}-11]}^{4}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(4)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[(f(4))^{2}-11]}^{4} & ={[(-3)^{2}-11]}^{4} \\\\  & =(-2)^{4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342268/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342268"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117575/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "a8ce3e53-e1f5-5591-a4eb-114c90c884d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117575/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f3c7636-e490-520a-b803-7e863dbc2962" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-117575/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-117575/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117575" :question/id #uuid "8910d732-402d-5d51-b841-b7875aafc6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117575" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-5\\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\pi )-3?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-5,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-5|=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117575/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117575"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117568/answer/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "0e2e8619-b6be-52f0-85e5-f414a28c3328" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117568/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ab1859-ecad-56e1-a2c1-455c5286b3f3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-117568/answer/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-117568/answer/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117568" :question/id #uuid "78732451-2db0-5c4e-aa11-0192b0cac8c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117568" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x-1)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=\\frac{3}{2},$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|\\frac{3}{2}|=\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-117568/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117568"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-236892/y/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1f28b567-f55c-56d8-8ef7-93dc314e8c2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-236892/y" :answer-field/id #uuid "858229eb-e174-5779-a638-38be37667ff3" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-236892/y/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-236892/y/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-236892" :question/id #uuid "5768bcd4-3be1-5ac3-bde3-3e84a242be37" :question/math-academy-id "q-236892" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $0$ and a maximum value of $4.$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{0+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-236892/y"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-236892"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-236903/y/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3" :answer/id #uuid "63dc6224-6431-586a-a206-9ad0db46ec63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-236903/y" :answer-field/id #uuid "d782ac80-c610-5fd0-b32b-b97970e2efb6" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-236903/y/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3" :answer-field/choices ["q-236903/y/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-236903" :question/id #uuid "968a76bd-d1b3-5d50-b7ae-97e1392795e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-236903" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $-14$ and a maximum value of $-4.$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-14+(-4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18}{2} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=-9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-236903/y"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-236903"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339731/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "7b881693-bfa3-5064-accc-6349d95b1535" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339731/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "512c6bf0-66f2-5e2b-aebd-63701a637a3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339731/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-339731/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339731/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "3e8d4895-61e5-563e-9628-0b60ef993e8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339731/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "db98dfee-cb1f-54a6-a984-ac6ce65a2605" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339731/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-339731/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339731" :question/id #uuid "77b4e96f-5072-5275-8cf8-c358fd213cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-339731" :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ 5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x) & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\pi -\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge \\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}(5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & =5+2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5+2(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339731/field-1" "q-339731/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339731"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-36695/left-limit/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "4ca66b35-65c0-58bf-b873-78af0e82f941" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36695/left-limit" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d801d0-4de9-5e1e-8201-11f884ef6c79" :answer-field/key "left-limit" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-36695/left-limit/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-36695/left-limit/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36695/right-limit/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "b28aa2e0-30f0-557d-8d6a-a9a9b931d232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36695/right-limit" :answer-field/id #uuid "f54c2141-d85b-57b8-b293-61693a3d01dd" :answer-field/key "right-limit" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-36695/right-limit/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-36695/right-limit/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36695" :question/id #uuid "98dc4d3c-dae5-5596-b008-46a065491e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36695" :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=0$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}x+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLeft limit: {{left-limit}}\n\nRight limit: {{right-limit}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(-x+1) \\\\  & =-(0)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}x+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}0+1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-36695/left-limit" "q-36695/right-limit"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36695"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339964/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "46387cb9-77c6-5309-9165-30b1b22f3401" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339964/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "260b2018-b639-5013-ac54-7fe6b71f58fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339964/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-339964/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339964/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "3eb759e9-9584-5702-9892-b88d8e48dfa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339964/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "00e11637-23e5-564b-a2cf-551237109653" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339964/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-339964/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339964" :question/id #uuid "c4bb0f1e-ed08-55ef-acdf-b88dc863724a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339964" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+3x-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=1$ from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+3x-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}[(x-2)^{2}+3x-5] \\\\  & =(1-2)^{2}+3(1)-5 \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+3-5 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=4$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}(4))+2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339964/field-1" "q-339964/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339964"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99965/right-limit-at-0/8d3ecab068cde52d8abdbf7b57cad4f74ea7fb4a6b5cd85bf418f95edcdd3063" :answer/id #uuid "97b5afec-6aea-5b34-aa8e-b918a052b12a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99965/right-limit-at-0" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c0abca7-57dc-510b-b6ed-2d60e726d21c" :answer-field/key "right-limit-at-0" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-99965/right-limit-at-0/8d3ecab068cde52d8abdbf7b57cad4f74ea7fb4a6b5cd85bf418f95edcdd3063" :answer-field/choices ["q-99965/right-limit-at-0/8d3ecab068cde52d8abdbf7b57cad4f74ea7fb4a6b5cd85bf418f95edcdd3063"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99965/right-limit-at-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "2b83a115-55ea-5df1-8226-796d68ded253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99965/right-limit-at-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a774014d-cc48-5612-b573-ac8602b5cf5a" :answer-field/key "right-limit-at-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-99965/right-limit-at-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-99965/right-limit-at-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99965" :question/id #uuid "135d935c-2bd7-5669-bc52-6f55c09e6c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-99965" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRight limit at 0: {{right-limit-at-0}}\n\nRight limit at 1: {{right-limit-at-1}}\n\nIf a requested limit does not exist, enter \"Does not exist\"." :question/worked-solution "The right-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,1).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99965/right-limit-at-0" "q-99965/right-limit-at-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99965"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-340307/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "7528b06a-a2d6-5ebb-bfb3-2d4a4bd9e29b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340307/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fec6cb4-e826-5dbe-bb32-219c987b8a6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-340307/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-340307/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340307" :question/id #uuid "7ddd0688-dbb1-5edb-8d08-525b26a06be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-340307" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}c-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ to be equal to $8,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c-x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}c-x \\\\  & =c-(-1) \\\\  & =c+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{x}^{2}-2x-1 \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-2(-1)-1 \\\\  & =1+2-1 \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ c+1 & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340307/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340307"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-36744/b/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "32b89fbf-b014-5640-a34d-31a623a93f88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36744/b" :answer-field/id #uuid "dbfb6eaf-b9f3-53be-be85-c1121d3491a0" :answer-field/key "b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-36744/b/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-36744/b/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36744/c/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "6c8cbf0f-8ac1-5598-b1c6-00543cf63c2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36744/c" :answer-field/id #uuid "e9df87ad-3a19-5812-8a5e-fef466e5ea8d" :answer-field/key "c" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-36744/c/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-36744/c/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36744" :question/id #uuid "03dad123-1833-5311-a4f2-ae8087879f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-36744" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+cx+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$\n\n$b$: {{b}}\n\n$c$: {{c}}" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-3x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x+b) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-3(2)+b \\\\  & =4-6+b \\\\  & =b-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =1 \\\\ b-2 & =1 \\\\ b & =1+2 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+cx+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+cx+1) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2}+c(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+2c+1 \\\\  & =2c-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =1 \\\\ 2c-3 & =1 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1+3}{2} \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36744/b" "q-36744/c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36744"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a56ef72d-5597-55cb-9d06-627f7b68b3dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "dec88327-3398-521e-bf43-d73f8b93ee45" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ffaf444c-54a3-5b3a-aced-cf563009d2c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "a78c54fe-b2e5-5eb5-83ac-6e9175fcf819" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "930f846a-0de4-5297-bf95-95ab3ae4dead" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "30a05358-5f44-575b-a9de-ce1822364fc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "7d9b7624-8b63-5c92-9e87-99970e683bdd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "19b0761b-be8e-589d-b2c9-ce99c5c71159" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b6e0b01-1512-5734-99de-0dd680458bf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112591/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-112591/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-112591/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-112591/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-112591/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-112591/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-112591/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-112591/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-112591/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112591" :question/id #uuid "f8190dc0-5e3e-595b-8144-fb26d1f0ac64" :question/math-academy-id "q-112591" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $x=-2$ is a simple root.\nII. - $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\nIII. - $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112591/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112591"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-302976/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "f8ee5394-b2dd-50d9-b7b0-77c1f5409d4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-302976/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "656f0b5e-8a20-5b17-b807-c16226a1ba77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-302976/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-302976/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-302976" :question/id #uuid "cf9923c2-2a75-5d31-b240-73c84287294f" :question/math-academy-id "q-302976" :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m}=\\sqrt{5},$ what is the value of constant $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we know that the limit of $\\sqrt{5x-m}$ exists, we can substitute $x=2$ into this expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m} & =\\sqrt{5(2)-m} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $\\sqrt{5},$ we can equate the above with $\\sqrt{5},$ and then solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10-m} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10-m & =5 \\\\ m & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-302976/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3eae4ea-fb38-5efa-843e-783327b82702"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-302976"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337889/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "3d50905c-b55c-5093-a351-7a4a8afb1004" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337889/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc19a03-5ebe-5d9b-8e53-685a0cef23f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-337889/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-337889/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337889" :question/id #uuid "eed63dc5-4938-533a-9ddd-3ca6174a3bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-337889" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{8(x-1)}+2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{8(x-1)}+2) & =\\sqrt[3]{8(1-1)}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{0}+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337889/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631dd0e-a893-5099-8b52-e7a543e7f783"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337889"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99782/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "50270b86-8bb2-51d5-82d9-8fdc05982042" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99782/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "85f844fb-7e4e-5a95-9c72-fb00470fcfd3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-99782/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-99782/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99782" :question/id #uuid "4794869c-06a9-5c29-93c1-d60c4f9c5809" :question/math-academy-id "q-99782" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{4(-1)+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99782/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631dd0e-a893-5099-8b52-e7a543e7f783"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99782"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336433/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "45d1a1b1-aaed-55d9-93ee-6e710a836e33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336433/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "dbbe1e2b-3bac-52e4-9a4d-e5eeb164f79a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336433/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-336433/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336433" :question/id #uuid "2eb4629d-fc7e-5d92-9fc1-95bd60f68d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-336433" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by\n$v(t)=\\frac{t+3}{t+1}.$\nCalculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhen $a=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ the velocity is $v=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t+3}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot (t+1)-(t+3)\\cdot (1)}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{t+1-t-3}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ (t+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ t+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ t & =-3,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $t=1$ since $t\\ge 0.$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=1,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{1+3}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\text{ m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-336433/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336433"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-2358/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "1add7981-398b-563f-aaac-42b2a6611944" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2358/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee2b73d0-b94d-51bd-8d0a-191182b5bec9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-2358/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-2358/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2358" :question/id #uuid "5a37d360-2bb9-53ba-8a4f-830d6a3e6ba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-2358" :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=6{t}^{3}-19{t}^{2}+13,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the time when the particle's acceleration is equal to $a=70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{3}-19{t}^{2}+13) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-38t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(18{t}^{2}-38t) \\\\  & =36t-38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=70,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70 & =36t-38 \\\\ 36t & =108 \\\\ t & =3\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time when the acceleration is $a=70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $3$ seconds." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-2358/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2358"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-46975/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "62b06ea6-f6ee-5ce4-8188-756ab212a69e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-46975/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "511ca430-2669-5d58-94b7-ee16594c46e4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-46975/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-46975/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-46975" :question/id #uuid "5b240cab-24fa-5335-a943-6cdfe8310c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-46975" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0,\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-46975/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-46975"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334171/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "1c4fbdff-9f6e-56e1-a6c8-7bcfab6bbe8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334171/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "13b5cebc-ed49-5730-9a88-a8e0f04c3d34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334171/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334171/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334171" :question/id #uuid "0de860f6-11f7-5006-bbaa-d1ca0fb6d2bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334171" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334171/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334171"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334208/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a6bb6be2-87df-5a90-b8da-3fd783476439" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334208/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b713fe42-1112-55c0-a3e9-179614c29a7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334208/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334208/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334208" :question/id #uuid "521e4421-8c12-5483-a9ad-eb3663c791eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334208" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/26e2e4a51d40e9ed8e6a8fab342935e944e750587534c7cedc1855cbfddfbfc5.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}+5x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}+5x-6$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-334208/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334208"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334189/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "5268fcad-4ae8-573e-80fe-07a9521ab94c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334189/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd90552-824a-5df9-b4cd-4c041b6d04fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334189/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334189/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334189" :question/id #uuid "9c04f322-6297-5a59-99f0-946aa65e8f91" :question/math-academy-id "q-334189" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{5}-4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot 0=0\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334189/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334189"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48221/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "268d2a23-f9fc-5723-8101-b984f9a1d733" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48221/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "699c09d3-0aa4-5110-9ac4-f1705eebb66d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-48221/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-48221/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48221" :question/id #uuid "5c4a4a0b-e1a5-5f41-82c8-6f7ce8f75158" :question/math-academy-id "q-48221" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\nx{e}^{-2x}=\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-48221/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5414e634-9b33-5dac-9686-4e570efbc7e6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48221"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78021/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "57ddf1a0-f2e2-5046-81cb-f38c18a6850a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78021/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "60911185-f2dd-57ed-8b27-cf6afcaf67fc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-78021/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-78021/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78021" :question/id #uuid "f70a3773-bf2a-5b73-abc3-5cb2357d373c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78021" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2{e}^{x/5}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $2{e}^{x/5}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2{e}^{x/5}}=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78021/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5414e634-9b33-5dac-9686-4e570efbc7e6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78021"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338679/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "ea78c6f0-f62c-57c9-8f0a-9bae1cffea30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338679/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9be028ea-240c-5f99-b73d-942fa2a9e307" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338679/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-338679/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338679" :question/id #uuid "ae096ba8-7305-5c9b-9b5b-b942d8e27c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338679" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=0-3=-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338679/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338679"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48208/answer/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "4384c527-a3e2-5dee-a56c-d7db6110ce47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48208/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "173daa7b-200e-5db0-a98f-a4d6280ca3f2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-48208/answer/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-48208/answer/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48208" :question/id #uuid "3187ea03-2a2b-58df-8eac-de1e0d9676e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-48208" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7)=0+7=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48208/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48208"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338671/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "578b2cf5-183e-5a1f-b8c5-5dba90fd4584" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338671/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8ec972-c67d-5fea-a1b3-1aac198c5c4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338671/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-338671/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338671" :question/id #uuid "d11fc22e-27dc-5e16-bb71-552dd8da0121" :question/math-academy-id "q-338671" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3(x+2)}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338671/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338671"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48869/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "1af60cbf-868c-5faa-828d-3f9f7ecac8ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48869/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "09dbbc95-19f3-5123-b473-bbf74d305dc5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-48869/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-48869/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48869" :question/id #uuid "a6e8da81-1916-527c-8075-091e80f07bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-48869" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n3\\le \\operatorname{cos}x+4\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2c4c732472ffcfa4a42e7a0dd7e1ff55a9b4d2f0d5b0b652b875efa284d9274d.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48869/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48869"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48866/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "859a9968-4603-567a-8951-158894e28316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48866/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "a44932b0-57d2-57a8-a5ab-685dc9746c7b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-48866/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-48866/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48866" :question/id #uuid "72cf5a4e-ff4b-5bf3-a1c6-4b089563c4f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-48866" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $3{x}^{2}+x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/80dcb33426c9981efdb4eddb4bbd33e75aeb5267cd776acfbda99758e010128a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48866/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48866"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100706/answer/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "a1d337be-4dbd-5c73-afde-d1f2e603c6c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100706/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "632752f1-0d8d-5dfe-b7aa-4366797c3958" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100706/answer/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-100706/answer/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100706" :question/id #uuid "b2471a67-c72a-575a-8f10-13bf4266225f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100706" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-100706/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100706"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-26531/answer/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "59290d28-256a-5f61-9e44-a1bb0c14ec02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26531/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe21dfa5-a8d0-5e9b-927a-e5312b9901ce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-26531/answer/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer-field/choices ["q-26531/answer/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-26531" :question/id #uuid "f84765eb-bbc5-55fb-8f02-81e4b4814923" :question/math-academy-id "q-26531" :question/problem "If ${log}_{3}(6-2x)=4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3})$, then what is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & =4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ {log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}({(\\sqrt{3})}^{4}) \\\\ {log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(9) \\\\ 6-2x & =9 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(6-2x) & ={log}_{3}(6-2(-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(6+3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(9) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3})^{4} \\\\  & =4{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{3}).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-26531/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-26531"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110798/answer/2569eadfee40e0d27b5537dd21d237233116da072dfeb349d6a28ee66ddb635a" :answer/id #uuid "c626e4c3-0071-5e2d-8f3c-bb8499222473" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110798/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba70de7f-710d-52b0-9dbf-4fe40595a65c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-110798/answer/2569eadfee40e0d27b5537dd21d237233116da072dfeb349d6a28ee66ddb635a" :answer-field/choices ["q-110798/answer/2569eadfee40e0d27b5537dd21d237233116da072dfeb349d6a28ee66ddb635a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110798" :question/id #uuid "cbf7b752-cbd4-57de-8347-862618b357a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-110798" :question/problem "Solve ${log}_{2}(x-2)={log}_{2}(2x-3)$ for $x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No real solutions\"." :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & ={log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ x-2 & =2x-3 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(1-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2\\cdot 1-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, ${log}_{2}(-1)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110798/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110798"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-26532/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "5bac530d-a6a5-5769-907f-8d1987b9d0e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26532/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "49227727-c2d1-5d49-a240-d8df77f0c7ac" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-26532/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-26532/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-26532" :question/id #uuid "154ed866-22c4-5f95-b448-f473ba0b174a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26532" :question/problem "Given that ${log}_{2}(3x+1)={log}_{2}(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3x+1) & ={log}_{2}(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(5-1) \\\\ {log}_{2}(4) & ={log}_{2}(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-26532/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-26532"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100771/answer/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "23c87e17-e068-58e6-b62b-35ecceb2fd6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100771/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "2552e629-ed36-5d2e-a592-f43ee66159f7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100771/answer/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer-field/choices ["q-100771/answer/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100771" :question/id #uuid "501f700f-e881-502f-a49c-ed376789d61f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100771" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36)={log}_{5}(-16v-28).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & ={log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {v}^{2}+36 & =-16v-28 \\\\ {v}^{2}+16v+64 & =0 \\\\ (v+8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}((-8)^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16(-8)-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}(100) & ={log}_{5}(100).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-8$ is a valid solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-100771/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100771"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-302152/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "e5f158ff-5efa-5a7b-be91-b288a1bd7ed3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-302152/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a167e5e-f8ff-5323-922c-31faa60e4d63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-302152/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-302152/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-302152" :question/id #uuid "03fac39f-7bd4-593c-8143-5d6d29276bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-302152" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{6}(4x-2)$ is well defined for $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}(4\\cdot 2-2)}{2+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}({6}^{1})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-302152/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-302152"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339827/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "2e4ec5d3-d8f6-557d-baf7-74248b61e78e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339827/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b52ad5f2-2eaf-5cc7-b3fc-39152ab8f994" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339827/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-339827/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339827" :question/id #uuid "e2bb04bd-6dcb-51fa-87c5-f2822ddf4961" :question/math-academy-id "q-339827" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)$ is well defined for $x>-7$ and $x<5,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x) & ={log}_{3}(2+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-2) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(9)\\cdot {log}_{3}(3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}({3}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339827/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339827"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81991/x/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "726da436-dce6-5f8c-b1ef-84c00c0076f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81991/x" :answer-field/id #uuid "dfe480b7-11d3-57ba-abf4-57847ae2ac5f" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-81991/x/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-81991/x/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81991" :question/id #uuid "a143be78-236c-5255-bc5e-3d7bfb88f2a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81991" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=10\\operatorname{ln}(5x).$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81991/x"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6f4cfa-db03-5ae6-9f43-79fd3167c473"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81991"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81999/answer/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "69f64368-5b0a-5b99-8eaf-d411acf40c1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81999/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "508e2a54-77a6-5f39-8819-5f2487fafdb2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-81999/answer/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-81999/answer/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81999" :question/id #uuid "af0bbf2d-74bd-50a6-bf1f-aad032b60e21" :question/math-academy-id "q-81999" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=2{log}_{2}(x)-6.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2{log}_{2}(x)-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}(x)-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{log}_{2}(x) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{2}(x) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}(x)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81999/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81999"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82002/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "0f457522-0e79-5d09-af53-68b18678b277" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82002/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a5771f8-f3ac-5bd9-b0b9-18e96060513c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-82002/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-82002/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82002" :question/id #uuid "55eb1db9-fbcd-5c61-8a62-80c4dc797847" :question/math-academy-id "q-82002" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x)=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(2-x)} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ 2-x & =1 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-82002/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82002"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82001/answer/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01" :answer/id #uuid "279dcb68-2aa0-5ed9-b074-fd704ed393a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82001/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "5db17994-04e0-5902-9afc-c0a8af4f42ed" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-82001/answer/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01" :answer-field/choices ["q-82001/answer/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82001" :question/id #uuid "116feb8e-6c77-562d-8f5b-52579754920b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82001" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{5}(x-1)+1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}(x-1)+1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}(x-1)+1 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{5}(x-1) & =-1 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}(x-1)} & ={5}^{-1} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-82001/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82001"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81996/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "b36d48c9-4e46-5f1b-b5f8-a6214e35c8f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81996/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "16ff1213-7f56-51ef-8a9e-3aa52c1880f2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-81996/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-81996/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81996" :question/id #uuid "77ef5dca-afc9-54e0-8284-755f1d588fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81996" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{2}(3x-1)-3.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}(3x-1)-3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3x-1)-3 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{2}(3x-1) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}(3x-1)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ 3x-1 & =8 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81996/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81996"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336955/field-1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "eb907c0d-3b8a-5e38-bd9a-bee10c75c1dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336955/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f90da28f-f2df-5f89-bc5c-259715754e58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336955/field-1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-336955/field-1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336955" :question/id #uuid "39ff6f11-126f-5d17-90bb-5fef510de3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-336955" :question/problem "If $f(x)=3{x}^{9}$ and $g(x)=-2{x}^{-11}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\cdot g(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot f(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (3{x}^{9})=3{x}^{11}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)$ and $g(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\cdot g(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{11}\\cdot (-2{x}^{-11})) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-6 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-336955/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336955"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-67580/answer/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "57d42ae6-dec0-587f-8e65-a76bd0d3d23f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-67580/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e97405-b554-57b0-ad9e-c3f78a6c8f79" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-67580/answer/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer-field/choices ["q-67580/answer/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-67580" :question/id #uuid "818d4509-0f7c-512b-8135-c5e0fbca7d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67580" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}$, what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}})=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{8}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (8) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-67580/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-67580"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-320665/field-1/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583" :answer/id #uuid "ab2ed601-4938-54fd-9c7a-c1bcd1067fb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320665/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1afda0f2-c499-5fb4-a591-efc3a186f139" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320665/field-1/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583" :answer-field/choices ["q-320665/field-1/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320665" :question/id #uuid "e1e3d3bc-82fc-5fa2-90ce-eb77683f685b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320665" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-320665/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-320665"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-321138/field-1/c3c6c459ed4a41e1787e73fcd54c86c54db8639b1d960afc80ba4dad429691c3" :answer/id #uuid "04ac59b0-6e4f-5dce-ae60-9b5f04dd2348" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321138/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3181f7fa-572e-51e1-934e-8f5364b3af9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321138/field-1/c3c6c459ed4a41e1787e73fcd54c86c54db8639b1d960afc80ba4dad429691c3" :answer-field/choices ["q-321138/field-1/c3c6c459ed4a41e1787e73fcd54c86c54db8639b1d960afc80ba4dad429691c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321138" :question/id #uuid "100a789f-1084-51be-ba86-3a2a27edfbe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-321138" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arctan}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4arctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=4\\cdot (-{30}^{∘})={-120}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-321138/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321138"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14181/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "0977bb5b-ffae-5c4b-bba5-09591162b930" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14181/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce413f5-be82-5903-af44-36b68c1aca65" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-14181/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-14181/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14181" :question/id #uuid "b428fa76-e87c-5e0d-99bc-e0b26ffcdc6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-14181" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[8]{{n}^{5}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/8}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{8}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $3$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-14181/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14181"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "ce1df8e6-3183-51e1-a552-9f34aff2a38e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "0645d5d5-baad-584c-aff9-0f80ef6607f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "d83b6992-3443-5d27-9c0f-2a55881bba6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "1591b743-d64e-5e9b-979e-bafe889add72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "0872d019-2ad9-51d8-a445-ad0ce08f1417" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "c7769ec2-e466-5eea-8c4a-7bf0a2abde28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "b153e169-8d41-569a-91f2-4f53841c969a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "2fcdf911-2fd8-5105-811b-a33cc3048e7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a2b5171-b74e-56c5-a6ac-c68736954407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96676/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-96676/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-96676/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-96676/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-96676/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-96676/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-96676/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-96676/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-96676/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96676" :question/id #uuid "17943a85-f5f4-5759-b534-e7aca0b60db9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96676" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $3{y}^{''}+2x{y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{5}{y}^{'}=4x$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{y}^{'}}+7{x}^{3}={e}^{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\n- Equation III is also a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-96676/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96676"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-347375/field-1/abf694d790bc670b12675c62d70ac1749acc3e7462895ecbd9999b3eb41ae31e" :answer/id #uuid "68cf9dd0-e510-5126-b4d0-c090d47eb468" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac9{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347375/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d850b06c-59bd-5254-9a07-b72bfab72cfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-347375/field-1/abf694d790bc670b12675c62d70ac1749acc3e7462895ecbd9999b3eb41ae31e" :answer-field/choices ["q-347375/field-1/abf694d790bc670b12675c62d70ac1749acc3e7462895ecbd9999b3eb41ae31e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-347375" :question/id #uuid "a6de2ae0-e267-50e2-aafb-3b65201ff387" :question/math-academy-id "q-347375" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{27}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{27}{125})}{(\\frac{9}{25})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{25})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{25})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-347375/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-347375"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "5434b004-2468-5add-bf08-b34db24725d9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "df5dd82f-cd2e-5351-bdeb-d1a91d62486a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "46879293-9588-5332-9d71-9bc89252aa2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "4ac94f30-91f1-58ff-9677-bb0ee6545524" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "650b0d5b-3ebf-5077-9ac9-272237398d93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "ff1148d6-c098-5ca6-80f2-f7f770795e87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "99d7b8dc-c539-5a30-b787-d5cb6fbad3a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "ef47b9b8-b919-5c03-a9a0-ad6ea6f59897" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "acf614fb-085c-5189-ba08-b842d326e7c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143201/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-143201/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-143201/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-143201/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-143201/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-143201/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-143201/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-143201/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-143201/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143201" :question/id #uuid "b089f948-9e77-5fe2-b184-bf4a5b05b38e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143201" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8}{n+4}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8}{n+4}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{n+5})}{(\\frac{8}{n+4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(n+4)}{8(n+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{n+4}{n+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\frac{n+4}{n+5}<1,\n$$\n\nsince the numerator is always smaller than the denominator. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0<L<1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-143201/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90b485f8-a07d-55f4-8cde-f699070eeae7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143201"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75638/answer/ae88b0e4e39efaf2d30ebd55cc98cb3ed5733c3f224b932ff48a4224b22ca5a1" :answer/id #uuid "371532bf-637a-5fc9-8f8a-f85ac7cc22f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "125" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75638/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "5559560e-cfaf-5633-befc-a9c77a85e410" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-75638/answer/ae88b0e4e39efaf2d30ebd55cc98cb3ed5733c3f224b932ff48a4224b22ca5a1" :answer-field/choices ["q-75638/answer/ae88b0e4e39efaf2d30ebd55cc98cb3ed5733c3f224b932ff48a4224b22ca5a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75638" :question/id #uuid "248ca286-7948-5cc6-99a7-a8138552af97" :question/math-academy-id "q-75638" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $75$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{24}{5}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=75$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{24}{5}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{24}{5})}{75} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(\\frac{24}{5})(\\frac{1}{75}) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{8}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =75(\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75638/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75638"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352279/field-1/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "c5d98fb1-73c0-52a8-aa21-b27565c0b3d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352279/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "146a7171-bd46-5961-a19f-d6ee20c17aea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352279/field-1/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer-field/choices ["q-352279/field-1/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352279" :question/id #uuid "1b90e1c4-d521-5585-b6db-776d9a11ab9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352279" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9{a}_{n}$ given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=4.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factor outside of the summation sign gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9{a}_{n} & =9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\  & =9(4) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-352279/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352279"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-349060/field-1/f57108c65956383aeb6d71e60fb708ffaf0faf7bb73fab16fe9735f48e2555b1" :answer/id #uuid "1f822b0c-bbbe-5841-a487-068d8891fd12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "168" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-349060/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f615646-a0cf-5571-a02a-aaabfb151da4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-349060/field-1/f57108c65956383aeb6d71e60fb708ffaf0faf7bb73fab16fe9735f48e2555b1" :answer-field/choices ["q-349060/field-1/f57108c65956383aeb6d71e60fb708ffaf0faf7bb73fab16fe9735f48e2555b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-349060" :question/id #uuid "752cdffe-91c8-55b4-9c94-2e01e1ed83a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-349060" :question/problem "Find the eighth partial sum ${s}_{8}$ of the series  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n+3).$\n\n${s}_{8}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=4n+3.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(1)+3=7 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4(2)+3=11 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4(3)+3=15,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=4$ and first term ${a}_{1}=7.$\n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{8}=4(8)+3=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n{s}_{8}=\\frac{8}{2}(7+35)=168.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-349060/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8a9c045-18f6-58c4-9746-9a119ed6975c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-349060"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76754/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer/id #uuid "b99b927d-a15d-5e87-87ff-e4c8ca2814a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76754/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer/id #uuid "d0adf388-9909-53e6-a5cb-176699b204ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76754/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "56c70d2c-1a17-5ef9-ae9c-3702bfeb3a2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76754/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer-field/choices ["q-76754/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" "q-76754/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76754" :question/id #uuid "75e5636b-4302-5507-ac71-dd5e0fb70999" :question/math-academy-id "q-76754" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}?$" :question/worked-solution "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a convergent series results in a convergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n*If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.*\n\nSo, since ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, we have that ${\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76754/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d74dd4ee-45f1-5ca7-ae6c-9cbb768ac636"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76754"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336160/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "4500fac8-af20-5c20-911d-1f32dbb57955" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336160/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "335c7175-1070-581d-8e1f-409546a66e39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336160/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-336160/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336160" :question/id #uuid "9e386cce-90e7-5313-a0f9-874041a6503c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336160" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t,\\,0<t<\\pi .\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n$v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-336160/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336160"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336189/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "400aa6d7-ad0b-55c5-9730-4139ca77b867" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336189/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d79b4b85-5bf6-5426-8987-1d99cefbf281" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336189/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-336189/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336189" :question/id #uuid "d1f7c427-fd09-51c4-aeca-22ae40dad476" :question/math-academy-id "q-336189" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}t-8\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}t-8\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t+8\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =6\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+8\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =6(0)+8(-1) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-8|=8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-336189/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b380ed-1974-5876-8c88-a45773741b48"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336189"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117331/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "05093784-be42-5759-bb5b-604a75a80dd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117331/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f40ab244-9e72-55f0-b52a-d771ac9a7696" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117331/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "82e7bbd8-4fef-5055-a846-e47b2a0ffed3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117331/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "04ac6c12-ac73-51cc-a51b-a8742a4800c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117331/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "cad6966a-4554-523d-89eb-dce58e73f94b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117331/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "64dd75a1-90f3-5ad7-8e60-4e14e84283db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure 