,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-70711/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70711"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117039/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "16f4a9c6-555a-5cc0-a62a-a0ef7a517e5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117039/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae