eld/choices ["q-157136/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-157136/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-157136/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-157136/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-157136/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-157136/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-157136/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-157136/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157136" :question/id #uuid "f21f9a92-e265-5a42-858b-c3523a61288f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157136" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that ${b}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1.$\n\nI. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is also divergent for $K\\ne 0.$\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any non-zero multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is true. The sum of a convergent series and a divergent series is divergent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Note that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is a **finite series.** Therefore, ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-157136/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18b8b49-3de3-54ba-8b4d-ca3c8a50c2c6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157136"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "9764d03e-d2a5-51c2-b135-b591e8355b42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "13f34607-fa97-57d2-b597-fa8c4bd337b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ddf52af7-4622-5274-b8d9-df77922937e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "4be3504a-0f66-5048-9275-871a324af799" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2950368e-f30f-54a6-9cbc-e39d2b227801" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "c88b193c-1e36-53f0-bba8-c3b41432dc35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "fb78b81a-08e0-5883-b8c1-d2b3762bd274" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "5c5027dd-4dbf-56e5-b293-30e24a303a20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c39958ab-9a75-5986-89db-259aee63a2ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49836/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-49836/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-49836/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-49836/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-49836/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-49836/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-49836/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-49836/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-49836/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49836" :question/id #uuid "9f068f64-42c9-5eab-a9dd-69dbbca65fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49836" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that ${a}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1.$\n\nI. - $\\frac{1}{10\\,000}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=10}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is also divergent for $K\\ne 0.$\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any non-zero multiple of a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}$ is a **finite series.** Therefore, ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Removing a finite number of terms from a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-49836/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18b8b49-3de3-54ba-8b4d-ca3c8a50c2c6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49836"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133324/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "93e8b85a-cbcf-55fd-b21d-724a4d35c312" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133324/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c911e3-9ea8-5569-8799-f7127763fe8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133324/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-133324/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133324" :question/id #uuid "7c776c27-a6a4-5d10-97a0-9fb241329ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133324" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1186087a359b3065400101c7f436c3e885bf937477bdd9f73fbdaab6771aa48a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3}).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-2,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133324/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2b72a2-c486-5a7a-a65e-57e83b78f6cc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133324"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133326/field-1/60a0a5e4a383ede207efed8649cd5493f6592c479822f55723fa63cb39446a29" :answer/id #uuid "039afb05-3dd1-55c1-8034-3ea673ee4a22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1/4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133326/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "209517ec-e06e-50a2-b3b4-440b13e55c97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133326/field-1/60a0a5e4a383ede207efed8649cd5493f6592c479822f55723fa63cb39446a29" :answer-field/choices ["q-133326/field-1/60a0a5e4a383ede207efed8649cd5493f6592c479822f55723fa63cb39446a29"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133326" :question/id #uuid "8c3c9278-79a7-5543-b4fd-fdc095dcc49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-133326" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{2-2}{(2)^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133326/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2072b915-15f9-5296-96dd-707e38df3d10"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133326"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133327/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "25f4ed6e-501b-5b5e-9497-a5e6e518ce62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133327/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e480ad3-4c1f-58fa-acab-2ec945f606c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133327/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-133327/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133327" :question/id #uuid "8555738f-0375-5e38-b16f-5d036c583f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-133327" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})$" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}[\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133327/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5feb914d-efad-5d89-85a9-db99befc9928"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133327"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-101719/answer/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "e6ece883-5457-512b-817a-6a540f5cafb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101719/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "daadac8d-e9fa-50ed-9624-0e832b2c29a3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-101719/answer/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-101719/answer/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-101719" :question/id #uuid "5712e498-177c-5ff5-9270-29807a04505c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101719" :question/problem "Given that $y=7-4{log}_{3}x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-4{log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =0-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} & ={(-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3})\\,|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-101719/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-340014/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer/id #uuid "bdd5f721-f706-5012-82b2-dd84492aa8b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340014/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9364ab94-e028-59cc-bb87-1b9ee5e2b212" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-340014/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer-field/choices ["q-340014/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340014" :question/id #uuid "84b41208-fbbb-5c93-b4f6-118635b12b3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340014" :question/problem "Given the function $y=5{x}^{2}+6\\operatorname{ln}x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2}+6\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2x+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =10x+\\frac{6}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(10x+\\frac{6}{x})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3+\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =32\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340014/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340014"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-9293/answer/d0d01bb2e3e816b9fd6aee47accd74e6df07f3f36fe536db4d7bfbcde2546d68" :answer/id #uuid "441dd12d-ad06-54c0-b50d-8bafad595eac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9293/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "078bc752-e359-55d9-ac87-b5b28ffbedb8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-9293/answer/d0d01bb2e3e816b9fd6aee47accd74e6df07f3f36fe536db4d7bfbcde2546d68" :answer-field/choices ["q-9293/answer/d0d01bb2e3e816b9fd6aee47accd74e6df07f3f36fe536db4d7bfbcde2546d68"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-9293" :question/id #uuid "770c1ff4-046b-5d4d-9ce0-c706d161310b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9293" :question/problem "If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=0.5,\\,$ ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=9$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}=-2,$ then calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factors outside of the summation signs gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}+8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n} \\\\  & =3(0.5)+2(9)+8(-2) \\\\  & =1.5+18-16 \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-9293/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-9293"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124064/answer/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "42142022-6da5-5db6-96e0-957c33de6428" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124064/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "83199529-9873-5d3d-9f07-29285488bb05" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-124064/answer/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-124064/answer/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124064" :question/id #uuid "8c0a953b-56c6-5668-952a-c90e6b1ea708" :question/math-academy-id "q-124064" :question/problem "If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=-\\frac{3}{4}$, \nthen calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{a}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factor outside of the summation sign gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{a}_{n} & =8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124064/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124064"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-157059/answer/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f081fa25-f9ba-5f97-b23b-48406f2126a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157059/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2d6360e-374c-5656-a211-5ddc29753536" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-157059/answer/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-157059/answer/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-157059" :question/id #uuid "6515e93f-5048-550d-a93c-1295e9ffd539" :question/math-academy-id "q-157059" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent,  determine all real values of $K$ for which  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{K}{2}-1)\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is **convergent.**\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nHowever, multiplying the terms of a divergent series by $0$ gives a series that converges to $0.$\n\nTherefore, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{K}{2}-1)\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis convergent when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K}{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{K}{2} & =1 \\\\ K & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-157059/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-157059"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-156162/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "d14ffde9-6472-5c5c-908f-3d98789b82d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156162/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "0aaf5648-6c39-5954-ba61-f78b65318168" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-156162/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-156162/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-156162" :question/id #uuid "6028f7b5-360d-5374-9eaf-4f3a559f7277" :question/math-academy-id "q-156162" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(K-3)\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is **convergent.**\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nHowever, multiplying the terms of a divergent series by $0$ gives a series that converges to $0.$\n\nTherefore, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(K-3)\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis convergent when \n\n$$\nK-3=0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-156162/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156162"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121912/answer/f2d93ea59bd63ae2d383a218117c572dfd32c3c2bbcb56fed58e84e82187a063" :answer/id #uuid "651f2754-926f-5093-8c02-3934d8e51f2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "560" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121912/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9536be4-f080-5106-b857-a93e80b63cc1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-121912/answer/f2d93ea59bd63ae2d383a218117c572dfd32c3c2bbcb56fed58e84e82187a063" :answer-field/choices ["q-121912/answer/f2d93ea59bd63ae2d383a218117c572dfd32c3c2bbcb56fed58e84e82187a063"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121912" :question/id #uuid "18ed24b1-d375-51e8-ad2b-0848def589e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-121912" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2})+1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2})+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{{60}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & =-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={100}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={220}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={340}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={100}^{∘},{220}^{∘},{340}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$ is $220+340=560.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-121912/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f40f50e-49a7-5eda-b642-dc7085569b23"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121912"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "6c158c77-0adb-5f06-8d4b-c07d37822cc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6313b72d-4ece-5327-8d7f-7d8b27de6a3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-328346/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-328346/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346/field-2/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412" :answer/id #uuid "2f7935d6-915d-546d-9ca6-05f8521d9bd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee14a56f-6429-5c5e-a654-22356ab98625" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-328346/field-2/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412" :answer-field/choices ["q-328346/field-2/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346/field-3/23191a7abb2085fa58cfcf0bd067eedb8c4feb2e427bc3ee2024594244bb9b18" :answer/id #uuid "797a29ae-b1ec-5db8-ac8e-934955f9ac1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "180" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "79538ab1-c604-5e0b-ab5a-c1801bb24957" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-328346/field-3/23191a7abb2085fa58cfcf0bd067eedb8c4feb2e427bc3ee2024594244bb9b18" :answer-field/choices ["q-328346/field-3/23191a7abb2085fa58cfcf0bd067eedb8c4feb2e427bc3ee2024594244bb9b18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346/field-4/1ad763bfa28dd8bf929699f6c31cff536336c19979c6baa533b7a886875a37b7" :answer/id #uuid "f754096a-e449-5d46-aad7-4001bd2da0e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "270" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "c520450c-f7cf-5826-9d4e-e16ba09003e0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-328346/field-4/1ad763bfa28dd8bf929699f6c31cff536336c19979c6baa533b7a886875a37b7" :answer-field/choices ["q-328346/field-4/1ad763bfa28dd8bf929699f6c31cff536336c19979c6baa533b7a886875a37b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328346" :question/id #uuid "2ef87672-04a1-57b6-b8ef-a21f1ff660eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-328346" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the **exact solutions** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}(2x+{45}^{∘})=1,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{45}^{∘} & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-{45}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =n\\cdot {90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={0}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={90}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={180}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={270}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={270}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-328346/field-1" "q-328346/field-2" "q-328346/field-3" "q-328346/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3bd5e5c-b8d1-53e5-9968-8bef8faceb4b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-328346"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-274706/field-1/0dcf700800b055b31767fdc86c8e47a75f2d7b6155778dc97b3e55be2443dcfa" :answer/id #uuid "05b71290-7e7c-5c68-9cc7-10f0d22fc82b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-150" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274706/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb31c8a-3267-5b07-9e99-2ac26ce7c640" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274706/field-1/0dcf700800b055b31767fdc86c8e47a75f2d7b6155778dc97b3e55be2443dcfa" :answer-field/choices ["q-274706/field-1/0dcf700800b055b31767fdc86c8e47a75f2d7b6155778dc97b3e55be2443dcfa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274706/field-2/ebfc0e0786b9e08f32fe2539ae50e791a60b7c4613bb2a0820cefbd3ea742454" :answer/id #uuid "64951150-f61b-5d0c-a61d-34e60a478028" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274706/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "a07176f6-1dc2-5cfa-8111-55c57611ed93" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274706/field-2/ebfc0e0786b9e08f32fe2539ae50e791a60b7c4613bb2a0820cefbd3ea742454" :answer-field/choices ["q-274706/field-2/ebfc0e0786b9e08f32fe2539ae50e791a60b7c4613bb2a0820cefbd3ea742454"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274706" :question/id #uuid "28b54476-13f4-5be8-9db7-b73daab42ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-274706" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the **exact solutions** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}(2x-{90}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<0,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{90}^{∘} & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={90}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<0.$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-{60}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=-2$ gives $x=-{150}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<0.$ \n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={-150}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={-60}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-274706/field-1" "q-274706/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9eb12a99-5169-52f7-8830-5de0d2d854f6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-274706"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350576/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b12c1066-af2f-5cfe-a611-8a4523274537" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350576/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f56f715-e231-5fc2-8f7e-fd84242b7707" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350576/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-350576/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350576" :question/id #uuid "d2e44366-3661-52c4-bfa0-64b9706d86c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350576" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=7$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.4$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=7.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{(1-(-0.4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{(1+0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1.4} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-350576/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350576"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350571/field-1/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer/id #uuid "96f638d7-8251-569a-a6ec-34300fefaa66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350571/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "30687167-a416-5b3d-a4e8-5eea820277d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350571/field-1/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer-field/choices ["q-350571/field-1/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350571" :question/id #uuid "978fbe70-b917-5f3f-8f89-44fd798a2554" :question/math-academy-id "q-350571" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-7$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=0.5$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-7.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-7)}{(1-0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-7)}{(0.5)} \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-350571/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350571"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124585/answer/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "8947bc3a-7ecb-51af-ac74-5b49f9403f42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124585/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf43fe7a-26b2-5fc0-8910-6663e9a0868c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-124585/answer/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-124585/answer/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124585" :question/id #uuid "9e0bc989-6c13-538f-9204-1071f5bfda3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124585" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}{15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124585/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124585"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124580/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "f52124e0-1658-58b3-b02d-b5db6a8e4113" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124580/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "89695a32-33de-5291-a31b-c425b3e0c9b3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-124580/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-124580/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124580" :question/id #uuid "fe72df8e-159f-5c71-9114-1755109effd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124580" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }45(0.1)^{n}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=45(0.1)^{1}=4.5.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{45(0.1)^{2}}{45(0.1)^{1}}=0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }45(0.1)^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{(1-0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{0.9} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{9} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124580/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124580"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124587/answer/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "fd9bc9cd-64be-5155-9556-0bcc2ac93274" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124587/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "02f66923-1333-5f92-8020-0f438e3502a0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-124587/answer/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-124587/answer/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124587" :question/id #uuid "51438042-d192-59cd-9eae-aac65ca4ba26" :question/math-academy-id "q-124587" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=12 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{24}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{24}{5})}{12}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124587/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124587"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350620/field-1/734d74928a78be6974b19009b44f8d068dacfbb2cada7180aee9e63a87b6007c" :answer/id #uuid "c93478d7-5ad1-5168-bdbd-35f035c0cd48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350620/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "865a5466-9fc0-5e19-ac48-97dd76755a81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350620/field-1/734d74928a78be6974b19009b44f8d068dacfbb2cada7180aee9e63a87b6007c" :answer-field/choices ["q-350620/field-1/734d74928a78be6974b19009b44f8d068dacfbb2cada7180aee9e63a87b6007c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350620" :question/id #uuid "a590ae1b-a6f1-59bc-ae08-476c39ab9fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-350620" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{64}{9}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{64}{9}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4}=\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{64}{9}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5}=\\frac{1}{144}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{144})}{(\\frac{1}{36})}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{64}{9}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{36})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{36})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-350620/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350620"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350648/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "f218525b-e998-507f-b69d-bc0b09424db6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350648/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd4bec2-b996-5e03-87f5-0771e4052cd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350648/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-350648/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350648" :question/id #uuid "7729376e-a449-523f-b26a-16e4bafe18ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-350648" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{\\,n-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{3-2}=6(-\\frac{1}{5})=-\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\n$$\n{a}_{4}=6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{4-2}=6(\\frac{1}{25})=\\frac{6}{25}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{25})}{(-\\frac{6}{5})}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{\\,n-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{6}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{6}{5})}{(\\frac{6}{5})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-350648/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350648"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14088/answer/110c6c3872c93f65f5b61a9c78b5df669eabb0454f6d2f1b3adec7ef182197c3" :answer/id #uuid "00a19c25-9a8a-5f7c-8819-b75bf130ed1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.05" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14088/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c94b1fa-56ab-5b4b-9e3b-4d7264d2e3ba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-14088/answer/110c6c3872c93f65f5b61a9c78b5df669eabb0454f6d2f1b3adec7ef182197c3" :answer-field/choices ["q-14088/answer/110c6c3872c93f65f5b61a9c78b5df669eabb0454f6d2f1b3adec7ef182197c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14088" :question/id #uuid "308c69f7-025f-5fb5-b821-d31e639575ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-14088" :question/problem "Determine the value of the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(-0.2)}^{3n}$  rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6(-0.2)^{3(1)}=6(-0.2)^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=6(-0.2)^{3(2)}=6(-0.2)^{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6(-0.2)^{6}}{6(-0.2)^{3}}=(-0.2)^{3}=-0.008.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(-0.2)}^{3n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-0.2)^{3}}{1-(-0.008)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-0.008)}{1.008} \\\\  & \\approx -0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-14088/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14088"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-123961/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer/id #uuid "75282ae4-fa28-5274-b61f-5ff217fb2b6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-123961/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer/id #uuid "7b01190d-f335-5a2f-9c2f-a1d6a06a1693" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-123961/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "97963b45-365e-5cfd-b622-26978b6bae07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-123961/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-123961/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" "q-123961/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-123961" :question/id #uuid "743b96cc-15a3-5525-bc3d-b9c616d0fdc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123961" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{3}).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})=-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})=-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})=-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=-\\sqrt{3}n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(-\\sqrt{3}n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{3})$ is also divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-123961/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ca055ce-2958-54d3-b362-468dfb5f63ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-123961"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75010/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer/id #uuid "6da93657-2a94-5e85-b951-99d639f9bda3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75010/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer/id #uuid "60848fc6-9815-5a84-9685-23d15ae5a995" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75010/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "66ac0be6-6684-550f-8d5f-5fd3e9e13f6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75010/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-75010/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" "q-75010/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75010" :question/id #uuid "9f2a5a3d-c14f-568e-bca3-40489a2b109e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75010" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{5}).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\sqrt{5}+(-\\sqrt{5})=-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =-\\sqrt{5}+(-\\sqrt{5})+(-\\sqrt{5})=-3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\sqrt{5}+(-\\sqrt{5})+(-\\sqrt{5})+(-\\sqrt{5})=-4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=-\\sqrt{5}\\,n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(-\\sqrt{5}\\,n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{5})$ is also divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75010/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ca055ce-2958-54d3-b362-468dfb5f63ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75010"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-123969/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer/id #uuid "522c1f8e-78bc-58fa-9df4-84520078285a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-123969/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer/id #uuid "a66ce1c5-7ce5-5c02-afed-46f3d17ce2e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-123969/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc98c227-8f2c-5b60-8bb3-540c6f306dcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-123969/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-123969/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" "q-123969/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-123969" :question/id #uuid "07ca5e2d-77a2-57ef-9113-ced1d4775a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-123969" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =0+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we can see that the sequence of partial sums oscillates between $0$ and $-1$ forever. \n\nSo the limit of the sequence of partial sums does not exist, and therefore the series is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-123969/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fbf48c3-47f2-594f-90f0-b0cd659b0239"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-123969"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49642/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer/id #uuid "f0422631-e627-5668-a3fd-c50f7d2e4c0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49642/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer/id #uuid "3ab84687-4674-50da-a455-1f6360b70d58" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49642/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8bafb7c5-b67d-5e0b-9773-76f093b2d804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49642/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-49642/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" "q-49642/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49642" :question/id #uuid "335820dd-ba2b-5e7f-92b7-d9a01208fa93" :question/math-academy-id "q-49642" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{n}.$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{1}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{2}=0 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{3}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we can see that the sequence of partial sums oscillates between  $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $0$ forever. \n\nSo the limit of the sequence of partial sums does not exist, and therefore the series is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49642/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fbf48c3-47f2-594f-90f0-b0cd659b0239"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49642"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75133/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer/id #uuid "f17b3011-3910-51b9-b03b-b12b6857b9f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75133/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer/id #uuid "62c93bf3-207c-59ce-a1b9-e78bffd77c61" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75133/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cbd5f5-4303-5bab-a71c-9e19c41003cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75133/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-75133/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" "q-75133/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75133" :question/id #uuid "c6c03b37-a458-5686-aa0d-f791bd5b116f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75133" :question/problem "Given that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is defined by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\,n=1 \\\\ 1,\\,n=2 \\\\ -2,\\,n\\ge 3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =0+1=1 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =1-2=-1 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-1-2=-3 \\\\ {s}_{5} & =-3-2=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $n\\ge 3$ is\n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=1-2(n-2)=5-2n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(5-2n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ diverges." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75133/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9afc8277-0fcd-5fdd-9ced-a8de9e5d4168"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75133"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124060/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer/id #uuid "a3688785-1084-5422-8f3a-12fc901bf9f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124060/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer/id #uuid "8d67d966-acfc-5538-936e-5bb90e6f6882" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124060/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b69e70c1-efa4-5882-aa08-8ecc147d051d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124060/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-124060/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" "q-124060/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124060" :question/id #uuid "c556c0fb-0999-5d66-8c7d-1dbc40d7dbff" :question/math-academy-id "q-124060" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{2}.$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "The given series is an arithmetic series. Its first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand its $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{n}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we get the following formula for the $n$th partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{n} & =\\frac{n}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{n}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n(1+n)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the limit of the partial sums, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n(1+n)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{n}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the limit of the sequence of partial sums is infinite, the series is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124060/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a580ed7b-87a7-5603-ad67-1882a44782b0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124060"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124062/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" :answer/id #uuid "16877732-d7c6-5ba5-a177-69fec44dad3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124062/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer/id #uuid "db8ae490-21a6-564a-8c12-32e6bd7fc5f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124062/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5291b67e-7185-5f08-96fc-2f36ec0b9d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124062/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-124062/selection/51fd10dbedb33ad93fd55b8f7c554f5a1c68a3c598ab8e5e39bb43bf80ec7a73" "q-124062/selection/2f3c1dd1bc7b5e6c12fd1a66e58ffc5b07ff34b9ca432abacf624236100c14e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124062" :question/id #uuid "afd7d27e-c371-5649-a501-b00a45a6a8b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124062" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(n+3).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "The given series is an arithmetic series. Its first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1+3=4,\n$$\n\nIts $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=n+3.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we get the following formula for the $n$th partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{n} & =\\frac{n}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{2}(4+n+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{n(7+n)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the limit of the partial sums, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n(7+n)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{n}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the limit of the sequence of partial sums is infinite, the series is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-124062/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a580ed7b-87a7-5603-ad67-1882a44782b0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124062"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "fa692625-fe97-50db-8394-916e1a62167d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1c429934-d74c-5e2b-871b-b0adb3d332c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b670e50d-9d06-52f7-885f-7b6145c0fa0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3275672b-7eac-59f2-ae9c-df07c077799a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "1997d23c-8dc6-513e-9f79-d1e8a5b147d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "fef1c9c1-1d1b-5361-8e97-6f610efc8c53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "7a14d661-c47c-528c-ad49-f3111573954f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "e6e1a970-75b2-517d-92c0-66364a778fe7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8dfdb71c-4f55-5217-b06e-b6a0b696eb0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143182/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-143182/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-143182/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-143182/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-143182/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-143182/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-143182/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-143182/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-143182/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143182" :question/id #uuid "866beddf-cf9f-5dad-a386-56b1a393c58f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143182" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n>2$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{(2n+2)!}{{5}^{n+1}})}{(\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{n}(2n+2)!}{{5}^{n+1}(2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{n}\\cdot (2n+2)\\cdot (2n+1)\\cdot (2n)!}{{5}^{n}\\cdot 5\\cdot (2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{5}^{n}}\\cdot (2n+2)\\cdot (2n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(2n)!}}{\\cancel{{5}^{n}}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{(2n)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2n+2)(2n+1)}{5} \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-143182/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "169f59f9-ee45-5375-a22d-4906b7a09ad0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143182"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143210/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "acfaca99-cf56-5b14-b665-be234d6a14a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143210/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8169b930-ea3a-5678-a86f-b3ce0eab37d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143210/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "d8cb2cae-6d51-59af-afcb-9fafc4cf378c" :answer/type :answer