d686e8c9e8e6" "q-143210/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-143210/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-143210/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143210" :question/id #uuid "59532c68-165f-5d14-a2f1-4b6da63b73ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-143210" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}={e}^{n+1}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}={e}^{n+1}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{n+2}}{{e}^{n+1}} \\\\  & ={e}^{n+2-(n+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{n+2-n-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{1} \\\\  & =e \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-143210/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d7e9b2-b321-5c29-992a-775750c570f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143210"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143215/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "b56ebf76-8647-51e6-