9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-143220/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-143220/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143220" :question/id #uuid "27707620-ae31-5355-beb9-34dcaa2b3212" :question/math-academy-id "q-143220" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n+1}}{{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n+1-n} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-143220/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d7e9b2-b321-5c29-992a-775750c570f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143220"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97980/b/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "7ec7bfd3-8f80-5b87-aa75-1ec8afd1b0ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97980/b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec1a2131-1d28-5ffa-8e30-33f1d353ae90" :answer-field/key "b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-97980/b/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer-field/choices ["q-97980/b/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97980" :question/id #uuid "70cc7789-9f93-5c83-af83-334149cbeb2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97980" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1\n$$\n\n \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=b\\pi +6n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $b?$\n\n$b =$: {{b}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97980/b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8166bb-3a9d-5808-af53-a18ca99920f8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97980"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-16355/a/c53cea686227da43c70e9578e3314f631566d0db2b5fd1a2d52faa1c540efabd" :answer/id #uuid "bcd9ab5e-9ca8-5494-88f9-a90a5a2fef22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16355/a" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb349ee-5aff-5464-b3b9-949ef5685eb8" :answer-field/key "a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-16355/a/c53cea686227da43c70e9578e3314f631566d0db2b5fd1a2d52faa1c540efabd" :answer-field/choices ["q-16355/a/c53cea686227da43c70e9578e3314f631566d0db2b5fd1a2d52faa1c540efabd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-16355" :question/id #uuid "83f30889-f878-583f-b150-a995dc476f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16355" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{24}+\\pi n & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-\\frac{5}{24}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-16355/a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a180dc90-c6b9-577e-9eef-ed60ecafe4e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-16355"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-328242/field-1/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer/id #uuid "8481b97c-79bd-5680-a936-855eee350617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-328242/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c059408-4e1e-56d3-9bee-5e0446a61f1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-328242/field-1/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer-field/choices ["q-328242/field-1/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328242" :question/id #uuid "47d2cbf1-1164-5cb5-ab6c-facf3e8732c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-328242" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\pi +2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n \\\\ 6x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{9}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-328242/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a180dc90-c6b9-577e-9eef-ed60ecafe4e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-328242"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121065/a/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" :answer/id #uuid "8bf506eb-2b5f-58df-abc0-fd517d134b8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121065/a" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f5b13d7-90f7-5557-ad9b-bb080e3046c5" :answer-field/key "a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-121065/a/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" :answer-field/choices ["q-121065/a/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121065" :question/id #uuid "7be751c5-5761-5b27-a477-d52010e0eb74" :question/math-academy-id "q-121065" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(2x)=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(2x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{30}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=15.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121065/a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97786302-e865-5581-b08e-8da7c6b7a01b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121065"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-16364/a/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer/id #uuid "53134b9c-2ec9-5e5a-adbc-78b1a7ed6b45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16364/a" :answer-field/id #uuid "da0790fc-3f61-57e0-ae49-9f30ce6de3f8" :answer-field/key "a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-16364/a/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer-field/choices ["q-16364/a/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-16364" :question/id #uuid "6404a99e-7757-52ba-89dd-f5be5b3def0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-16364" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{8}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-16364/a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97786302-e865-5581-b08e-8da7c6b7a01b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-16364"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98030/answer/d5ed3ac547233a6f335c11bcc929110d91d964f6abceff82a41a501ec761b8f3" :answer/id #uuid "c1013782-3d79-51a2-9739-3f2e166b9f97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "350" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98030/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5bb9771-544b-5e26-b5f1-fe053e541d83" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-98030/answer/d5ed3ac547233a6f335c11bcc929110d91d964f6abceff82a41a501ec761b8f3" :answer-field/choices ["q-98030/answer/d5ed3ac547233a6f335c11bcc929110d91d964f6abceff82a41a501ec761b8f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98030" :question/id #uuid "579b5dc7-11ff-5357-9fa6-16fce5adbb8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98030" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(x-{20}^{∘})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x={a}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-{20}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nx-{20}^{∘}={30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x-{20}^{∘}={330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x={20}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x={20}^{∘}+{330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x={50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x={350}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=350.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-98030/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98030"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-328449/field-1/eb2edadb0f7505521d9a6ae795b65e5ac328fc8a10f3bacc9b421a0af92deee7" :answer/id #uuid "c9797fa8-ae3c-59f1-81ef-9302007dc2a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-328449/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "73c1882f-1dd2-54b0-974a-249353e6af9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-328449/field-1/eb2edadb0f7505521d9a6ae795b65e5ac328fc8a10f3bacc9b421a0af92deee7" :answer-field/choices ["q-328449/field-1/eb2edadb0f7505521d9a6ae795b65e5ac328fc8a10f3bacc9b421a0af92deee7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328449" :question/id #uuid "3b14cf9c-c1ee-5a69-a0d8-213d7f9bcecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-328449" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(4x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\\,x=a\\pi +\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(4x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n4x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,4x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n, & \\,4x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}, & \\,x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{11}{24}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-328449/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-328449"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-9983/answer/4ca7e04cb32d17c1971e27f96c827d785e809ee86fbe2e04813f39f29bdc8cde" :answer/id #uuid "f02455f8-7263-5aee-b6ca-44c762028d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9983/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "1921c358-4dd0-59c7-bde4-fa7983f50160" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-9983/answer/4ca7e04cb32d17c1971e27f96c827d785e809ee86fbe2e04813f39f29bdc8cde" :answer-field/choices ["q-9983/answer/4ca7e04cb32d17c1971e27f96c827d785e809ee86fbe2e04813f39f29bdc8cde"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-9983" :question/id #uuid "e2fe4fd1-88fd-50df-b7b9-458bd013b3aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-9983" :question/problem "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $10-2+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-2}{10}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{5}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1+\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{6}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-9983/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55b978ec-9ec6-5349-a103-0c11d9a904c1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-9983"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14060/answer/a2ce0d7121680ad5ca64b109a32cd456c311ade4af21eb0a0dd652e8319117f1" :answer/id #uuid "5d59d8b0-4b56-5725-be31-52c36a0cb78c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14060/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "5eb399e4-3dec-5639-9a17-a37effe5f77c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-14060/answer/a2ce0d7121680ad5ca64b109a32cd456c311ade4af21eb0a0dd652e8319117f1" :answer-field/choices ["q-14060/answer/a2ce0d7121680ad5ca64b109a32cd456c311ade4af21eb0a0dd652e8319117f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14060" :question/id #uuid "97aba825-3afc-5081-87e1-01c8ec528803" :question/math-academy-id "q-14060" :question/problem "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $40+20+10+5+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=40$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{20}{40}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-14060/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55b978ec-9ec6-5349-a103-0c11d9a904c1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14060"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75633/answer/d580852fdbac0f5bd4cb4dd0f25981bd69bfe2e4f91d8512f3032596d1d3eccc" :answer/id #uuid "9458b0d4-8d31-555b-a01c-fe2986e57d4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75633/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "25dbef3a-b117-5f99-b906-3d759d8ef1a3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-75633/answer/d580852fdbac0f5bd4cb4dd0f25981bd69bfe2e4f91d8512f3032596d1d3eccc" :answer-field/choices ["q-75633/answer/d580852fdbac0f5bd4cb4dd0f25981bd69bfe2e4f91d8512f3032596d1d3eccc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75633" :question/id #uuid "3d0cbb3a-0823-5976-9f6e-21a39f72136a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75633" :question/problem "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $3.2+2.4+1.8+1.35+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=3.2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2.4}{3.2}=0.75.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.2}{1-0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.2}{0.25} \\\\  & =12.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75633/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2954b59f-d701-502f-963b-b62098a87c34"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75633"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75626/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "b94b13f9-7a1a-550f-a64c-b7417d391efe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75626/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac99111-4cfe-5110-ae08-6fd3410179bf" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-75626/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-75626/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75626" :question/id #uuid "c1cd2129-a959-502d-807d-9a8a76abff61" :question/math-academy-id "q-75626" :question/problem "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $2.1+0.63+0.189+0.056\\,7+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=2.1$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.63}{2.1}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.1}{1-0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.1}{0.7} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75626/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2954b59f-d701-502f-963b-b62098a87c34"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75626"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40286/answer/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" :answer/id #uuid "db8b76fd-8b6b-55c3-8edc-c3327759fc25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40286/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b0833d4-4144-569c-b006-dceef4d41ca4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-40286/answer/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" :answer-field/choices ["q-40286/answer/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40286" :question/id #uuid "44cf182f-9628-54ce-8c6a-39e321172edf" :question/math-academy-id "q-40286" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a fifth term equal to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and an eighth term equal to $-\\frac{1}{16}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{5}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${a}_{8}=-\\frac{1}{16}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{8-5} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{16})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(-\\frac{1}{16})(-2) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=5$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40286/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40286"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-67278/answer/0674b229b15216f6ea870ccaaf87984357f5d6af66421aad0753c15d921d419a" :answer/id #uuid "35f1fe2b-5643-5b0a-907c-3906ef26c821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-67278/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "2615b029-78a2-5f37-bd7f-39a215cd4284" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-67278/answer/0674b229b15216f6ea870ccaaf87984357f5d6af66421aad0753c15d921d419a" :answer-field/choices ["q-67278/answer/0674b229b15216f6ea870ccaaf87984357f5d6af66421aad0753c15d921d419a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-67278" :question/id #uuid "bd4b8c3e-dfee-5ef6-85a6-3f80440979fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-67278" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $48$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{3}{4}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=48$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{48} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-67278/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-67278"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-308798/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "d6faeda4-9cd8-5fc1-a2cd-ee250d4c774f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-308798/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5df9d6fb-6be9-590e-a368-cd71009532f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-308798/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-308798/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-308798" :question/id #uuid "4147d71c-0780-5d7a-b644-4ac51dc7dac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-308798" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+3x)=2x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(-1)=2(-1)+3=-2+3=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-308798/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-308798"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342142/field-1/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" :answer/id #uuid "0d6aa3ee-91e6-588f-9610-d2b871f52395" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342142/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1de8457e-012a-5e41-81aa-c2795332adbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342142/field-1/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342142/field-1/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342142" :question/id #uuid "3545fe3a-a0e6-548e-85e2-2ea53ccf0c60" :question/math-academy-id "q-342142" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-3{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4-3{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-3{x}^{2})=-6x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=-6(1)=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342142/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342142"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342147/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "1a79e81d-7957-5034-8f49-136ab4cac68c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342147/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f400028-7c20-514f-a009-3d6ef64a38fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342147/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer-field/choices ["q-342147/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342147" :question/id #uuid "7fdc1ded-84f3-5aa0-9f75-d6f219a0924f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342147" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}$ at the point $(2,-8).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2})=\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4}-8x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-8):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=\\frac{5}{4}({2}^{4})-8(2)=20-16=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342147/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342147"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71525/answer/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer/id #uuid "32db2457-07c5-5427-8a92-9bd848a6b907" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71525/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "42e70d83-930f-5340-bd0c-7bd247ddc733" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-71525/answer/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-71525/answer/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71525" :question/id #uuid "37f69159-8a56-5bc8-8f1b-0361b230c90b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71525" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-x+3$ at the point $(1,4).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-x+3.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-x+3)=6{x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(1)=6(1)-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{'}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71525/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71525"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342152/field-1/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "7bc826bc-caaa-5fab-8d91-1740d9955e1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342152/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "46e92c24-80e6-5d0f-be8f-9482056003c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342152/field-1/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer-field/choices ["q-342152/field-1/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342152" :question/id #uuid "c5af5c6e-7b27-5022-a2e9-5d5ddf2fa935" :question/math-academy-id "q-342152" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ at the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{-4}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{-5} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(-1)=6{(-1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{(-1)^{5}}=6-4=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342152/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342152"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-340942/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "30f677b4-0a78-5d93-84e2-c44c254ad921" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340942/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "77c31624-34ce-5c6a-a27d-a8f7ce71483e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-340942/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-340942/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340942/field-2/0fcb2bb891929980a55765997d5489abfc663cf792cecb9464e97759b6cc7a6f" :answer/id #uuid "e1c7d0d4-e1bb-56b4-aad3-eb3d736dbab3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "74" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340942/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6839d6-dcb1-5941-bca7-1c53a24e01f4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-340942/field-2/0fcb2bb891929980a55765997d5489abfc663cf792cecb9464e97759b6cc7a6f" :answer-field/choices ["q-340942/field-2/0fcb2bb891929980a55765997d5489abfc663cf792cecb9464e97759b6cc7a6f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340942" :question/id #uuid "f1ee8101-ef90-5936-9218-2b307b36327b" :question/math-academy-id "q-340942" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ at the point $(2,9).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+1.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+1)=4x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,9):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=4(2)=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ 8y-72 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+8\\cdot y & =74.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-340942/field-1" "q-340942/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340942"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47405/answer/4fdf863e2167c017fd78fcd2b05159f7a97bb8c63015c994fd6fc0138b182308" :answer/id #uuid "520a9952-36d1-5b28-9d1a-d3da42f14a77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "362" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47405/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d2e2314-4883-5c9a-a3d2-ba10b8b0172d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-47405/answer/4fdf863e2167c017fd78fcd2b05159f7a97bb8c63015c994fd6fc0138b182308" :answer-field/choices ["q-47405/answer/4fdf863e2167c017fd78fcd2b05159f7a97bb8c63015c994fd6fc0138b182308"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47405" :question/id #uuid "2dd83d18-2e82-5500-9a29-406ec35162d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47405" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,16)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})=6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)^{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{'}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(16) & =-\\frac{1}{24}(x-(2)) \\\\ 24y-384 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+24y & =386.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=386-24=362.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47405/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47405"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342163/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "8ba24c34-cdde-566c-b78b-46a9c4176575" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342163/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "08fbda2d-307a-5e11-987c-cf4a2e89319d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342163/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342163/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342163/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "34358e83-d26d-565c-a696-0c6976f8d3a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342163/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "42e0fcf9-5fb0-513d-9339-ad66240b2699" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342163/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-342163/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342163" :question/id #uuid "e1a6ea14-1871-540c-9817-e8bcb2170f98" :question/math-academy-id "q-342163" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=4x-{x}^{3}$ at the point $(1,3).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4x-{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-{x}^{3})=4-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=4-3({1}^{2})=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-1\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+1\\cdot y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342163/field-1" "q-342163/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342163"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71542/coordinate-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "2e1eb68a-e252-5e78-a0ae-e38739c43457" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71542/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8baad589-7eab-517d-90c5-8bf0ac079d1a" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-71542/coordinate-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-71542/coordinate-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71542/coordinate-2/787ba90009a2104196393a6fa203fa7646fc82176685c8ab9f72d2176e83792a" :answer/id #uuid "0d7aaa54-efcb-5abb-8a7e-be2121262538" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71542/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3e89ab2-4682-5c69-9be4-fd67450d1798" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-71542/coordinate-2/787ba90009a2104196393a6fa203fa7646fc82176685c8ab9f72d2176e83792a" :answer-field/choices ["q-71542/coordinate-2/787ba90009a2104196393a6fa203fa7646fc82176685c8ab9f72d2176e83792a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71542" :question/id #uuid "a51e04ce-0c90-5337-b907-f8c65ca8414a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71542" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-4x+1$ at the point $(2,5)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-4x+1)=6x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)-4=8,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{4}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 0+\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\frac{21}{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71542/coordinate-1" "q-71542/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-10878/coordinate-1/7495b22a8694caf4eab331e172464242c9c26d3eafc6582b001b0ab03e9f01a4" :answer/id #uuid "852cbd16-a914-5ad4-9056-4d9ec5c12889" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "57" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-10878/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "39467e20-e20d-5502-9bf8-881bbc694917" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-10878/coordinate-1/7495b22a8694caf4eab331e172464242c9c26d3eafc6582b001b0ab03e9f01a4" :answer-field/choices ["q-10878/coordinate-1/7495b22a8694caf4eab331e172464242c9c26d3eafc6582b001b0ab03e9f01a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-10878/coordinate-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "46c65590-ea51-547b-a16f-ef7e7566fc67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-10878/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3b98be8-fbca-50cf-a182-625dd393f4fc" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-10878/coordinate-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-10878/coordinate-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-10878" :question/id #uuid "99463695-96b3-52fc-80ac-01a64fec0d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-10878" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}$ at the point $(3,9)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(3)=2(3)=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{6}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}+9 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{19}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}x \\\\ x & =57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(57,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-10878/coordinate-1" "q-10878/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-10878"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-19049/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "9f7b78d4-1cc0-5070-b66f-31145be45620" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19049/r1c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e926ece9-9c6a-5930-b891-d76a9c4b2b6a" :answer-field/key "r1c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-19049/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-19049/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19049/r2c1/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "c838a260-aad2-5399-833d-b5dbda46900c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19049/r2c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a50714a1-8282-57e5-8d21-2bb9829fc692" :answer-field/key "r2c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-19049/r2c1/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer-field/choices ["q-19049/r2c1/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19049/r3c1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "e83ad01d-cddb-50a4-9e42-14abe9456bdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19049/r3c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "019580bf-188b-5cfc-bd21-39b4613134ce" :answer-field/key "r3c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-19049/r3c1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-19049/r3c1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19049" :question/id #uuid "92ba06b2-5956-5b83-8cb1-36fb1d3ffbe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-19049" :question/problem "If $r=⟨0,-2,4⟩$ and $s=i-6j$, then $r+s=$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 3, column 1: {{r3c1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\ns=i-6j=⟨1,-6,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum, we just add each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =⟨0,-2,4⟩+⟨1,-6,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,-2-6,4+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-8,4⟩ \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-19049/r1c1" "q-19049/r2c1" "q-19049/r3c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f819127-1ab3-5a85-a381-9e30e32884aa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-19049"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-318377/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "a1403987-3894-5423-9bcc-4b82657f9801" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-318377/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "33603e3b-595e-5033-8240-5f2bd5947655" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.typ