ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70908" :question/id #uuid "ca52945b-4f7a-5b78-870a-fc1840d01e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-70908" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{2x-3}=1$\nII. - $\\sqrt{4x+1}=-6$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{2x-1}=12$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only II states that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equation II has no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-70908/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70908"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-214154/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "9f0de88a-188e-520b-92ae-cabf356afd49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-214154/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "00420ee4-74b7-5f21-9e8b-16d3139d38b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-214154/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-214154/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-214154" :question/id #uuid "3e1a7869-1d36-5605-ac92-b7a41430847f" :question/math-academy-id "q-214154" :question/problem "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)\\,,\\,f(1)=3\\,,\\,f(2)=-1,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe fourth term of the sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The fourth term is represented by $f(4).$\n\nTo compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =(-1)+3 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =2+(-1) \\\\ f(4) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is $f(4)=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-214154/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-214154"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-24395/root-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d9410aef-859a-50f6-97ef-91aa9b99d3e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-24395/root-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3483412-84da-5ca5-8b46-ea4c410dfb39" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-24395/root-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-24395/root-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-24395/root-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "dc60f7c2-bdc3-55b2-8f01-98d1b2da6836" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-24395/root-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d24f560-5de1-53ca-a224-e5768fffe719" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-24395/root-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-24395/root-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-24395" :question/id #uuid "fe159bf6-a623-5b2b-9a60-8be08ef9e05c" :question/math-academy-id "q-24395" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/6d2d3e924745d0703358b87c1a201113bcce2d02618ad1dce27d51d58eb6fbf7.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above.\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(2,0)$ and $(3,0).$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-24395/root-1" "q-24395/root-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa081d4-f85a-5239-b034-b78894429611"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-24395"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15940/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "327dfb30-a023-5c23-bba9-91f243856467" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15940/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e28de3b-4f3d-59d5-b4c1-13d8631a52fb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-15940/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-15940/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15940" :question/id #uuid "be5e99bc-6a52-5455-9180-0bb659dfe8a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15940" :question/problem "Find the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[10,13]$ given the following table of values for $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ | $\\,15\\,$ | $\\,17\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(13)-f(10)}{13-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-1}{13-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15940/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f7224e3-9c13-51e7-a3f7-0e379f63a861"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15940"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-11670/complement-event/9b27ae95d850fd11f39a611933d2702c402276d70d1fefe284aa072607b25a4f" :answer/id #uuid "1694fcf5-640e-59e5-9571-f2a08d9ddcad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The patient is not a smoker" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11670/complement-event/85575a5604e0b45a50f645fd6587e65006c9034073e1c9adff13cc3312c07801" :answer/id #uuid "71109537-5d34-5063-b2cc-92d751d6c0ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The patient is a smoker" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11670/complement-event" :answer-field/id #uuid "402bb104-7405-5d66-aabd-ca4094fb9864" :answer-field/key "complement-event" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-11670/complement-event/9b27ae95d850fd11f39a611933d2702c402276d70d1fefe284aa072607b25a4f" :answer-field/choices ["q-11670/complement-event/9b27ae95d850fd11f39a611933d2702c402276d70d1fefe284aa072607b25a4f" "q-11670/complement-event/85575a5604e0b45a50f645fd6587e65006c9034073e1c9adff13cc3312c07801"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-11670/complement-probability/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f" :answer/id #uuid "5a35fb8f-d89c-565c-9fb6-7a7ddaee8c0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11670/complement-probability" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f30e9b-cffc-5a2e-98ab-4d61c92e0d17" :answer-field/key "complement-probability" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-11670/complement-probability/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f" :answer-field/choices ["q-11670/complement-probability/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-11670" :question/id #uuid "d2dcf9c8-21c2-5756-902c-6ae34835148c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11670" :question/problem "A nutritionist has $25$ patients. Of his patients, $10$ of them are smokers, and $15$ of them have diabetes. We randomly select a patient among the $25$ patients. \n\nDefine $A$ to be the event \"the patient is a smoker.\" What is ${A}^{'}$ described in natural language, and what is $P({A}^{'})?$\n\nComplement event: {{complement-event}}\n\nComplement probability: {{complement-probability}}" :question/worked-solution "The **complement** of an event $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'},$ is the *opposite* of $A.$ That is to say, ${A}^{'}$  is the set of outcomes in which $A$ *does not* happen.\n\nHere, ${A}^{'}$ is the opposite of the event \"the patient is a smoker.\" So ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the patient doesn't smoke.\" \n\nNow, let's calculate the probability $P({A}^{'}).$  There are $25$ patients, and $10$ of them are smokers, so there are $25-10=15$ non-smokers. Therefore, \n\n$$\nP({A}^{'})=\\frac{15}{25}=0.6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-11670/complement-event" "q-11670/complement-probability"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "355573c6-a39e-5cd2-804f-bb07e2639ef7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-11670"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-119699/answer/250d79f96fe0538363a39ff6475c3291963c6a55bc727ce128daeb62e061a964" :answer/id #uuid "c0c7da23-01a0-51be-9e7e-72e594b04dd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119699/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d625369-3894-5861-8ca4-9400a3db1aba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-119699/answer/250d79f96fe0538363a39ff6475c3291963c6a55bc727ce128daeb62e061a964" :answer-field/choices ["q-119699/answer/250d79f96fe0538363a39ff6475c3291963c6a55bc727ce128daeb62e061a964"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-119699" :question/id #uuid "76002dd4-3fa5-5b97-999e-21ec06af100c" :question/math-academy-id "q-119699" :question/problem "Three balls are drawn from a bag that contains $5$ white balls and $4$ black balls. Define $A$ to be the event \"at least one white ball is drawn.\" It is known that $P(A)=0.95$ to $2$ decimal places. To two decimal places, what is the probability of drawing three black balls?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The event \"three black balls are drawn\" is the opposite of the event \"at least one white ball is drawn.\"\n\nSo, the event \"three black balls are drawn\" is the complement of $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'}.$\n\nSince we know that $P(A)=0.95$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.95 \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-119699/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "732b0025-3172-5685-aa98-1f4afcf387ab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-119699"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-41911/answer/bc99aa5f3ee62c40cb834b274ce88683dbffd8fee8404db2b536df0189aa9cdc" :answer/id #uuid "720e1293-6223-51a2-8ae6-b8a8efbd2fe0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-41911/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "99a1e029-57c9-5535-a5a5-9b4e8d91d4df" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-41911/answer/bc99aa5f3ee62c40cb834b274ce88683dbffd8fee8404db2b536df0189aa9cdc" :answer-field/choices ["q-41911/answer/bc99aa5f3ee62c40cb834b274ce88683dbffd8fee8404db2b536df0189aa9cdc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-41911" :question/id #uuid "4ceee2f4-61b4-5f77-aefc-d5befcee1142" :question/math-academy-id "q-41911" :question/problem "Two balls are drawn from a bag that contains two red balls and two black balls. Define $A$ to be the event \"no black balls are selected.\" It is known that $P(A)=0.25.$ What is the probability of selecting at least one black ball?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The event \"at least one black ball is selected\" is the opposite of the event \"no black balls are selected.\"\n\nSo, the event \"at least one black ball is selected\" is the complement of $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'}.$\n\nSince we know that $P(A)=0.25$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-41911/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "732b0025-3172-5685-aa98-1f4afcf387ab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-41911"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "9429ffb0-d529-544d-8017-1e51bef97808" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "616f6e0e-482f-510b-b573-5c356d87d0f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "5ba7c15d-d3cb-59aa-9e04-f73793693b28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e82f6fdf-cd6a-518c-bf93-5bcfbd58572d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "dbb099e6-0299-5d14-85a1-e2f505b0f3c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6de1e2ae-c9eb-5c1a-af4b-7ad5418ce020" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "1812c78b-a55b-57c3-832c-8eaffa65f449" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "f53f81a0-20c4-5d62-822d-d15e0208bb72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5749a1ac-dbc4-5b78-8fcc-ffe805a26b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84489/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-84489/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-84489/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-84489/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-84489/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-84489/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-84489/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-84489/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-84489/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84489" :question/id #uuid "bbd53da4-9111-5ade-a6dc-05cf5e290645" :question/math-academy-id "q-84489" :question/problem "Two fair $6$-sided dice are rolled. Define $A$ to be the event \"the sum of outcomes is equal to $5.$\" Which of the following statements are true regarding this event?\n\nI. - $P(A)=0.15$ to $2$ decimal places\nII. - ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the sum of the outcomes is less than $5$\"\nIII. - $P({A}^{'})=0.89$ to $2$ decimal places" :question/worked-solution "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are $36$ possible outcomes and $4$ of these have the sum of the outcomes equal to $5,$ namely $(1,4),(4,1),(2,3),(3,2).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{4}{36}=0.11\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false. The complement of $A$ is the event ${A}^{'},$ \"the sum of the outcomes is not equal to $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A)=0.11$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.11 \\\\  & =0.89.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-84489/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "081e58b9-a8ba-5ad7-8e1c-8f8510a33587"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84489"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f70090e3-98ef-5f3f-b28c-00eb47513251" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e1c0e67e-d808-53cd-9858-cc01631827c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "bce7f2da-3789-5c2b-8e94-19ed4626c645" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "226a80ab-c24c-5168-9995-96b4cdccd0e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "3d67ee63-54ab-5bf4-8076-feb68e3aa3e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "940c2463-620e-5c9c-b7fe-91995fe351c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "59ad92fe-06ee-57cb-aec2-77491ac1bb6e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "653dfee8-c0bc-5bea-86ab-851d707ca336" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b17352a-c753-5c59-820d-fe9d7e06eb41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-119661/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-119661/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-119661/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-119661/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-119661/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-119661/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-119661/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-119661/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-119661/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-119661" :question/id #uuid "ccb354f9-ccd4-5d1d-9d93-05b8eecb5e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-119661" :question/problem "Two fair $6$-sided dice are rolled. Define $A$ to be the event \"the sum of outcomes is a prime number\" Which of the following statements are true regarding this event?\n\nI. - $P(A)=0.42$ to $2$ decimal places\nII. - ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the sum of the outcomes is an even number\"\nIII. - $P({A}^{'})=0.58$ to $2$ decimal places" :question/worked-solution "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. There are $36$ possible outcomes and $15$ of these have the sum of the outcomes equal to a prime, namely \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,4),(4,1),(2,3),(3,2),(1,6), \\\\  & (6,1),(3,4),(4,3),(2,5),(5,2),(5,6),(6,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{15}{36}=0.42\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false. The complement of $A$ is the event ${A}^{'},$ \"the sum of the outcomes is a composite number\".\n\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A)=0.42$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.42 \\\\  & =0.58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-119661/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "081e58b9-a8ba-5ad7-8e1c-8f8510a33587"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-119661"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-2601/p/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "e0ca1201-59d6-5d99-8425-54337f9aba1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2601/p" :answer-field/id #uuid "85f980d9-837e-5661-9515-10e93fcf55b9" :answer-field/key "p" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-2601/p/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-2601/p/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2601/q/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "17151e89-3a7d-5b5c-884f-a5edd5e6675e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2601/q" :answer-field/id #uuid "270ac144-5953-5cbb-ba46-e5db7c41213c" :answer-field/key "q" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-2601/q/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-2601/q/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2601" :question/id #uuid "a30fbd5f-352a-5d56-829a-7c77316d6cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-2601" :question/problem "If $\\frac{16a-8}{4}$ is written in the form $pa+q,$ what are the values of $p$ and $q?$\n\n$p$: {{p}}\n\n$q$: {{q}}" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16a-8}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{16a}{4}-\\frac{8}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{16}{4}\\cdot a-2 & = \\\\ 4a-2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $pa+qb,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(4)}_{⏟}}_{p}a+{{(-2)}_{⏟}}_{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4$ and $q=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2601/p" "q-2601/q"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "724bdf2f-c9f0-564e-ae33-c64d44c582ef"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2601"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133024/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "513a423d-a88f-5e18-9d15-f24ca8887917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133024/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "948fdae7-05ca-5624-a16e-022fb33b1775" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133024/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-133024/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133024" :question/id #uuid "e6095b63-ddc6-568b-9380-729c9ade9f90" :question/math-academy-id "q-133024" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4\\sqrt{x}-2 \\\\ f(4) & =4\\sqrt{4}-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133024/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133024"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133025/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "238dbe8d-4db0-537e-aa65-e66cdd944e03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133025/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "72b4136f-4387-5525-beff-16f19e2b0713" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133025/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-133025/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133025" :question/id #uuid "f1754b32-47e1-5590-8a91-bbc36a0323fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-133025" :question/problem "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>2,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=5-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133025/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8daba5bf-5162-5779-9512-f22d0c8c0320"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133025"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133026/field-1/a79c9adb944199c23ccbcfd1fa9aa06d928121fb5fa50c8d03d4785b6aceff6d" :answer/id #uuid "80f8910e-5b03-5984-9910-47019bc5d244" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1/3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133026/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe208381-c8c6-5813-a86d-b35b92287858" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133026/field-1/a79c9adb944199c23ccbcfd1fa9aa06d928121fb5fa50c8d03d4785b6aceff6d" :answer-field/choices ["q-133026/field-1/a79c9adb944199c23ccbcfd1fa9aa06d928121fb5fa50c8d03d4785b6aceff6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133026" :question/id #uuid "6c8691c2-0337-5d26-bf63-fbc25bd0f7e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-133026" :question/problem "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133026/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d522a8-ae3c-5723-909e-540ddac1d9da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133026"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133027/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "93e5b92f-ac10-506e-a57a-e16a167dbe6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133027/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "57a6ec67-c027-538e-9233-46de151c33f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133027/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-133027/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133027" :question/id #uuid "84eeaa13-d68d-5cfc-8888-807b5ced7f65" :question/math-academy-id "q-133027" :question/problem "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,6],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bc25d9452e02cfd80ae586368897ef3d917ab9bc5c8e7ea1b74c154f1d4ebb19.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-2.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133027/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133027"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133029/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "d332894f-e277-5c10-8905-e605f713fc58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133029/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6df0ed28-1b60-5812-9a56-ad326420afaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133029/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-133029/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133029" :question/id #uuid "f6b2a0d8-4028-5a47-a42c-61c14cfc6c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-133029" :question/problem "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =6 \\\\ h(4) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{6}{1}=6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133029/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133029"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133030/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "945b8d50-f440-5780-8ea7-c9e76f811169" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133030/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "731f05d4-c4f7-5886-9532-afcb439d53c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133030/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-133030/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133030" :question/id #uuid "d2f3209b-4bde-5973-b485-cd4659645aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-133030" :question/problem "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$ \n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=1.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=1$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{1}-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133030/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f954ff1-3f02-5fb1-aca2-5911d5636167"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133030"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133031/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "96ef80c8-2db1-5351-9296-57af895684bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133031/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "06a77d84-2e97-5cb1-b50c-242f92a052c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133031/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-133031/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133031/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "8c191908-eb0e-5337-9407-0cca774ce695" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133031/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca89e50d-c4a3-56d4-a2ff-cc57d8270589" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133031/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-133031/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133031" :question/id #uuid "2339506b-6632-5223-9e01-443389461a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-133031" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/819903d614018e7945117ddd14b26cf669777c1d4f3742e095b1cebc212bf879.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-4.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,-4).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(1,2).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-4$ and $2,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-4\\le y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-4\\le g(x)\\le 2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-133031/field-1" "q-133031/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133031"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133032/field-1/a79c9adb944199c23ccbcfd1fa9aa06d928121fb5fa50c8d03d4785b6aceff6d" :answer/id #uuid "29abe2ec-1d6d-5b36-9243-f4f5463a1ce6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1/3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133032/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b320bbe-c8b3-5d1b-b588-89fe83588f77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133032/field-1/a79c9adb944199c23ccbcfd1fa9aa06d928121fb5fa50c8d03d4785b6aceff6d" :answer-field/choices ["q-133032/field-1/a79c9adb944199c23ccbcfd1fa9aa06d928121fb5fa50c8d03d4785b6aceff6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133032" :question/id #uuid "6672f6f6-d069-5e08-8c51-22e371f34dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-133032" :question/problem "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-1$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-1)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{1}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-133032/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133032"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-262388/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "4bf1192f-e906-594d-8ba6-63ec104447d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-262388/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "423b274d-aff4-55df-8241-04b8772148bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-262388/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-262388/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-262388" :question/id #uuid "73fddfea-f037-5816-ab9d-7f787859e070" :question/math-academy-id "q-262388" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sin}({180}^{∘})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles ${180}^{∘}$ and ${270}^{∘}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle ${270}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${270}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle ${180}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({180}^{∘})=0.$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sin}({180}^{∘})=0+0=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-262388/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b406f464-6326-5dbf-b35c-8cde390a65de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-262388"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64863/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "e6c93262-23b3-5291-822c-b2fc2431d836" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64863/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "1854554f-c17d-57f3-91f8-95d189868d2d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-64863/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-64863/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64863" :question/id #uuid "5d76d623-1c6f-5d51-9983-82a36796ab6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64863" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The angle $0$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}0=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-64863/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b406f464-6326-5dbf-b35c-8cde390a65de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64863"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-1700/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df" :answer/id #uuid "0ad12b9e-4974-5164-a400-ca66e15d7e60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-1700/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "effc45c7-2b7f-53bd-9606-791d0a6f57ca" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-1700/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df" :answer-field/choices ["q-1700/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-1700" :question/id #uuid "c7e6e397-9e1b-53ed-9539-68c7fd844687" :question/math-academy-id "q-1700" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." :question/worked-solution "The angle ${270}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${270}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({270}^{∘})=-1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({270}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})}=\\frac{-1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})$ is undefined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-1700/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b547a211-99ec-5325-8d76-da3f7da13174"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-1700"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-85486/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df" :answer/id #uuid "f1176aca-177b-56ab-8497-2f78a7b72522" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85486/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc95bd45-30c4-5602-8cc2-a03cbae23f68" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-85486/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df" :answer-field/choices ["q-85486/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-85486" :question/id #uuid "aa9617ac-766d-5c56-b9e5-48a4a947fb5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-85486" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." :question/worked-solution "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘})$ is undefined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-85486/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b547a211-99ec-5325-8d76-da3f7da13174"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-85486"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-123583/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a90d3cb0-00ad-5297-8b6e-e1655f932b2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-123583/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "45720d87-6b35-533d-ae59-ce362ab3b985" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-123583/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-123583/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-123583" :question/id #uuid "606b2a8d-a2fe-5af3-a7b5-53405c89d3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-123583" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles $\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $\\pi .$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\pi =-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi =0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}\\pi }=\\frac{0}{-1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0+0=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-123583/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b547a211-99ec-5325-8d76-da3f7da13174"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-123583"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-123544/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df" :answer/id #uuid "fc8cdc6f-a69d-584f-b0fc-dabae251979e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-123544/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "68fb5449-9e08-575e-bf36-b120ccb0ea69" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-123544/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df" :answer-field/choices ["q-123544/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-123544" :question/id #uuid "147149ab-0381-536f-a9c7-1b31461b25be" :question/math-academy-id "q-123544" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." :question/worked-solution "The angle ${0}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${0}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({0}^{∘})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({0}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})$ is undefined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-123544/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2e28247-1113-5d9f-9c76-49af8b498fc5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-123544"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-262541/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "afb72c02-64d8-52ba-87e1-0d7943bea5d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-262541/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fef343c-8013-5791-9491-769519311f08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-262541/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-262541/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-262541" :question/id #uuid "aff059bf-4c64-5257-9909-c76ee946a250" :question/math-academy-id "q-262541" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}\\pi +\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}\\pi +\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles $\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $\\pi .$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\pi =-1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\pi =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\pi }=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\pi +\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1+1=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-262541/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2e28247-1113-5d9f-9c76-49af8b498fc5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-262541"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70412/r1c1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "8c4ada3f-ec52-5933-a92d-326b5d35a8ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70412/r1c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "64f3e97d-de55-5fb7-ad5d-d89294d4847f" :answer-field/key "r1c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70412/r1c1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-70412/r1c1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70412/r2c1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "e89259cc-eacd-5135-93d3-15fe21297e1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70412/r2c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6f32894-5980-59f9-9518-27185a1289eb" :answer-field/key "r2c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70412/r2c1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-70412/r2c1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70412" :question/id #uuid "ed8c6342-312a-5e87-b415-a663154a8416" :question/math-academy-id "q-70412" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$, $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ Find $3a-(b+4c).$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-(b+4c) & =3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]-([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}]-([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -24\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-70412/r1c1" "q-70412/r2c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a21360f-af5f-5730-85b2-b617de30eaf6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70412"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70414/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "1ddbb658-236b-51ec-8c51-94f2fd1dbbc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70414/r1c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1de335df-b994-51b7-92ca-da5c84a8cd11" :answer-field/key "r1c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70414/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-70414/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70414/r2c1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "1a06eab5-830d-579d-a65c-e49bd9fed32f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70414/r2c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c60283f6-0620-52ea-b3a9-32dd6ce73210" :answer-field/key "r2c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70414/r2c1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-70414/r2c1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70414" :question/id #uuid "12cf330f-781a-5288-af56-a50437c935bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-70414" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find $a+(2b-2c).$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+(2b-2c) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-70414/r1c1" "q-70414/r2c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a21360f-af5f-5730-85b2-b617de30eaf6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70414"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-26597/coordinate-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "daec97e4-b893-5a00-8712-be94dd2588ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26597/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "02800155-9bcb-5259-ba8e-424d9ec268b5" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-26597/coordinate-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-26597/coordinate-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-26597/coordinate-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "80e6f508-e53b-518c-98fa-cbc0b562a713" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-26597/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4474c4f-228a-53d0-ae2e-3764680f1481" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-26597/coordinate-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-26597/coordinate-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-26597" :question/id #uuid "49cddfeb-1204-5342-9323-0f53acd49339" :question/math-academy-id "q-26597" :question/problem "The point  $(0,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+2.$ What points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-3?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0,4-2)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(2))=(0,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $3$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-3.$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-2-3)=(0,-5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0)+2=4\\,⟹\\,f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-3.$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(0)-3 \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-5)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-26597/coordinate-1" "q-26597/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-26597"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-158542/coordinate-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "f25075c9-151d-51ec-ad1b-51c290720160" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-158542/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "543cbfb7-1173-5a65-bc37-f5518618feb6" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-158542/coordinate-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-158542/coordinate-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-158542/coordinate-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "74696480-27e8-50b8-8e68-1f5bc2c8821e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-158542/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbbd9a8-a3e8-5417-a704-9319b9fef99a" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-158542/coordinate-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-158542/coordinate-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-158542" :question/id #uuid "56d9614f-42ba-58e8-a0d6-4b222638dad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158542" :question/problem "The point  $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-1).$ What points **must** lie on $y=f(x)+2?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0-1,3)=(-1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,3+2)=(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0-1)=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-158542/coordinate-1" "q-158542/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-158542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-211305/coordinate-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "c37f8b76-b2a0-54d0-81ef-dc0d585b9280" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211305/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7886f806-4b4e-5ec7-9ff0-f5abc6c38247" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-211305/coordinate-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-211305/coordinate-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211305/coordinate-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "25a0f757-7428-5a0c-9bb3-857b616e17c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211305/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "18331c92-2d7a-5990-b3ed-933af437497e" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-211305/coordinate-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-211305/coordinate-2/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211305" :question/id #uuid "3ec07d68-7e85-5648-b443-8e43087e0d24" :question/math-academy-id "q-211305" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-1),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-1).$\n\n- Then, we  stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot (-2),-1)=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result  along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $3$ and get the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-4,3\\cdot (-1))=(-4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ is $(-4,-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-211305/coordinate-1" "q-211305/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-211305"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-211461/coordinate-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "93da801d-0b72-5360-a947-82b1e7f55971" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211461/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a87437f4-6972-5d12-b08e-6f25c6683f33" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-211461/coordinate-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-211461/coordinate-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211461/coordinate-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "a3290094-9762-5a7e-9778-bbfbd1085202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211461/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ccb4329-6487-5563-a24d-77de4314a055" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-211461/coordinate-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-211461/coordinate-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211461" :question/id #uuid "99887be0-5847-549d-86a8-9ddaeb49436c" :question/math-academy-id "q-211461" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/57f2d73dd902d3e0bafec168cfe6ecd46dfc7d7a9e05f3cf33d710ca3f6b1e76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=2f(x)+5$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $5$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=2f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=2f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,2\\cdot (-5))=(4,-10).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result parallel to the $y$-axis by $5$ units up and get the graph of $y=2f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-10)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-10+5)=(4,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5$ is $(4,-5).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-211461/coordinate-1" "q-211461/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-211461"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-211304/coordinate-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "8cb2da4c-01a9-5aec-8ed0-4837588db794" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211304/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8f3da4-f6ae-5e81-af3b-b85a93e4272f" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-211304/coordinate-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-211304/coordinate-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211304/coordinate-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "41938051-cf64-5229-9f63-7c6cf400baea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-211304/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "079eb80a-3cfb-5b85-a9f2-6944f89285d6" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-211304/coordinate-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-211304/coordinate-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-211304" :question/id #uuid "792abd45-247a-55f0-b21d-fe9a605db7a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-211304" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/73da11bf77a8bc10c00f5636176321ed1b44bbf614c8d4658e574e8ae5f85831.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(3x)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(15,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(3x)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(15,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(15,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 15,-5)=(5,-5).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(3x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(5,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(5,-5+4)=(5,-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4$ is $(5,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-211304/coordinate-1" "q-211304/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-211304"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167194/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "825436cb-9673-51cf-85dc-e619d63d520d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167194/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b772ad6-06db-5726-b6ce-d03788093751" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167194/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer-field/choices ["q-167194/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167194/field-2/c5b7aea79f73ebc562cbcff24725697bdc076b45efa2d86abecbb1b4fa682579" :answer/id #uuid "99557b4b-cf24-5413-9953-5485c6e07968" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167194/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d309dc-1a7d-576d-82a8-c54ce41c71fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167194/field-2/c5b7aea79f73ebc562cbcff24725697bdc076b45efa2d86abecbb1b4fa682579" :answer-field/choices ["q-167194/field-2/c5b7aea79f73ebc562cbcff24725697bdc076b45efa2d86abecbb1b4fa682579"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167194" :question/id #uuid "0afbd217-b5b5-5642-933f-a0e397418911" :question/math-academy-id "q-167194" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $20b+400a=7\\,200.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=480-10a \\\\ 20b+400a=7\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=480-10a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 20(480-10a)+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600-200a+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600+200a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 200a & =-2\\,400 \\\\ a & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-12$ into $b=480-10a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =480-10(-12) \\\\ b & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-12,\\,b=600.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167194/field-1" "q-167194/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167194"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167195/field-1/4977fa96074bb9b51b40fb6084881ab3f6f490e9ea7ed8e068a1a1cd69dce96b" :answer/id #uuid "354f5d76-8f03-5e6e-97fa-a25e850f3cf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167195/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "43acb66d-8004-5c0e-a631-d3d64fd7793b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167195/field-1/4977fa96074bb9b51b40fb6084881ab3f6f490e9ea7ed8e068a1a1cd69dce96b" :answer-field/choices ["q-167195/field-1/4977fa96074bb9b51b40fb6084881ab3f6f490e9ea7ed8e068a1a1cd69dce96b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167195" :question/id #uuid "2a5fdcf4-99c8-52a0-adf5-3a386b9372b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167195" :question/problem "Determine the weekly profit if the company produces $40$ couches in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-12{x}^{2}+600x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=40$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(40) & =-12(40)^{2}+600(40) \\\\  & =-19\\,200+24\\,000 \\\\  & =4\\,800\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$4\\,800.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167195/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167195"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167197/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "d8069a5e-c350-59af-92e9-2784d9ed4e96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167197/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e13021a-bbe5-5777-8b5e-ea6b09aed017" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167197/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-167197/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167197" :question/id #uuid "9a0fc448-799b-505a-a910-4ee7e12527c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167197" :question/problem "Find the minimum number of couches the company needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$7\\,200.$\n\nThe company needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ couches per week." :question/worked-solution "From part 5, the required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12\\cdot ({x}^{2}-50x+600)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $12,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-50x+600=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(600)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-50).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-30).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-50x=-20x-30x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-50x+600 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-30x+600 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-30+600 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-30x+600) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-30(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-20) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=30.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ couches per week to make a profit of $$7\\,200.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167197/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c9ba00d-9171-5732-8539-2e2943cce58e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167197"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-4825/answer/906fb68e52f34c93aaa89471be2f2ff4994dc9adb32c934377257fa64a54e307" :answer/id #uuid "f84ad872-89b4-594c-b109-88bee658a266" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4825/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "178b3ae7-011a-542f-917e-72857a90aa25" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-4825/answer/906fb68e52f34c93aaa89471be2f2ff4994dc9adb32c934377257fa64a54e307" :answer-field/choices ["q-4825/answer/906fb68e52f34c93aaa89471be2f2ff4994dc9adb32c934377257fa64a54e307"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-4825" :question/id #uuid "bd6a8a55-36de-5b05-883a-a794caef72c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-4825" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x$ and $g(x)=9-2x,$ find $(fg)(-1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}+5(-1)=-3 \\\\ g(-1) & =9-2(-1)=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=-3\\cdot 11=-33\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(2{x}^{2}+5x)(9-2x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+45x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =-4(-1)^{3}+8(-1)^{2}+45(-1) \\\\  & =4+8-45 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-4825/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-4825"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70044/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "0bf8de10-d41e-55cc-bf82-e50fe6e6a886" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70044/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8818533-22cd-5f3d-895a-867d995e5be4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70044/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-70044/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70044" :question/id #uuid "1a8dcfa2-7a22-559e-8b4a-993daded6d09" :question/math-academy-id "q-70044" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=3-2x$ and $g(x)=x+6,$ find  $(f+g)(5).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(5),$ we find $f(5)$ and $g(5),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(5)$ and $g(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =3-2(5)=-7 \\\\ g(5) & =5+6=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(5)=f(5)+g(5)=(-7)+11=4\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(3-2x)+(x+6) \\\\  & =9-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(5) & =9-5=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-70044/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70044"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-51843/answer/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "27dd64a2-7bd5-5f97-9dad-e0c5611a4652" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51843/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "740de7d4-004d-5340-938d-4b8a451d2832" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-51843/answer/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-51843/answer/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-51843" :question/id #uuid "aaf574a4-1edb-5e4e-ad18-e36c426d7d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-51843" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=-3$ and ${a}_{2}=5,$ then ${a}_{4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${a}_{4},$ we first need to compute ${a}_{3}.$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+2} & ={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n} \\\\ {a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =5+(-3) \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute ${a}_{4},$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2+2} & ={a}_{2+1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2+5 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{4}=7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-51843/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-51843"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-51884/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "258fcf2a-82f0-5ea8-bd85-4a4bad015507" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51884/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "eee2c678-c53b-5c59-9f8e-f6f6412565fe" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-51884/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-51884/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-51884" :question/id #uuid "1511ae51-ebcc-5ba6-b833-34f0d648c90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-51884" :question/problem "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=2$ and $f(2)=7,$ then $f(3)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =7+2 \\\\ f(3) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(3)=9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-51884/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-51884"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-103226/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "c9442754-d4d9-588b-9302-dd7a2e482a5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103226/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c0e081-89dc-5965-9267-824eb2083173" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-103226/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-103226/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103226" :question/id #uuid "81f8219b-91a8-52ec-8e9f-7635a6f67073" :question/math-academy-id "q-103226" :question/problem "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=0$ and $f(2)=2,$ then $f(4)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =2+0 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =2+2 \\\\ f(4) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(4)=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-103226/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103226"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14388/answer/ebe3072a1dd0240f82f7c04e06603ef971401ed1aea6a421c38664ef5adb09f3" :answer/id #uuid "b2799b7b-24b4-5d16-80c2-ac7ae521d926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14388/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "41bc5adc-5b0b-54f7-8e1e-d70f29a06994" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-14388/answer/ebe3072a1dd0240f82f7c04e06603ef971401ed1aea6a421c38664ef5adb09f3" :answer-field/choices ["q-14388/answer/ebe3072a1dd0240f82f7c04e06603ef971401ed1aea6a421c38664ef5adb09f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14388" :question/id #uuid "b8589e67-69ad-5bf0-a4d3-99bbd55f2e6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-14388" :question/problem "Given the recursive sequence $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=1$ and $f(2)=3$, what is the value of $f(7)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The recursion relation is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =3+1 \\\\ f(3) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =4+3 \\\\ f(4) & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, substituting $n=3,4,5$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5)= & f(3+2) & =f(3+1)+f(3)=f(4)+f(3)=7+4=11, \\\\ f(6)= & f(4+2) & =f(4+1)+f(4)=f(5)+f(4)=11+7=18, \\\\ f(7)= & f(5+2) & =f(5+1)+f(5)=f(6)+f(5)=18+11=29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(7)=29.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-14388/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14388"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100673/answer/88e0ab03b16547a40249d9f21db4f21cdbca533ca3deabf5ae3476c4d1de1c25" :answer/id #uuid "c3dbb7fe-750e-5953-9a22-6daaaa63fb4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.285" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100673/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "f19c826c-626c-5bc4-83fd-030e4431b402" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100673/answer/88e0ab03b16547a40249d9f21db4f21cdbca533ca3deabf5ae3476c4d1de1c25" :answer-field/choices ["q-100673/answer/88e0ab03b16547a40249d9f21db4f21cdbca533ca3deabf5ae3476c4d1de1c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100673" :question/id #uuid "51214ed3-9217-5f35-8c62-58f93e8509fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-100673" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{18}(41)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{18}(41)$ by changing the base $18$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=18,$ $X=41,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{18}(41) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(41)}{{log}_{10}(18)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(41)}{\\operatorname{log}(18)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.612\\,784}{1.255\\,273} \\\\  & \\approx 1.285\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100673/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100673"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-67700/answer/5cd5dc93f37c743976373ab57c37b5388ba149bd286c5b2139ed2c89c249424d" :answer/id #uuid "99c8788e-b678-5c57-9fef-cb3884fabb5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-67700/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "9079c108-3383-5bba-a4f3-e607d0cdd310" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-67700/answer/5cd5dc93f37c743976373ab57c37b5388ba149bd286c5b2139ed2c89c249424d" :answer-field/choices ["q-67700/answer/5cd5dc93f37c743976373ab57c37b5388ba149bd286c5b2139ed2c89c249424d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-67700" :question/id #uuid "ddb68ee1-fc65-5b56-84b0-11e3e63cec4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67700" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d81155f8c9b683e85a7f00ef41ac9ddcf36e303b40ca51c6de3e5d74833d5825.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=9.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3(3)-10)^{3} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3} \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9-3)^{2} \\\\  & ={6}^{2} \\\\  & =36\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)(36) \\\\  & =-36\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-67700/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-67700"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78093/answer/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer/id #uuid "91eb28b1-f951-5cc3-8a02-cf99fd93e522" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78093/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "641f80ba-c431-511c-8e84-51d38766fe3d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-78093/answer/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer-field/choices ["q-78093/answer/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78093" :question/id #uuid "d3fe2115-92b8-5f51-86a5-b888e4bd1095" :question/math-academy-id "q-78093" :question/problem "Suppose that $h(t)=90-5{t}^{2}$ represents the height of a ball thrown from the roof of a building, measured in meters from the ground level at $t$ seconds. What is the average rate of loss of height of $h(t)$ from $t=1$ to $t=4?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $h(t)=90-5{t}^{2}$ be the height of the ball, in meters, after $t$ seconds. To find the average rate of change of the ball's height from $t=1$ to $t=4$, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[1,4]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δh}{Δt} & =\\frac{h(b)-h(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{h(4)-h(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{[90-5(4)^{2}]-[90-5(1)^{2}]}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-85}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{75}{3} \\\\  & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of loss of height of $h(t)$ is $25$ meters per second." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78093/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d13310c-2b80-5945-ada3-31d886f1ec48"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78093"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "3cd1718f-44b0-551a-bbe4-cbdf43e9b0a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "4651d533-ecbc-522b-9a54-12bd4e20021e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "3dad651d-436f-570b-bf14-69598cf6d8ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3b03393d-0fc3-5bfe-bedd-0ae250e58214" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c143786c-c752-5d2e-9903-0c52b74f138f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "7eb5cd38-555a-5de4-ad48-126e9197b058" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "99746b9a-db42-5c76-8f6b-77a4de7baadd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "386e3c4b-41c6-5019-8f5b-b033d14e311d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "db82101f-7c0e-501d-90c5-0ffe2bb275d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-70907/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-70907/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-70907/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-70907/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-70907/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-70907/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-70907/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-70907/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-70907/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70907" :question/id #uuid "fd9d77ea-492f-56f5-8abf-5300e4f7832a" :question/math-academy-id "q-70907" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{3x+1}=-1$\nII. - $-\\sqrt{4-x}=6$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{2x+1}=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and II state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and II have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-70907/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70907"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-77743/answer/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer/id #uuid "13e8864d-bc03-575e-be49-c7cf32935637" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77743/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdd126b-7cfa-5038-8832-9a861d6fa162" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-77743/answer/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer-field/choices ["q-77743/answer/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77743" :question/id #uuid "336e3700-4bb5-5eae-b287-47106b8a9236" :question/math-academy-id "q-77743" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/44196cce68dab39addc447b332273371d5becb94bc0068e5200eeda596239ffa.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3))}^{8}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=-2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3)=2(-2)+3=-1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4)=-2+4=2\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+3))}^{8}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+4))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{8}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-77743/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77743"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100235/answer/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" :answer/id #uuid "3d496f36-d9ee-5b26-b44c-c1968857daf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100235/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "baff0e2d-9b61-5780-9004-7951a0b3c029" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100235/answer/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" :answer-field/choices ["q-100235/answer/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100235" :question/id #uuid "d37a6373-85f7-5021-93bf-bde115d91e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100235" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(x-2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(-1)^{4}+4)^{2}}{(-1-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{-27} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100235/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100235"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47299/answer/da9cd3f0d7d0d22103058884a443878ca6043275a54a9baf75c1708aeb53fcfb" :answer/id #uuid "554bd0ac-14d9-5d7e-b6fa-9a9fbd6f80d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.333" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47299/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "df64faa5-9c44-5a00-8262-d22fb90135ed" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-47299/answer/da9cd3f0d7d0d22103058884a443878ca6043275a54a9baf75c1708aeb53fcfb" :answer-field/choices ["q-47299/answer/da9cd3f0d7d0d22103058884a443878ca6043275a54a9baf75c1708aeb53fcfb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47299" :question/id #uuid "75eedbd1-62f9-545d-b2b8-ed88a19bd586" :question/math-academy-id "q-47299" :question/problem "Rose surveyed $168$ children in Arizona High School. She asked them about their favorite topics in math, between geometry and algebra. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below. \n\n|  | Geometry | Algebra | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $28$ | $63$ | $91$ |\n| Boys | $56$ | $21$ | $77$ |\n| Totals | $84$ | $84$ | $168$ |\n\nLet $B$ be the event that a randomly selected child is a **\"Boy\"** and $G$ the event that a randomly selected child likes **\"Geometry\"**. Estimate $P(B\\cap G).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $B\\cap G$ is the event that a randomly selected child is a boy and likes geometry. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $B\\cap G,$ we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Geometry\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Geometry\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Geometry | Algebra | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $28$ | $63$ | $91$ |\n| Boys | $56$ | $21$ | $77$ |\n| Totals | $84$ | $84$ | $168$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(168),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(B\\cap G)=\\frac{56}{168}=0.333.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-47299/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47299"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120211/answer/c8530b2891d9503fb6de9f0eb973be3fd49bf431b04889083e8f7ddb6861ad0f" :answer/id #uuid "2404a8fb-8cf7-5292-8507-88750527799b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120211/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea8f1de7-1eee-58e2-b14a-6b1290a784d1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-120211/answer/c8530b2891d9503fb6de9f0eb973be3fd49bf431b04889083e8f7ddb6861ad0f" :answer-field/choices ["q-120211/answer/c8530b2891d9503fb6de9f0eb973be3fd49bf431b04889083e8f7ddb6861ad0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120211" :question/id #uuid "02046115-abbf-5f23-b026-4fef673ef48b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120211" :question/problem "Some drinks of different sizes and flavors were sold during a school party. The two-way frequency table below summarizes the sold drinks.\n\n|  | Soda | Water | Coca-Cola | Fanta | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $8$ | $30$ |\n| Large | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| Totals | $11$ | $8$ | $16$ | $15$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected sold drink is a small Fanta.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected drink is a small Fanta, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Small\"** and the column **\"Fanta\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Small\"** and the column **\"Fanta\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Soda | Water | Coca-Cola | Fanta | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $8$ | $30$ |\n| Large | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| Totals | $11$ | $8$ | $16$ | $15$ | $50$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8}{50}=0.16.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120211/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120211"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-46814/answer/16088db09862c01af7d8100d6d5b2f026b7c92a46b7c55d4b9b17d0585af9f83" :answer/id #uuid "ae0f137c-f1e6-5c07-842d-8713cd771e0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-46814/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "d24908d8-a2f5-5436-aecb-56c6458ab874" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-46814/answer/16088db09862c01af7d8100d6d5b2f026b7c92a46b7c55d4b9b17d0585af9f83" :answer-field/choices ["q-46814/answer/16088db09862c01af7d8100d6d5b2f026b7c92a46b7c55d4b9b17d0585af9f83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-46814" :question/id #uuid "f0371bfd-8501-506c-b9fa-24609b84ea8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-46814" :question/problem "Martin surveyed some students in a school, asking them if they have cats or dogs at home. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\" Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(32),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9+10}{32}=\\frac{19}{32}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-46814/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-46814"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47139/answer/5b36c4b70758cba9f6fa0476faab4ecca020a862e5baaac6831d5712f6550044" :answer/id #uuid "c16e723f-f101-552c-b2b0-a44b2c6fdb5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.70" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47139/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "743e6ede-3996-50f5-bcb2-0cea97ae3446" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-47139/answer/5b36c4b70758cba9f6fa0476faab4ecca020a862e5baaac6831d5712f6550044" :answer-field/choices ["q-47139/answer/5b36c4b70758cba9f6fa0476faab4ecca020a862e5baaac6831d5712f6550044"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47139" :question/id #uuid "6ed5c80c-e475-58de-a971-282836200277" :question/math-academy-id "q-47139" :question/problem "The employees and the interns of a company were asked whether they commute to work by car or by bus. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Employee | Intern | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Car | $24$ | $16$ | $40$ |\n| Bus | $26$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $50$ | $30$ | $80$ |\n\nLet $I$ be the event that a randomly selected person is a **\"Intern\"**, and $B$ be the event that a randomly selected person commutes to work by **\"Bus\"**. Estimate $P(I\\cup B).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $I\\cup B$ is the event that a randomly selected person is an intern or person who commutes to work by bus. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $I\\cup B,$ we\n\n- add up the numbers in the column **\"Intern\"** or the row **\"Bus\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the column **\"Intern\"** or the row **\"Bus\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Employee | Intern | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Car | $24$ | $16$ | $40$ |\n| Bus | $26$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $50$ | $30$ | $80$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(80),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(I\\cup B)=\\frac{16+14+26}{80}=\\frac{56}{80}=0.70.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47139/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47139"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120875/answer/842947b00d8ffc5745884eed061ea57d57affe318d15d13be5c3612b41e13326" :answer/id #uuid "4500f0e5-fb34-5b1e-9aa7-f00bfd7572a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "84" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120875/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f6fb75-fc6d-5913-93b2-5261fe6c7f00" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-120875/answer/842947b00d8ffc5745884eed061ea57d57affe318d15d13be5c3612b41e13326" :answer-field/choices ["q-120875/answer/842947b00d8ffc5745884eed061ea57d57affe318d15d13be5c3612b41e13326"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120875" :question/id #uuid "2a26858a-19aa-5552-b214-8bb2060beda5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120875" :question/problem "Researchers surveyed students in School A on which superpower they would most like to have. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nSchool B has $300$ students. Assuming the distribution of results is the same for both schools, estimate the number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the number of students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Male\"** and the column **\"Flying\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points at the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Male\"** and the column **\"Flying\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing this number by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{28}{100}=0.28.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected School A student is a male and chooses to fly as his superpower.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower, we multiply the total number of School B students $(300)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =300\\cdot p \\\\  & =300\\cdot 0.28 \\\\  & =84\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120875/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120875"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47306/answer/1c773a28deb1532605bef1c821ed1793dc78d18dcfba98ebe53b00d875bbcaad" :answer/id #uuid "ddcdc7a1-98ce-5f26-a400-e2c7ecdb290f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "203" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47306/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "93ca5f3b-ab39-53ed-8854-3d49571cdace" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-47306/answer/1c773a28deb1532605bef1c821ed1793dc78d18dcfba98ebe53b00d875bbcaad" :answer-field/choices ["q-47306/answer/1c773a28deb1532605bef1c821ed1793dc78d18dcfba98ebe53b00d875bbcaad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47306" :question/id #uuid "146b2ef3-9c02-5d19-b5a0-7ac41d8998b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47306" :question/problem "A group of $5$th grade and $6$th grade students at Benjamin Franklin Elementary School are randomly selected for a survey. They are asked about their favorite places to go on summer vacations between the beach and the countryside. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Beach | Countryside | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$th grade | $55$ | $30$ | $85$ |\n| $6$th grade | $42$ | $33$ | $75$ |\n| Totals | $97$ | $63$ | $160$ |\n\nJohn Adams Elementary School has $250$ students in $5$th grade and $6$th grade. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both elementary schools, estimate the number of students at John Adams Elementary School who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations (or both). Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the number of students who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"$6$th grade\"** or the column **\"Beach\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | Beach | Countryside | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$th grade | $55$ | $30$ | $85$ |\n| $6$th grade | $42$ | $33$ | $75$ |\n| Totals | $97$ | $63$ | $160$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(160),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{55+42+33}{160}=\\frac{130}{160}=0.81.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is in $6$th grade or likes to go to the beach on summer vacations.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of John Adams Elementary School who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(250)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =250\\cdot p \\\\  & =250\\cdot 0.81 \\\\  & =202.5 \\\\  & =203,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-47306/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47306"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-103222/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "bbb57b6c-f1e6-5978-b142-a098b7bb0037" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103222/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "309ffe6e-1a0d-5db1-8e6e-7d5c01c645e2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-103222/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-103222/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103222" :question/id #uuid "0798bf8d-4438-5b94-a6a0-6a186fe4b314" :question/math-academy-id "q-103222" :question/problem "What is the fourth term of the following sequence?\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)\\,,\\,f(1)=-2\\,,\\,f(2)=0,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The fourth term is represented by $f(4).$\n\nTo compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =0+(-2) \\\\ f(3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =-2+0 \\\\ f(4) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is $f(4)=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-103222/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103222"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100674/answer/5940ee6529564b9bf383b1ba7e866b2b2605e77754125c31d02fd6e09d5e3c68" :answer/id #uuid "53710983-408a-5a6c-90ea-b34ff38359ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.850" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100674/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "2da1e781-a4dc-50ed-92d1-c932089c7436" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100674/answer/5940ee6529564b9bf383b1ba7e866b2b2605e77754125c31d02fd6e09d5e3c68" :answer-field/choices ["q-100674/answer/5940ee6529564b9bf383b1ba7e866b2b2605e77754125c31d02fd6e09d5e3c68"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100674" :question/id #uuid "70c8405e-c25e-581b-907c-1bd10f59fc85" :question/math-academy-id "q-100674" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{15}(10)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{15}(10)$ by changing the base $15$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=15,$ $X=10,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{15}(10) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(10)}{{log}_{10}(15)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(10)}{\\operatorname{log}(15)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1}{1.176\\,091} \\\\  & \\approx 0.850\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100674/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100674"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106046/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "29957c6e-c46a-5da1-952f-b90fda674c62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106046/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a39660-f172-58c9-b080-c4ef45aa5957" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-106046/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-106046/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106046" :question/id #uuid "79f2ac8f-706d-5c75-bc5d-c89a9e2fab4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106046" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cd847efd8b91b1c622e93b1ee97035bcee803a79a603aa7b4beee8fae77abf06.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-x+k \\\\ y & =-(0)^{2}+2(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=5$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106046/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae1738d1-3261-5769-ab80-0edcc76ab6f3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106046"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78108/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "f719f4d7-cf22-5f8a-8dfc-d38a20455eee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78108/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "788a116e-9747-58b0-969a-b50e6fe4712d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-78108/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-78108/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78108" :question/id #uuid "e6b1c009-5d14-58f7-8b31-aa7d55ec544b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78108" :question/problem "The number of bacteria in culture is given by the function $f(t)=-0.6{t}^{2}+10t+50,$ where $t$ is the time, in minutes, since the bacteria was isolated. What is the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first $10$ minutes?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=-0.6{t}^{2}+10t+50$ be the number of bacteria in the culture after $t$ minutes. To find the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first $10$ minutes, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,10]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)-f(0)}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-0.6(10)^{2}+10(10)+50]-[-0.6(0)^{2}+10(0)+50]}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{90-50}{10} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth over the first $10$ minutes is $4$ bacteria per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78108/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d13310c-2b80-5945-ada3-31d886f1ec48"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78108"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79540/answer/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "c5ea0012-4792-591a-9de7-6bbb67cc1e4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79540/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa6f51a-4684-5e94-8b70-ee3994a3c1cb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-79540/answer/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-79540/answer/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79540" :question/id #uuid "3c0f2338-2ab2-574c-954b-5e423f2531e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-79540" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{x-3}{2x}+\\frac{3+2x}{2x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $2x,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-3}{2x}+\\frac{3+2x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{x-3+3+2x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-3+3)+(x+2x)}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{3x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79540/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80d72276-63b0-52c6-b7cf-0f4e2e0392aa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79540"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "2d4c9c70-57f5-5518-a8ed-9b7f71ff2227" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "15950900-69d0-5101-973e-12dc011fb487" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "00f59731-286b-54c8-86e4-a1b98f41bf30" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7cd7cc04-afa2-5f31-b312-8df61565bf85" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "57308651-1460-57b4-a7d1-30b9fe7b42ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "126cee81-9a83-5954-83a2-22c702f4a5b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "e2230ade-0920-5220-9045-cd6d1c6a1d14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "b700074f-ff81-5931-a4ec-44982358a62b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2b5810-9eef-5df4-8922-1d1b9118af75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79249/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-79249/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-79249/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-79249/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-79249/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-79249/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-79249/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-79249/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-79249/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79249" :question/id #uuid "a1727d27-de04-53c4-a9de-9b0d34a8d70c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79249" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{3x+7}=-4$\nII. - $\\sqrt{5x-6}=2$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{4x-3}=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79249/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79249"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78618/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "ee485a03-1c8b-5057-919b-1f3f20f95696" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78618/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "61e3ceb3-20be-5e8a-9228-255334c275ce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-78618/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-78618/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78618" :question/id #uuid "2f233d26-a151-550e-abff-b0d56859a52f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78618" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=10-{x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=5-2x,$ find  $(f+g)(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(2),$ we find $f(2)$ and $g(2),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(2)$ and $g(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =10-(2)^{3}=2 \\\\ g(2) & =5-2(2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(2)=f(2)+g(2)=2+1=3\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(10-{x}^{3})+(5-2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2x+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(2) & =-(2)^{3}-2(2)+15=-8-4+15=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78618/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78618"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-549/answer/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "b721c5a8-f447-5d0a-8137-886105d797b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-549/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "690025c3-3162-54f0-9482-c6813f64a78f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-549/answer/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-549/answer/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-549" :question/id #uuid "f8437f94-1d26-5f61-9246-7a0a4a80faa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-549" :question/problem "${\\text{i}}^{14}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=14.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n14÷4=3\\,\\text{R}2.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{14}={\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-549/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "760a1a5b-4c06-547d-9248-a6acb97c4b84"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-549"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-538/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "da397cf0-577d-5fc2-a133-0ee5fec95c88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-538/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "8df60c92-dcf7-5f84-9b2b-e78a9ed3f90b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-538/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-538/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-538" :question/id #uuid "e22a5106-9b86-5df9-82bd-ae96786f3991" :question/math-academy-id "q-538" :question/problem "${({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{2015}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{2015}=(1)^{2015}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-538/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c51033-ad39-5776-9829-cf573214ef03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-538"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113033/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "663a2306-738c-57ca-bff9-92357aaf260b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113033/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "17777363-aed8-56fb-b2c6-70ad72085ac5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-113033/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-113033/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113033" :question/id #uuid "dbdb58f0-667e-5aa7-8bff-41bad7b32ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-113033" :question/problem "Simplify ${({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{26}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{26}=(1)^{26}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113033/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c51033-ad39-5776-9829-cf573214ef03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113033"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113087/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "4fc8407b-d6ef-533c-b4f4-5ab84427489d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113087/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "456339f4-a0d9-5d0b-a8d0-490f373da9dc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-113087/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-113087/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113087" :question/id #uuid "92f2f0b7-de4f-58b0-acaa-26e31d056305" :question/math-academy-id "q-113087" :question/problem "$9{\\text{i}}^{-4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n9{\\text{i}}^{-4}=\\frac{9}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{4}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}=\\frac{9}{1}=9.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-113087/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5128ae-312f-5a12-9a19-9158fa7ddb9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113087"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66490/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "593fe36f-a7fe-5072-a247-39f4491b9f08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66490/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "a58bb529-ef27-5657-bf19-9e025da5b449" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66490/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-66490/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66490" :question/id #uuid "1ba253e5-6b77-5991-9dca-b8648ddf6d5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66490" :question/problem "$-4{\\text{i}}^{-2}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n-4{\\text{i}}^{-2}=-\\frac{4}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}=-\\frac{4}{(-1)}=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-66490/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5128ae-312f-5a12-9a19-9158fa7ddb9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66490"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39830/answer/87ce719408fe82e1c74c35757a0410c396076303707c56172d25d0ac1fc27f60" :answer/id #uuid "d0798a66-db69-5497-b608-6cd7176099fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "142" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39830/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2b07a8-a82f-5b65-88a0-840cdbabae14" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-39830/answer/87ce719408fe82e1c74c35757a0410c396076303707c56172d25d0ac1fc27f60" :answer-field/choices ["q-39830/answer/87ce719408fe82e1c74c35757a0410c396076303707c56172d25d0ac1fc27f60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39830" :question/id #uuid "0845673e-0a7d-58b4-9254-65ae15f4f4cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39830" :question/problem "Solve $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the term $-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}$ by subtracting $8$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $-6$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2} & =12 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} & ={12}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =144 \\\\ x & =142.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{142+2}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{\\sqrt{144}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{12}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=142$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6$  is $x=142.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-39830/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39830"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39832/root-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "ef03f08c-e294-5878-842e-eb5f397b1825" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39832/root-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc8d9b98-e0a3-59ab-aa53-d00a41de0596" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-39832/root-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-39832/root-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39832/root-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "0b4e387d-4612-5b07-aae3-26f3b30882c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39832/root-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "736a8b92-b3e8-56c3-8089-3512e8c5dd0c" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-39832/root-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-39832/root-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39832" :question/id #uuid "98003bca-fa53-57e1-a8e6-79179173f6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-39832" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4}=4.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-4 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging the solutions into the original equation. \n\n- For $x=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-(-4)-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16+4-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-5-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-5-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values $x=-4,\\,5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4}=4$  are $x=-4$ and $x=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39832/root-1" "q-39832/root-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39832"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39833/root-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d3e1d694-1bdd-5948-8c96-890ceae62640" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39833/root-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "700ead04-7a16-50e8-975b-a572438d1758" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-39833/root-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-39833/root-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39833/root-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "99449027-c0b9-5dd4-9166-86aaa4b8715c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39833/root-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad8d745-e329-584d-8a11-ce5b919ce507" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-39833/root-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-39833/root-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39833" :question/id #uuid "d0f5b567-86e0-5c3b-94d7-e63615575a3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-39833" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37}=5.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37} & =5 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37})}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+37 & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging the solutions into the original equation. \n\n- For $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}-8\\cdot 2+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{4-16+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=6$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{6}^{2}-8\\cdot 6+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{36-48+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values $x=2,\\,6$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37}=5$  are $x=2$ and $x=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39833/root-1" "q-39833/root-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39833"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8d215e9d-5ee0-531c-9c56-59b308a6aae9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "986b5eb8-60ce-5158-9a08-c5ea42ea71a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "f0e5aee1-5d41-56e7-b00c-80ee9183cb2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "4c181833-3588-536c-81c1-ced79756a50a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "f1ebc174-5cc1-5840-9e5c-5604061f4908" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "3bac9522-9348-55e2-8330-c263d930694f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "ffe05e10-59ca-5e25-968e-10a0fd41d964" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "677dc537-57f0-5a09-8d3d-50b6e22a18b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f576e7a-0943-56d5-9e53-5531538e2081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79248/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-79248/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-79248/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-79248/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-79248/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-79248/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-79248/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-79248/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-79248/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79248" :question/id #uuid "e48707eb-c35d-5d97-8abc-4a048269c15c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79248" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{2x-4}=-2$\nII. - $\\sqrt{8-3x}=1$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{x-7}=4$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I states that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equation I has no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79248/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79248"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-4460/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "ba9a8eb1-1589-50a7-aec1-2595510c8f04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4460/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d213a5a-61a1-56dc-a89c-7e909aa9753d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-4460/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-4460/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-4460" :question/id #uuid "7f232a13-ac59-5c7f-b9ee-ff0c688c0095" :question/math-academy-id "q-4460" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(4x-2)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+3(1)+4}+6(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+3+4}+6 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+6 \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(4x-2) & =4(1)-2=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2} & =\\frac{8}{2}=4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-4460/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-4460"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-35252/answer/f1c9a73078cb2996ecfc62c940e8a02ddff429538e03df68ec9b668d318b2ed9" :answer/id #uuid "db5e330d-2cdf-5ad1-9ff4-ab4c886ff4bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "243" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35252/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e1331a0-8fbd-524a-8c07-5d64e2c40536" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-35252/answer/f1c9a73078cb2996ecfc62c940e8a02ddff429538e03df68ec9b668d318b2ed9" :answer-field/choices ["q-35252/answer/f1c9a73078cb2996ecfc62c940e8a02ddff429538e03df68ec9b668d318b2ed9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-35252" :question/id #uuid "dbe7843b-5fc3-5be1-af00-dea6856d2e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-35252" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2} & ={lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to 1}(4y-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{y\\to 1}(2{y}^{2}+1))}^{3}\\cdot {({lim}_{y\\to 1}(4y-1))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(2{(1)}^{2}+1)}^{3}{(4(1)-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{3}(3)^{2} \\\\  & =243\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-35252/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-35252"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133093/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9875c946-efae-5e29-b405-94de8fc3b770" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133093/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d72cf376-ab9d-5294-aa38-2dbf171dad01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133093/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-133093/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133093" :question/id #uuid "4facbf96-c4c8-5f38-a25b-4b60a9204fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-133093" :question/problem "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>3,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=2x-5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =2(4)-5 \\\\  & =8-5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133093/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8daba5bf-5162-5779-9512-f22d0c8c0320"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133093"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133094/field-1/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "8de2c845-8e92-58ae-94f4-adea8d283336" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133094/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3867252-3213-5383-8a8b-466b65ee7411" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133094/field-1/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer-field/choices ["q-133094/field-1/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133094" :question/id #uuid "dac5550f-7f7a-5ad7-9089-a64688784e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-133094" :question/problem "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133094/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d522a8-ae3c-5723-909e-540ddac1d9da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133094"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133095/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "583857a8-0144-5997-9ea1-7321299750da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133095/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4123edc7-00ac-585c-92fc-0df39a14aa4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133095/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-133095/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133095" :question/id #uuid "1d26441c-ac3b-59d3-b427-084701de0ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133095" :question/problem "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f76f7203f1ba17f8148bbc1140420034a631f46e6995a3bda5453ec3fd9a6f55.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-4.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133095/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133095"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133098/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "2ec85d45-705f-58f2-9ecb-beeb96887d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133098/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba2c0da-5e9e-5cee-afa1-b64d8b64f6c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133098/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-133098/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133098" :question/id #uuid "db7e3081-13ac-5bac-97f6-3cdfeabac772" :question/math-academy-id "q-133098" :question/problem "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$\n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=3.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(3) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133098/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f954ff1-3f02-5fb1-aca2-5911d5636167"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133098"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133099/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "b93ae47e-c9a4-5c74-b810-01aab25b220c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133099/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9082cc46-6619-50c2-b109-27b601c47f11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133099/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-133099/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133099/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "59a2bc2f-fe61-5929-96b2-b7033bdcf072" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133099/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d698e12-f6f5-591d-a04a-2546aa9ce49f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133099/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-133099/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133099" :question/id #uuid "a61a79f3-a45a-5593-b4bc-eb0ce92364fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-133099" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/47eb01a66773e5cf2b223cc3f8f4ff02caa424170a8e090ec0b1e45ed6d69741.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(2,4).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $4,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y\\le 4,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-2\\le g(x)\\le 4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-133099/field-1" "q-133099/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133099"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133100/field-1/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "607fe3ff-c70e-5e90-8be3-b217f42489ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133100/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c06794a5-596c-5119-8f53-ec6c5b564819" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-133100/field-1/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer-field/choices ["q-133100/field-1/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133100" :question/id #uuid "1c61acd1-ade2-5a44-8b2b-c8e5c8ab07c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-133100" :question/problem "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-2$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-2)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-133100/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133100"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-67696/answer/a5bcf5044d7131cdf0d721e80125a87ad3d00df1e947910bf38c9dd3889981de" :answer/id #uuid "db6c0abe-be01-5a18-89bf-81ac56e02c61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-67696/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7242dff-1d2d-5dcb-a2ec-c3039ce685d0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-67696/answer/a5bcf5044d7131cdf0d721e80125a87ad3d00df1e947910bf38c9dd3889981de" :answer-field/choices ["q-67696/answer/a5bcf5044d7131cdf0d721e80125a87ad3d00df1e947910bf38c9dd3889981de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-67696" :question/id #uuid "93e1f776-dbc1-58f3-a68d-2ac0bb33599b" :question/math-academy-id "q-67696" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{z\\to -1}(z-2)^{4}}{{lim}_{z\\to -1}(z+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{z\\to -1}(z-2))}^{4}}{{({lim}_{z\\to -1}(z+2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1-2)^{4}}{(-1+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{1} \\\\  & =81\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-67696/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-67696"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100236/answer/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" :answer/id #uuid "b4271776-2565-508b-a4c0-05856a0d5c1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "216" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100236/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "32124313-f85d-5185-9fc8-c729515e34da" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100236/answer/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" :answer-field/choices ["q-100236/answer/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100236" :question/id #uuid "91317a16-9fb5-5d43-9550-4a0b2404efdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-100236" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3) & =({lim}_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2)^{3})\\cdot ({lim}_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & ={({lim}_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2))}^{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & =((-2)^{3}+2)^{3}\\cdot (2\\cdot (-2)+3) \\\\  & =(-6)^{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100236/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100236"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100277/answer/ce9ad5740269388d77893fbd186fe766ceb1482af44117dcfc7940e9dca5a239" :answer/id #uuid "e708382f-3705-58a5-9a18-333922210b38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100277/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "5df85a12-1e10-5d7f-ad90-818a82b74222" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100277/answer/ce9ad5740269388d77893fbd186fe766ceb1482af44117dcfc7940e9dca5a239" :answer-field/choices ["q-100277/answer/ce9ad5740269388d77893fbd186fe766ceb1482af44117dcfc7940e9dca5a239"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100277" :question/id #uuid "d37402e0-45e5-5794-8d62-3744bcf93a6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100277" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ddf4b2d129022e27627afa1b0946f18ef7e98ab2ffe20dea6d609bedce65f84d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-3(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(3(2)-4(1))^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =-32\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100277/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100277"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-17280/answer/56036268a30c37b8f6f7ed9733d0d7c7d565bff4b2cccbbe7debbdd38b648585" :answer/id #uuid "8fbd1f6f-6266-508a-9271-24f3d4543ce2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-17280/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7063e60-d887-53de-8f03-68e4e7251e17" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-17280/answer/56036268a30c37b8f6f7ed9733d0d7c7d565bff4b2cccbbe7debbdd38b648585" :answer-field/choices ["q-17280/answer/56036268a30c37b8f6f7ed9733d0d7c7d565bff4b2cccbbe7debbdd38b648585"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-17280" :question/id #uuid "6f7fded4-a7f4-5dbc-a96e-2d5c3e995127" :question/math-academy-id "q-17280" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5e8383b56998815d4c981e72c946fdbf8de77ece15e52ca2ed5c7371c8f2c8af.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=4.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)=4(4)-12=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)={3}^{2}-2(3)+2=5\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-17280/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-17280"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100275/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7d6a2bcd-3314-5ee1-8472-b7a09d44d3db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100275/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "99476705-d581-5b54-a13c-b34652061ee2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100275/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-100275/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100275" :question/id #uuid "beb0d5cc-39b2-586c-83f5-8cf543207207" :question/math-academy-id "q-100275" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e8aac08a40b3a318cb682afca1783a8d9563bf67f0b51bdff2ead8a9bf28117c.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}={lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{3}+4)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(-8+4)}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-2(2))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-4)}^{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2}{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & =16\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100275/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100275"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-77745/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "f3a77654-921b-5889-8e20-20ce6a10e20c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77745/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "f97970c3-1cbe-5e91-83e2-dc25e22d9a12" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-77745/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-77745/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77745" :question/id #uuid "3fd05b19-c616-5951-a797-4ee3e4c55001" :question/math-academy-id "q-77745" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4b3ef4ce6c3c2d40c60639b8467649155fb7e8a3c018dff359bf766a86cf8054.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-1.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1)=3(-1)-1=-4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1)=-1-1=-2\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)-1))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-1))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{(-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-77745/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77745"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-35621/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "e2404cbf-3ca7-59ae-a93b-18a00631f502" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35621/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9111b4-8506-5726-8c62-7d58cfb11fa0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-35621/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-35621/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-35621" :question/id #uuid "18d96d23-85b4-508f-bbdc-a059435bb5b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-35621" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e647ebf6e8332e9be5abd75e0f5ac5102c89c4a40590cef21a8ddfc65a17701d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}={lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x))^{4}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3(-1)^{2}-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x))^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x)))}^{4} \\\\  & ={(2-1)}^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1))}^{2}{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x)))}^{4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-35621/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-35621"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100264/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "2e7f0189-8bdb-58cf-8aaa-952657ad5d46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100264/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "d92060c6-8853-5b65-a4a6-b4eb8d49cf80" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100264/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-100264/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100264" :question/id #uuid "855a5e48-e137-5598-94f7-f4832740f0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-100264" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{({lim}_{x\\to 4}({x}^{2}-3x-5))}^{5}}-\\sqrt{{({lim}_{x\\to 4}(2x-7))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{({4}^{2}-3(4)-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2(4)-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(16-12-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(8-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-1}-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-100264/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100264"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-77740/answer/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "052bd356-c7b0-5129-9bcb-82306acddb73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77740/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "288f7944-c565-5e24-8c60-31ea17ec42df" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-77740/answer/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer-field/choices ["q-77740/answer/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77740" :question/id #uuid "a8ee25c9-f495-51c7-80ce-fd000c80af30" :question/math-academy-id "q-77740" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1)}{{lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x-2)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1) & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+9}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25}-1 \\\\  & =5-1 \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x-2) & =3(4)-2 \\\\  & =10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2} & =\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-77740/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77740"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-35295/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "5bc01e6e-6ce6-5bc8-b813-3f42cc3847c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35295/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d3983f-d0e2-5d03-9246-2bc06892aa79" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-35295/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-35295/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-35295" :question/id #uuid "238f1bf7-e926-5a9a-b458-b3d344bdf40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35295" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/95e092490e087b5245f05b352b4738beb1ce3d9d649ea289ded881d0728129e5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}f(x)=1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}(4(f(x))^{4}+10x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(1)^{4}+10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-35295/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10100e89-21d6-53c5-a7de-f14fd73ce4b7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-35295"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-67702/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "249be7b5-1906-53fd-b666-f188a9dbdb3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-67702/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "369ba6c0-530d-5f3a-85c4-0d0286705012" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-67702/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-67702/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-67702" :question/id #uuid "f14e9302-b81e-5bd4-b3e0-dc1fe5b82277" :question/math-academy-id "q-67702" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1786cfa3ea3b08a97e59ec148a8fe710d6e4b11fc1cbc3582c4bf925a41bf19f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=4.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}(17-2f(x))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17-2\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-67702/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10100e89-21d6-53c5-a7de-f14fd73ce4b7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-67702"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-103216/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "36d47bf5-d562-56c1-981f-81ce1bfb0996" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103216/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a58f9f1-7f33-5634-9a0d-d31653864f38" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-103216/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-103216/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103216" :question/id #uuid "9cc04cfe-5c1c-58c0-b534-2ea8f4897bac" :question/math-academy-id "q-103216" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=-2$ and ${a}_{2}=0,$ then ${a}_{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =0+(-2) \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-103216/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103216"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-103217/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "8ffa1a3f-0c84-5504-a5b3-3da85ca9f1c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103217/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c87d266-0ee7-554e-9da5-6db1aebe77e7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-103217/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-103217/answer/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103217" :question/id #uuid "d61383ab-6ca0-5c21-90fc-da34d0ca7d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-103217" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=4$ and ${a}_{2}=5,$ then ${a}_{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4+5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-103217/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103217"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-103223/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7149c441-adba-55e5-97a8-21d1a37322d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103223/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "0531825e-15d9-55ac-8251-aacc8bb9e54a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-103223/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-103223/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103223" :question/id #uuid "22ea45b9-f009-53ac-920f-698b600ba579" :question/math-academy-id "q-103223" :question/problem "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=-1$ and $f(2)=1,$ then $f(3)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =1+(-1) \\\\ f(3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(3)=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-103223/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103223"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-23197/answer/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "de5aa4ab-4544-59c2-b200-37613f4e1558" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-23197/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "eaecb80d-b491-5cb5-a4f4-54d654e1a717" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-23197/answer/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-23197/answer/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-23197" :question/id #uuid "3c1dd647-0f9b-5884-88c2-d3741fd6e4c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-23197" :question/problem "Given that ${log}_{3}x=\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{k},$ what is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=3$ to base $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}x & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}3} & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{{log}_{9}({9}^{1/2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}x=\\frac{{log}_{9}x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}.\n$$\n\nHence, $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-23197/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87ee4eae-702f-59f0-aff4-46c9acb89f6c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-23197"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66880/answer/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "b03a8b08-8e7b-5cd4-a133-f21c8ad23ce8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66880/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "c060282b-a228-5149-88ee-93425f941ef2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66880/answer/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer-field/choices ["q-66880/answer/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66880" :question/id #uuid "bf175bf7-41d9-5d9c-9ca1-8254b6c5a5b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-66880" :question/problem "${log}_{25}(\\sqrt{125})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=25$ and $X=\\sqrt{125}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $5,$ we set $b=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{25}(\\sqrt{125}) & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(\\sqrt{125})}{{log}_{5}(25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(\\sqrt{{5}^{3}})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}({5}^{3/2})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-66880/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ab12b8-b1c9-5192-91be-cebc968d9587"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66880"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66934/answer/82ee0ad984b180e3da43b631af39d78de4dccdd3c66641b901f329ec15284009" :answer/id #uuid "fa06c276-2514-578f-8d2c-d26527f7e6c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.71" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66934/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "49f59870-2611-597e-8a30-5301563263a2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66934/answer/82ee0ad984b180e3da43b631af39d78de4dccdd3c66641b901f329ec15284009" :answer-field/choices ["q-66934/answer/82ee0ad984b180e3da43b631af39d78de4dccdd3c66641b901f329ec15284009"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66934" :question/id #uuid "1e980299-bf4e-5e95-b34b-d63085c5feea" :question/math-academy-id "q-66934" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}(35)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}(35)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=35,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(35) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(35)}{{log}_{10}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(35)}{\\operatorname{log}(8)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.544\\,07}{0.903\\,09} \\\\  & \\approx 1.71\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-66934/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66934"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-24635/answer/616600ecb5838b042dafdcd9a6effc2a64c325ef1a8410a0ccafed0e1f6dc75f" :answer/id #uuid "61dadb9c-cc86-5b9c-90e9-dd71a729ccee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No real roots" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-24635/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc1d6bc0-9357-50f3-a715-df23fe79ae2d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-24635/answer/616600ecb5838b042dafdcd9a6effc2a64c325ef1a8410a0ccafed0e1f6dc75f" :answer-field/choices ["q-24635/answer/616600ecb5838b042dafdcd9a6effc2a64c325ef1a8410a0ccafed0e1f6dc75f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-24635" :question/id #uuid "4ba2473a-7860-526f-a636-2580b5f71073" :question/math-academy-id "q-24635" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2c688a8451bcdf9a0fcc23f0dea2355c66c73f6114ef07f48535d9dd3e5eeb12.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No real roots\"." :question/worked-solution "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve does not intercept the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the parabola does not have any roots." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-24635/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa081d4-f85a-5239-b034-b78894429611"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-24635"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-3043/a/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "50506808-de9a-51bb-9395-185ea71aaaad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3043/a" :answer-field/id #uuid "34d701d3-71f6-53e4-bd4d-224633ad5700" :answer-field/key "a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-3043/a/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-3043/a/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-3043" :question/id #uuid "2b6e17eb-b41f-58d1-bd2b-5852d16cb44e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3043" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2.$\n\n$a$: {{a}}" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{2a+4}{5} & =4 \\\\ 2a+4 & =20 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 8+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{16+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $a=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2$  is $a=8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-3043/a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-3043"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39825/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "fcf4dce6-1e7d-5176-950f-96b4537b78af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39825/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "d059153d-357b-56ea-b351-d096c5692bff" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-39825/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-39825/answer/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39825" :question/id #uuid "193e9c3b-5823-5fe7-b42e-9a9b3e4a9d8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39825" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{8x-5}=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8x-5} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{8x-5})}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ 8x-5 & =3 \\\\ 8x & =8 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8\\cdot 1-5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{8-5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=1$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{8x-5}=\\sqrt{3}$  is $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-39825/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3"] 