ey "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114606/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-114606/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-114606/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-114606/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-114606/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-114606/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-114606/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-114606/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-114606/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114606" :question/id #uuid "55431f3f-1e50-5d2e-ae53-dc462ee5ce94" :question/math-academy-id "q-114606" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{7-2x}=-2$\nII. - $\\sqrt{x-3}=-7$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{6x-2}=5$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and II state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and II have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114606/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114606"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70913/root-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "b547e8bd-ee5a-56dc-abe0-5665aab273f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70913/root-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf3b6073-83d3-5a4b-be0c-891adaa49651" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70913/root-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-70913/root-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70913/root-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "624dc58c-08a0-55d9-80aa-dc7d9874467a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70913/root-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bf043e1-fb2b-52ea-83c7-e40ff55f2741" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70913/root-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-70913/root-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70913" :question/id #uuid "90b1010c-b5bd-524a-86d1-a7ed6794e479" :question/math-academy-id "q-70913" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root by adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+6 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-3(-2)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+6+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-3(5)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-15+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=-2$ and $x=5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-70913/root-1" "q-70913/root-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70913"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100676/answer/e28e19cff8fd8ef2c866b74dd070e53088112e7feeb02701648a2ab960c86ef7" :answer/id #uuid "28a98f76-933b-551d-9eab-cfe391420e07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.969" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100676/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "601db05c-084b-5144-a803-3fc02ba6ccb5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100676/answer/e28e19cff8fd8ef2c866b74dd070e53088112e7feeb02701648a2ab960c86ef7" :answer-field/choices ["q-100676/answer/e28e19cff8fd8ef2c866b74dd070e53088112e7feeb02701648a2ab960c86ef7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100676" :question/id #uuid "2c2c3dd1-20e9-5368-9ec4-088bb43cb692" :question/math-academy-id "q-100676" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{22}(20)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{22}(20)$ by changing the base $22$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=22,$ $X=20,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{22}(20) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(20)}{{log}_{10}(22)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(20)}{\\operatorname{log}(22)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.301\\,030}{1.342\\,423} \\\\  & \\approx 0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100676/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100676"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100675/answer/36f411d803d3d277481a0781d98c8e9c64d97ae3b1730405e61a65241c2d48e5" :answer/id #uuid "28c51bc3-a49c-557b-86c0-c22af227fbd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100675/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "722b0dfa-4a0b-5cc5-9de6-df174301443b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-100675/answer/36f411d803d3d277481a0781d98c8e9c64d97ae3b1730405e61a65241c2d48e5" :answer-field/choices ["q-100675/answer/36f411d803d3d277481a0781d98c8e9c64d97ae3b1730405e61a65241c2d48e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100675" :question/id #uuid "c2f720ea-4efd-5c38-87f6-787d4de5f50b" :question/math-academy-id "q-100675" :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}(30)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}(30)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=30,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(30) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(30)}{{log}_{10}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(30)}{\\operatorname{log}(8)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.477\\,12}{0.903\\,09} \\\\  & \\approx 1.64\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100675/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100675"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-108374/answer/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "f6bae5cc-a920-50b7-932f-de5a6e7134cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108374/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f15f019-2ba2-5997-922b-a7d174fc823e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-108374/answer/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer-field/choices ["q-108374/answer/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-108374" :question/id #uuid "4051d6e3-232c-59f8-9d4f-58de8b478ba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-108374" :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{125}(625)$ without using a calculator.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=125,$ and $X=625.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $5,$ we set $b=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{125}(625) & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(625)}{{log}_{5}(125)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}({5}^{4})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-108374/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ab12b8-b1c9-5192-91be-cebc968d9587"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108374"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66887/answer/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "a37b4e09-7c5d-59e7-869c-54c3b277f653" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66887/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e6003b-62b3-5c4a-a6fd-c807d1c3bba4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66887/answer/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer-field/choices ["q-66887/answer/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66887" :question/id #uuid "47a63a18-20ff-5e2a-89aa-c14176c04a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-66887" :question/problem "${log}_{9}(\\sqrt{27})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=9$ and $X=\\sqrt{27}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $3,$ we set $b=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}(\\sqrt{27}) & =\\frac{{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{27})}{{log}_{3}(9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{{3}^{3}})}{{log}_{3}({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}({3}^{3/2})}{{log}_{3}({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-66887/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ab12b8-b1c9-5192-91be-cebc968d9587"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66887"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-2432/answer/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" :answer/id #uuid "d7a199b2-1930-5e1a-a031-920f36d45057" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2432/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ef738b-16f7-55c6-9245-458eb8fb9ec6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-2432/answer/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" :answer-field/choices ["q-2432/answer/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2432" :question/id #uuid "de2abca8-7e9d-54bd-8cdb-f6670388afba" :question/math-academy-id "q-2432" :question/problem "A curve is defined as $y=3{x}^{2}-7.$ Find the average rate of change of $y$ over the interval $[2,5].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-7.$ Applying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(2)}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3(5)^{2}-7]-[3(2)^{2}-7]}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{68-5}{3} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2432/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68635d27-a9ef-541b-a57b-d9f5f422ae85"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2432"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-20823/answer/a20b3c9808c291c56ec5bfc5f0f93a856fc692714ca7ebd36c5e4bc361f7075c" :answer/id #uuid "a978ec35-daa3-5475-b451-48874291889c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20823/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "d69339cc-4899-5ceb-bc91-5dc45b77c967" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-20823/answer/a20b3c9808c291c56ec5bfc5f0f93a856fc692714ca7ebd36c5e4bc361f7075c" :answer-field/choices ["q-20823/answer/a20b3c9808c291c56ec5bfc5f0f93a856fc692714ca7ebd36c5e4bc361f7075c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-20823" :question/id #uuid "a2e31816-43ec-559b-a472-2ed0efb972fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-20823" :question/problem "A curve is defined as $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x.$ Find the average rate of change of $y$ over the interval $[1,4].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x.$  Applying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{[2({4}^{3})-{4}^{2}+5(4)]-[2({1}^{3})-{1}^{2}+5(1)]}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{132-6}{3} \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-20823/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68635d27-a9ef-541b-a57b-d9f5f422ae85"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-20823"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-19154/root-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "8877ffb7-f58c-5fbd-ba52-99c8f52a63f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19154/root-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f53d7482-1cba-564a-9af7-5083dcff0367" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-19154/root-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-19154/root-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19154/root-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "f11677ac-9262-56f2-8cbc-9d4cbc75e73a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19154/root-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f890d1d-8aea-54ba-95ff-94ffdb406cff" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-19154/root-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-19154/root-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19154" :question/id #uuid "149a5bb5-23b4-56cd-ac8a-edc3c7f35e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-19154" :question/problem "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,23]$ is $1.5,$ find the value of $p.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ | $\\,18\\,$ | $\\,21\\,$ | $\\,23\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-{p}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,-13\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ |\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(23)-f(1)}{23-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-(-{p}^{2}-4)}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=1.5$ on the interval $[1,23].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22}=1.5.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22} & =1.5 \\\\ {p}^{2}+17 & =33 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =33-17 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =16 \\\\ p & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=-4$ or $p=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-19154/root-1" "q-19154/root-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f7224e3-9c13-51e7-a3f7-0e379f63a861"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-19154"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-101730/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "92f610ab-f996-522b-87af-a4586c3c9d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101730/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "accec121-d526-5711-937f-a3434f20da60" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-101730/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-101730/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-101730" :question/id #uuid "a9ca4c60-8351-5109-8b21-60704b041116" :question/math-academy-id "q-101730" :question/problem "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ is $-1,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,4p+1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,p+6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(-5)}{5-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(p+6)-(4p+1)}{5+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-3p}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=-1$ on the interval $[-5,5].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3p}{10}=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-3p}{10} & =-1 \\\\ 5-3p & =-10 \\\\ -3p & =-10-5 \\\\ -3p & =-15 \\\\ p & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-101730/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f7224e3-9c13-51e7-a3f7-0e379f63a861"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101730"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-21123/answer/b0ddd5bc5736e2ec0bd1838fff648976f8b2ead559f325198e1377e7e75624e6" :answer/id #uuid "4dab1977-579e-50a4-86c6-15428cba7f1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "187" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21123/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "d381769d-59ee-54e7-87b7-fa734ad44d15" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-21123/answer/b0ddd5bc5736e2ec0bd1838fff648976f8b2ead559f325198e1377e7e75624e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-21123/answer/b0ddd5bc5736e2ec0bd1838fff648976f8b2ead559f325198e1377e7e75624e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21123" :question/id #uuid "95d04696-3038-5931-a2a7-b332a1053193" :question/math-academy-id "q-21123" :question/problem "The number of machines infected by a computer virus is represented by the function $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11,$ where $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus was first detected. What is the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11$ be the number of machines infected by the computer virus after $t$ days. To find the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,7]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)-f(0)}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[4(7)^{3}-2(7)^{2}+5(7)+11]-[4(0)^{3}-2(0)^{2}+5(0)+11]}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1309}{7} \\\\  & =187\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth over the first week is $187$ machines per day." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-21123/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d13310c-2b80-5945-ada3-31d886f1ec48"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21123"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-218682/field-1/4b741b651b6f24cd7370ee85fd4c2dd5b1d9bdb7c612965a77adc26bd59b4a9a" :answer/id #uuid "66b073db-b437-5dc9-988f-2b5987716d4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "41" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-218682/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "de6e5463-ac4d-5d4f-a5c3-955e6ac78200" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-218682/field-1/4b741b651b6f24cd7370ee85fd4c2dd5b1d9bdb7c612965a77adc26bd59b4a9a" :answer-field/choices ["q-218682/field-1/4b741b651b6f24cd7370ee85fd4c2dd5b1d9bdb7c612965a77adc26bd59b4a9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-218682" :question/id #uuid "d77c7163-8f4b-52b0-9277-4b1a28b029a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-218682" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of a city, measured in thousands of people, $t$ years after $2012,$ is given by the expression $P(t)=5{t}^{2}+t.$\n\nThe average annual population growth rate from $2012$ to $2020$ was\n{{field-1}}\nthousand people." :question/worked-solution "Let $P(t)=5{t}^{2}+t$ be the population of the city, in thousands of people, $t$ years after $2012.$ Since $2020$ is $8$ years after $2012$, to find the average annual population growth rate from $2012$ to $2020$, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,8]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(8)-f(0)}{8-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5({8}^{2})+8)-(5({0}^{2})+0)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{328-0}{8} \\\\  & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, The average annual population growth rate from $2012$ to $2020$ was $41$ thousand people." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-218682/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d13310c-2b80-5945-ada3-31d886f1ec48"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-218682"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-87562/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87562/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dbac9f-85f4-59c4-8a53-10bde3633c1d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-87562/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-87562/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-87562" :question/id #uuid "7dfa7943-f9fd-587a-96e7-82a3ae160d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-87562" :question/problem "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-87562/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eab4249-544e-56c6-81c0-e6db84033701"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-87562"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-19160/answer/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "7415d098-c067-5d3a-a6ae-5cd2e605e7b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19160/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3ef0df-5c83-5dcc-979e-1d960c4c3f11" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-19160/answer/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-19160/answer/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19160" :question/id #uuid "2e3ab4e9-a63e-5231-88df-c45b46948041" :question/math-academy-id "q-19160" :question/problem "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ is $3,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-11\\,$ | $\\,2p-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,p+10\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-2)}{3-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(p+10)-(2p-1)}{3+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-p}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=3$ on the interval $[-2,3].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{11-p}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11-p}{5} & =3 \\\\ 11-p & =15 \\\\ -p & =15-11 \\\\ -p & =4 \\\\ p & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-19160/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f7224e3-9c13-51e7-a3f7-0e379f63a861"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-19160"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-41478/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7475235a-62e7-5fae-98a6-1664adf39916" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-41478/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "1891fb5a-ae3c-53e5-bcfb-2e2ebdc3a191" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-41478/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-41478/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-41478" :question/id #uuid "894785c9-b37d-573f-b85e-093f2abc4eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-41478" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/34ba4a4dcd6655d68fbc20fb6192354ae755cc7eaf14a2459e9b7012ffe6d9ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $4,$ what is the value of $f(62)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the period is $4,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(62) & =f(62-4) \\\\  & =f(62-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(62-15\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =f(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-41478/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "282b873c-1ba0-5205-b75f-c58721ffaaff"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-41478"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e35d3b70-777c-576e-9426-bd095fa9b141" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6be2d733-af51-531c-a6ae-fb3b8f9f6ff2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "8f387637-8b17-53e2-9637-872e8c44aabf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "8b88f3ab-2919-5f84-8aa0-b48c86a90793" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2de2d434-323b-5bd0-88c3-4a9168bb5e3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "e6b21ac8-297f-5559-8890-905d5525ed71" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "2ab74f05-3afc-5968-87f4-be4d044e94c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "57b09224-30af-51ef-b156-ee01bb809d0a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1396d91b-da49-5297-9871-ffd0d3227bab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-90623/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-90623/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-90623/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-90623/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-90623/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-90623/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-90623/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-90623/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-90623/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-90623" :question/id #uuid "029317bc-fcf6-501c-9125-ecc74c217368" :question/math-academy-id "q-90623" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/632da466eb26480490a49f9c5a71e0d6f95653efa749a8f117d81e016fd969ae.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\nII. - $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=3$\nIII. - $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(4).$ For example, $f(1)>f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function does *not* have a global minimum at $x=3.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is smaller than $f(3).$ For example, $f(6)<f(3).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=1.$ For example, in the interval $(0.5,1.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (0.5,1.5).$ \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-90623/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c98ce69-8410-51a4-8d9c-eec7b46fb83b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90623"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-88205/coordinate-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "58d8976a-8d6a-571f-aa3c-4dfb863d3bb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88205/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4523332-86c1-57a4-af85-58822a8ad619" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-88205/coordinate-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-88205/coordinate-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-88205/coordinate-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "2fcfc92c-4860-5df6-b3a0-3c09ec63b05a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88205/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "52a41c4b-0097-56b8-a60f-6552b9d773bb" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-88205/coordinate-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-88205/coordinate-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-88205" :question/id #uuid "76d5aa8d-6b7f-5a59-a437-5e21bbd91bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-88205" :question/problem "A stretch of factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and invariant $x$-axis is applied to the point $(5,-6).$  What is the resulting point?\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying a stretch of factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and invariant $x$-axis to a point means that the $y$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,-\\frac{1}{2}y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is\n\n$$\n(5,-6)↦(5,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6))=(5,3).\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/76787875dff29fa140881c6de2ee0d7c08baf394994e31246204d21bbcf61f56.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-88205/coordinate-1" "q-88205/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8effb25-abe6-5e37-8f2c-91ad12868b1a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-88205"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-5145/answer/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "ab16b9c5-9977-585e-b838-599705aeba9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-5145/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "81f591d8-00b2-566c-99df-1c6a9f783203" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-5145/answer/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-5145/answer/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-5145" :question/id #uuid "64793999-0fa3-539c-b945-76159b1213b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-5145" :question/problem "Find $p$ if $2$ is a root of $f(x)=12{x}^{2}-3px.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since  $x=2$ is a root, we have $f(2)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=2,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(2)^{2}-3p(2) & =0 \\\\ 12(4)-6p & =0 \\\\ 48-6p & =0 \\\\ -6p & =-48 \\\\ p & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-5145/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9328cc9a-2e6c-5ac2-bae4-f02c86fe9d29"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-5145"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72548/answer/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "f6489295-a743-5a1b-ba1c-3a1a21887946" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72548/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d2fb2ba-1a63-50be-8a2f-06d6293b98a4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-72548/answer/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-72548/answer/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72548" :question/id #uuid "0e3fd00d-e0e6-58dd-a905-2ad7d3579ca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72548" :question/problem "The lines $2y+10x=3$ and $y=px-3$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The second line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=px-3$ is $p.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $2y+10x=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+10x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =-10x+3 \\\\ y & =-5x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $2y+10x=3$ is $-5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-5\\cdot p=-1\\,⟹\\,p=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-72548/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72548"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127614/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "18c6267b-b6e1-588e-a95e-c1c0f10bdcac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127614/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "71885dd8-5771-5616-abe8-685fda47e1b8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-127614/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-127614/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127614" :question/id #uuid "4b0686b1-334e-545d-8566-76f487f19d14" :question/math-academy-id "q-127614" :question/problem "If\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127614/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccdde46-a4ab-517e-910b-571a3a166b6a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127614"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-134527/answer/52a6ad1bd1a24d6b7503ec5dcc6b68b626c406fc446833302b43b6c1e407465a" :answer/id #uuid "429a1aa9-f72c-50b7-a22c-27192a8b7efa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.65%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134527/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca19158c-3589-51ce-9250-40e2ad38e966" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-134527/answer/52a6ad1bd1a24d6b7503ec5dcc6b68b626c406fc446833302b43b6c1e407465a" :answer-field/choices ["q-134527/answer/52a6ad1bd1a24d6b7503ec5dcc6b68b626c406fc446833302b43b6c1e407465a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-134527" :question/id #uuid "fbd05050-bcf2-5bb4-9011-c5d2c51ceabc" :question/math-academy-id "q-134527" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.1)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}\\approx 1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.1)^{5} & =(1+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & \\approx 1+5(0.1)+10(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.1)+10(0.01) \\\\  & =1+0.5+0.1 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.1)^{5}-1.6|}{(1.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & \\approx 0.65%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-134527/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-134527"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127701/answer/87f70942b9483cb5cedbd7b4150ebc995451772f024ba0c75aaaea5741b3bd06" :answer/id #uuid "cedb97a5-a477-5288-a35c-c5df85b5d92d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "68" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127701/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f71b00e-de6c-5483-9764-a413e5bd29b4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-127701/answer/87f70942b9483cb5cedbd7b4150ebc995451772f024ba0c75aaaea5741b3bd06" :answer-field/choices ["q-127701/answer/87f70942b9483cb5cedbd7b4150ebc995451772f024ba0c75aaaea5741b3bd06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127701" :question/id #uuid "f7eee920-abd1-5e92-872a-5f2d162a508d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127701" :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{32}$ in the matrix\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ (third row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}8 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 3 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8\\cdot 6-4\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =48+20 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127701/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e1b4442-eca7-5283-9d5d-5bcbd1d67975"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127701"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-73017/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "cb5f0847-6dca-5e86-959a-5ce40230bdf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73017/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "708fe7b0-f364-5757-a9a4-3709636c5143" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-73017/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-73017/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73017" :question/id #uuid "4b55051a-8f37-5733-840a-d51d5d26cfae" :question/math-academy-id "q-73017" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $9!=9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!$ and $6!=6\\cdot 5!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $7!$ and $5!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!} & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!\\cdot 5!}{7!\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{7!}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{6} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-73017/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "203386bf-cdf1-5a78-92fd-36fababfb971"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73017"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532/r1c1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "f00ea6a0-13f9-519c-9283-1433fc6754ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532/r1c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "50d71f43-0198-534a-a76e-78a5c7ff66fc" :answer-field/key "r1c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-11532/r1c1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-11532/r1c1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532/r1c2/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "12c3c137-8602-5f1d-96d8-8266ec5a6048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532/r1c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "de2d9f41-421d-5340-a0d1-369099542203" :answer-field/key "r1c2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-11532/r1c2/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer-field/choices ["q-11532/r1c2/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532/r2c1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "3e54d8f1-4f94-5d30-b05f-3a8b2466941f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532/r2c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e14cc66-77c0-5c25-ba6e-f662695dd674" :answer-field/key "r2c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-11532/r2c1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-11532/r2c1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532/r2c2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "a707a8e1-6dba-5c6c-96e5-30b0f82458c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532/r2c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "440e4888-7883-5d03-99f9-c1a1ee936bb1" :answer-field/key "r2c2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-11532/r2c2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-11532/r2c2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-11532" :question/id #uuid "68388deb-c99d-58dc-919b-11e840c4302b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11532" :question/problem "Calculate $2H-3L$, where\n$H=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$L=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 1, column 2: {{r1c2}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 2: {{r2c2}}" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $H$ by $2$ and each entry of $L$ by $3,$ and then subtract the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2H-3L & =2[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 0 & 3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-0 & -4-3 \\\\ 4-6 & -2-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-11532/r1c1" "q-11532/r1c2" "q-11532/r2c1" "q-11532/r2c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6727fdd-a614-5ead-9273-08c0a7fec997"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-11532"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "19fea0f1-db68-53b8-b64d-2d81bb965cd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550/r1c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8e1263-f3fe-579d-8e20-d7beb1ea8ae8" :answer-field/key "r1c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-13550/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-13550/r1c1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550/r1c2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "87bc8ad6-e689-5792-9a80-b23182d9d445" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550/r1c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "db11b31c-592b-587b-af27-7145a6680bc1" :answer-field/key "r1c2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-13550/r1c2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-13550/r1c2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550/r2c1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "25e2da5a-b65e-5688-8adf-7728bf6c3d29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550/r2c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a458555-43c2-595b-9bea-97070415fc6b" :answer-field/key "r2c1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-13550/r2c1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-13550/r2c1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550/r2c2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "827ee7ec-62cb-5317-8a23-cb58c8957e2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550/r2c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "73f5c65a-f17f-5663-b1ae-b6481c8333c5" :answer-field/key "r2c2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-13550/r2c2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-13550/r2c2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-13550" :question/id #uuid "eb48a317-e064-5f22-b418-b65055f852ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-13550" :question/problem "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 1, column 2: {{r1c2}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 2: {{r2c2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-5 & 6-2 \\\\ 6-2 & 6-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-13550/r1c1" "q-13550/r1c2" "q-13550/r2c1" "q-13550/r2c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e62b0e-adda-5d35-aeb9-961057dce8f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-13550"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72754/answer/733e1f1630e5b9bbf4c7e90deb3cf950eea43b1a87f451328dc7f3d552965eab" :answer/id #uuid "c97c84c3-c608-56b1-bf04-384879e31964" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "91" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72754/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "6392d35b-90a6-526e-b49f-ded054099625" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-72754/answer/733e1f1630e5b9bbf4c7e90deb3cf950eea43b1a87f451328dc7f3d552965eab" :answer-field/choices ["q-72754/answer/733e1f1630e5b9bbf4c7e90deb3cf950eea43b1a87f451328dc7f3d552965eab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72754" :question/id #uuid "8714b293-e1b5-58d0-b1e0-a6deda18c342" :question/math-academy-id "q-72754" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/38e096b8d57404135b7fe3244e50989e66021f94bb2275c56956d8663575b4f8.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 67.403\\,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =23.908\\,7+67.403\\,3 \\\\  & =91.311\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $91.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-72754/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72754"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-67160/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "e4d4730e-784d-5184-9134-019f0cdedc08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-67160/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f33194a-533e-5d5f-aeea-98fb2111edad" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-67160/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-67160/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-67160" :question/id #uuid "dedda1be-748d-5578-a854-6406afc3ca44" :question/math-academy-id "q-67160" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=2$, the $6$th term ${a}_{6}=22$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=288.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{6}-{a}_{1}}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{22-2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=2$, $d=4$, and ${S}_{N}=288$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 2+(N-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4+4N-4) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4N) \\\\ 288 & =2{N}^{2} \\\\ 144 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-67160/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-67160"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-24882/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "15c856a2-1456-55d7-b5b2-a5c22576cfc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-24882/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad4a00f5-1b51-5614-8cb1-f90d49d0fe81" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-24882/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-24882/answer/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-24882" :question/id #uuid "0d5a3ff6-af15-5e95-830a-b355da2ec009" :question/math-academy-id "q-24882" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{log}({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10t)$ at $t=10.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $t=10$ into the function. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10t) & =\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-24882/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afb7f5bb-1a71-5bf5-a4a0-1354256be22b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-24882"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99705/answer/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "a2ed6d89-2369-535d-945f-1639d7ec8515" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99705/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc46136-de90-5e72-8301-b5f5942256c9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-99705/answer/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-99705/answer/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99705" :question/id #uuid "2160c283-c3ec-5a1f-9e6b-bbbe6f28e2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99705" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}(100)}-\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $100$ and $1\\,000$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 1\\,000 & ={10}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}(100)}-\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000)=\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})}-\\operatorname{log}({10}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2$ and $x=3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})}-\\operatorname{log}({10}^{3})=\\frac{7}{2}-3=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-99705/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afb7f5bb-1a71-5bf5-a4a0-1354256be22b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99705"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66631/answer/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "475a2c31-42f1-50cb-a261-1edd8acd5e39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66631/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "acd6482b-f428-5998-8399-25cb88d42795" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66631/answer/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer-field/choices ["q-66631/answer/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66631" :question/id #uuid "eeafaca2-0567-55ba-95dd-d0ca684cdc15" :question/math-academy-id "q-66631" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}(\\sqrt[4]{10})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[4]{10}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{10}={10}^{1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(\\sqrt[4]{10})=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{1/4})=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-66631/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67d294d0-542a-5332-b837-256792817dde"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66631"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99719/answer/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "68bc143c-0ae4-5757-9301-c1143621dc0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99719/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "e2cbaf4a-f7a5-5f3a-951b-c7f20d653587" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-99719/answer/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-99719/answer/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99719" :question/id #uuid "cd2e08d3-b64d-5c1f-866d-cc3f34b11473" :question/math-academy-id "q-99719" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}(0.000\\,01)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $0.000\\,01$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.000\\,01={10}^{-5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(0.000\\,01)=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-5})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-5.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-5})=-5\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99719/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67d294d0-542a-5332-b837-256792817dde"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78744/answer/5b36c4b70758cba9f6fa0476faab4ecca020a862e5baaac6831d5712f6550044" :answer/id #uuid "ee202b27-89f2-5d76-8c0b-be03fefdec59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.70" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78744/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "0451953f-c9c5-59ea-9f3d-e90993c1e47f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-78744/answer/5b36c4b70758cba9f6fa0476faab4ecca020a862e5baaac6831d5712f6550044" :answer-field/choices ["q-78744/answer/5b36c4b70758cba9f6fa0476faab4ecca020a862e5baaac6831d5712f6550044"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78744" :question/id #uuid "4700c93d-5a4c-5fb9-b8c3-61a4dc38cc19" :question/math-academy-id "q-78744" :question/problem "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, find the value of $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(5x)-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2x)}{x}$ at $x=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we replace $x=2$ and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =\\operatorname{log}(5\\cdot 2)-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2\\cdot 2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}(10)-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(4)}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(4)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required value to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =1-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(4)}{2} \\\\  & \\approx 1-\\frac{0.602\\,06}{2} \\\\  & \\approx 1-0.301\\,03 \\\\  & =0.698\\,97 \\\\  & \\approx 0.70\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78744/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60270e0c-a60c-5a5f-8993-3d8362081c8f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78744"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47274/answer/9412faa23240b1f9fc19677a019f4c19aa4e7cced93be9d5ba7fb773ca1855e5" :answer/id #uuid "dbb38578-339f-547d-a018-7c880ceec33c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.408" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47274/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "c90c1f9c-f963-5c96-a354-804058c647dc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-47274/answer/9412faa23240b1f9fc19677a019f4c19aa4e7cced93be9d5ba7fb773ca1855e5" :answer-field/choices ["q-47274/answer/9412faa23240b1f9fc19677a019f4c19aa4e7cced93be9d5ba7fb773ca1855e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47274" :question/id #uuid "10395e19-686b-58f5-b08a-69774a480eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-47274" :question/problem "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, find the value of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{log}(x)-4\\operatorname{log}(2x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we replace $x=4$ and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required value to $3$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $6$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(8) \\\\  & \\approx 2(0.602\\,060)-4(0.903\\,090) \\\\  & =1.204\\,120-3.612\\,360 \\\\  & =-2.408\\,240 \\\\  & \\approx -2.408\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47274/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60270e0c-a60c-5a5f-8993-3d8362081c8f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47274"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70042/answer/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "2af7aef1-c83a-5f90-b617-ed5f9a36c70b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70042/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b1f79e3-52c1-5680-8033-9d1772967ff2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70042/answer/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-70042/answer/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70042" :question/id #uuid "08ea8cf2-f1c0-54d8-b1f4-c3ee612c64fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-70042" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x+3$ and $g(x)=-2x+1,$ find $(f+g)(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(2),$ we find $f(2)$ and $g(2),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(2)$ and $g(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =2+3=5 \\\\ g(2) & =-2(2)+1=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(2)=f(2)+g(2)=5+(-3)=2\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(x+3)+(-2x+1) \\\\  & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(2) & =-2+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-70042/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70042"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-13594/answer/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708" :answer/id #uuid "2df2d8f5-89b3-57e4-8f4a-d270b42092e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-13594/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e585b38-6c42-5ef4-ad3e-c2eee41d3fb6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-13594/answer/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708" :answer-field/choices ["q-13594/answer/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-13594" :question/id #uuid "084cf399-5cc4-5ae8-9e6f-6bbf5eb1b6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-13594" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=7x+3$ and $g(x)=2-5x,$ find $(fg)(1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(1),$ we find $f(1)$ and $g(1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =7(1)+3=10 \\\\ g(1) & =2-5(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(1)=f(1)g(1)=10\\cdot (-3)=-30\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(7x+3)(2-5x) \\\\  & =-35{x}^{2}-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(1) & =-35(1)^{2}-(1)+6 \\\\  & =-35-1+6 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-13594/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-13594"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66420/answer/bdda2450817a39aa33f63c44268d508ac25aa607d31de284a7a3098288a61525" :answer/id #uuid "7629391d-2609-5c56-8323-73c227842648" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66420/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "e28444a1-1bf8-5433-8bf8-e1265d606a0e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66420/answer/bdda2450817a39aa33f63c44268d508ac25aa607d31de284a7a3098288a61525" :answer-field/choices ["q-66420/answer/bdda2450817a39aa33f63c44268d508ac25aa607d31de284a7a3098288a61525"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66420" :question/id #uuid "7f3adebb-be1f-5f32-94a7-24221953a6e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-66420" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-4$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+2,$ find  $(fg)(-2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-2),$ we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4(-2)^{2}-4=12 \\\\ g(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+2=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-2)=f(-2)g(-2)=12\\cdot 6=72\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(4{x}^{2}-4)({x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-2) & =4(-2)^{4}+4(-2)^{2}-8 \\\\  & =64+16-8 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-66420/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66420"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-13595/answer/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "50417128-ff7a-5375-8f9b-6380919d7727" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-13595/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "60051f1b-1cc5-57b3-9d12-2baaf0da0965" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-13595/answer/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-13595/answer/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-13595" :question/id #uuid "8c2c26f6-ca4a-50fd-a1f5-f21eba02c0f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-13595" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x+2$ and $g(x)=x-5$, find $(fg)(-1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)+2=1 \\\\ g(-1) & =(-1)-5=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=1\\cdot (-6)=-6\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(x+2)(x-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+2x-10 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =(-1)^{2}-3(-1)-10 \\\\  & =1+3-10 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-13595/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-13595"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66425/answer/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "b9267840-5ce8-574d-a2fc-24a332eabb5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66425/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "61952005-9460-5d76-b16f-7f533c1dbb6b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66425/answer/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-66425/answer/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66425" :question/id #uuid "be22410d-413d-5639-ac9e-a2e4fceb3697" :question/math-academy-id "q-66425" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-6$ and $g(x)=-2x+2,$ find  $(f/g)(3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then divide the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2(3)^{2}-6=12 \\\\ g(3) & =-2(3)+2=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(3)=\\frac{f(3)}{g(3)}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{-2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(3) & =\\frac{2(3)^{2}-6}{-2(3)+2}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-66425/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66425"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66481/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df" :answer/id #uuid "972fdea6-91a0-5c04-915e-c97d847aea79" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66481/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "db91a2a4-f747-5abc-b3f2-624d8c8adf48" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66481/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df" :answer-field/choices ["q-66481/answer/d6f27b7c7939a102723fed67409856455f84c69bf3e5c2599003ca6bf26244df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66481" :question/id #uuid "935c56eb-56da-5d6e-947a-de0e34bd7c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-66481" :question/problem "Find  $(f/g)(-2)$  for $f(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+2x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(-2),$ we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2),$ and then divide the results. \n\nFirst, we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+(-2)=2 \\\\ g(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, since the denominator of a fraction can't be zero, we can't divide $f(-2)$ by $g(-2).$\n\nTherefore, the value  $(f/g)(-2)$  is undefined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-66481/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66481"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104652/answer/b78cefa051ec62e42babb0396ce415bc1c6b03c29d281d68224ab0312a503d34" :answer/id #uuid "38fc0134-3e32-5936-adb6-119a15f96bb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104652/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "b04a25bf-fa89-50dc-84d6-5b32bd7d2347" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-104652/answer/b78cefa051ec62e42babb0396ce415bc1c6b03c29d281d68224ab0312a503d34" :answer-field/choices ["q-104652/answer/b78cefa051ec62e42babb0396ce415bc1c6b03c29d281d68224ab0312a503d34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104652" :question/id #uuid "297ab20b-08c4-5fef-9db0-2144be93be03" :question/math-academy-id "q-104652" :question/problem "Given that $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}}={t}^{n},$ what is $n?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we use that $\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}={t}^{-7}$, then we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and finally, we apply the power rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{t}^{-7}} & = \\\\ {({t}^{-7})}^{1/3} & = \\\\ {t}^{(-7)\\cdot (1/3)} & = \\\\ {t}^{-7/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n=-\\frac{7}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104652/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acc85958-ad98-5ce2-912c-e2ba70d025ca"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104652"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-20276/answer/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583" :answer/id #uuid "9903e779-9ba3-5920-8450-53ab46f9cc63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20276/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "505379a5-d388-5642-8c53-555ca99c500e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-20276/answer/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583" :answer-field/choices ["q-20276/answer/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-20276" :question/id #uuid "aaff4c14-16ea-5dec-8f92-9c9a5e0030ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-20276" :question/problem "Frank has a bag with $10$ different balls. In how many ways can Frank select a set of $8$ balls from the bag, considering that the order does not matter?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ balls, so $n=10.$ We want to select $8$ of those balls from the bag, so we have $r=8.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential sets of $8$ balls is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{8} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-8)!\\,8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{2!8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot \\cancel{8!}}{2!\\cancel{8!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-20276/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-20276"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118638/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b7c4db9d-25e2-5919-b3ae-fcdeff4ae1ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118638/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "70bc7d00-d148-5978-b0f0-bbea8032d6aa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-118638/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-118638/answer/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118638" :question/id #uuid "28fafe7a-73c7-5a73-a89e-2b98b4be1492" :question/math-academy-id "q-118638" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0beb4cb3deb6bf528f399d93f4fad9e9f4ddb141f7187608bdf9a9bd047eb9a7.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{YZ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{WX}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WZ & =XZ \\\\ 2x+2 & =17-x \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-118638/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cda2e87-1a65-5075-96fd-98a8a4fd7d7a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118638"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/degree/88783d5270dc6a14ac19d9cc8b06b60d6f7179e821ab6b5c1ab252c9bb63fb0a" :answer/id #uuid "9473f7b6-a284-5fde-aeb0-9dfe495353c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/degree/2bae547cf5cfc52d7f830e7a6a3d1ae48998d5a22a8b67f527f11b87e78c3e26" :answer/id #uuid "9e18b255-f768-5dae-b54d-2ae92a5196da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quadratic" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/degree/1269e9deb93ceea04e0c4c7c2b9ec880be11902cbceb9dc006e7641f1bdc7d82" :answer/id #uuid "b7782976-559c-51db-b8f9-00d346dba3ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cubic" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/degree/4372a3f1efe2160aac9783ad7dfa22062a11be6af67e5a9c860f47ffe8df5e4f" :answer/id #uuid "8d38caf7-a6c9-5d09-b74b-d2b4b2412298" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quartic" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/degree/babb391a97ee392823c56f6aa2e097ba94c64efc8adf38de53a837ea41b9465b" :answer/id #uuid "15d3eddf-834d-5006-bbf7-cc0d476c9924" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quintic" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/degree" :answer-field/id #uuid "b750a845-1c7b-5d4f-a4ad-29faa7db7a8b" :answer-field/key "degree" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-77896/degree/4372a3f1efe2160aac9783ad7dfa22062a11be6af67e5a9c860f47ffe8df5e4f" :answer-field/choices ["q-77896/degree/88783d5270dc6a14ac19d9cc8b06b60d6f7179e821ab6b5c1ab252c9bb63fb0a" "q-77896/degree/2bae547cf5cfc52d7f830e7a6a3d1ae48998d5a22a8b67f527f11b87e78c3e26" "q-77896/degree/1269e9deb93ceea04e0c4c7c2b9ec880be11902cbceb9dc006e7641f1bdc7d82" "q-77896/degree/4372a3f1efe2160aac9783ad7dfa22062a11be6af67e5a9c860f47ffe8df5e4f" "q-77896/degree/babb391a97ee392823c56f6aa2e097ba94c64efc8adf38de53a837ea41b9465b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/terms/934306b8ac80f26cd9cb48539114b3f6c4b22fd6be0c53a52d10beee8379c2bc" :answer/id #uuid "e72d8d82-9fba-5dc8-8296-9f13d453cba1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/terms/a7d2f5d56fbebaf841eb7973920c8082766eab58af43cfba002f20e242c9afac" :answer/id #uuid "ff74270a-5fc4-5dfa-b0bf-77e73a8f2ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/terms/5b26e854af91f5a0b8490bce5fb922ef4816a8476983434504fbfa1ce6060b39" :answer/id #uuid "9f58d42d-e476-5fab-a622-86cecad4798f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/terms/3b80cca2fcc3cc9d472d176ec2508568c25acdc8173b80220c3b2d7a676cd5d5" :answer/id #uuid "2a4b604e-7684-5565-8a86-33f164b9defa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Four or more terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896/terms" :answer-field/id #uuid "d56f2c4a-4503-5de3-88ff-e2689ab3b2b2" :answer-field/key "terms" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-77896/terms/a7d2f5d56fbebaf841eb7973920c8082766eab58af43cfba002f20e242c9afac" :answer-field/choices ["q-77896/terms/934306b8ac80f26cd9cb48539114b3f6c4b22fd6be0c53a52d10beee8379c2bc" "q-77896/terms/a7d2f5d56fbebaf841eb7973920c8082766eab58af43cfba002f20e242c9afac" "q-77896/terms/5b26e854af91f5a0b8490bce5fb922ef4816a8476983434504fbfa1ce6060b39" "q-77896/terms/3b80cca2fcc3cc9d472d176ec2508568c25acdc8173b80220c3b2d7a676cd5d5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77896" :question/id #uuid "0a6f6828-8843-5046-833d-792745e1538f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77896" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial $8{t}^{2}-5{t}^{4}$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has.\n\ndegree: {{degree}}\n\nterms: {{terms}}" :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $8{t}^{2}-5{t}^{4}$ has two terms, so it is a binomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $4,$ so it is a quartic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quartic binomial." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-77896/degree" "q-77896/terms"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b8feb96-7432-5fe7-a6c7-b2d8ebe7e766"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77896"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-101625/answer/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "4de44592-363f-53b0-9452-63f02463612d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101625/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "12ffd4e0-bfb9-5b97-9e69-8e43ad3307ba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-101625/answer/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer-field/choices ["q-101625/answer/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-101625" :question/id #uuid "486b29b8-ce2e-59f6-a2b3-65c777f54464" :question/math-academy-id "q-101625" :question/problem "If $p+2=3(p-3)+6,$ then $p=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses on the right-hand-side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =3(p-3)+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3\\cdot p-3\\cdot 3+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3p-9+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must now bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $p$ on the left-hand side and $3p$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $p$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $p$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =3p-3 \\\\ p+2-p & =3p-3-p \\\\ 2 & =2p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2p-3 \\\\ 2+3 & =2p-3+3 \\\\ 5 & =2p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{2p}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}p}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-101625/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75d27dfc-1297-5045-bedd-10e171209430"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101625"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-44205/answer/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" :answer/id #uuid "9e802ab2-21bf-5bbc-9750-1cc6f48bf884" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44205/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a52611a-e49a-575e-a27a-b6d409261943" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-44205/answer/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" :answer-field/choices ["q-44205/answer/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-44205" :question/id #uuid "fcdcd9f3-7122-5f59-93b6-53cc1a94a313" :question/math-academy-id "q-44205" :question/problem "Solve for $t$ where $\\frac{1-3t}{5}=-\\frac{4+t}{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We transfer the negative into the numerator of the fraction, and proceed with cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3t}{5} & =-\\frac{4+t}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1-3t}{5} & =\\frac{-(4+t)}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1-3t}{5} & =\\frac{-4-t}{2} \\\\ (1-3t)\\cdot 2 & =5\\cdot (-4-t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-3t)\\cdot 2 & =5\\cdot (-4-t) \\\\ 2-6t & =-20-5t \\\\ 2-6t+5t & =-20-5t+5t \\\\ 2-t & =-20 \\\\ 2-t-2 & =-20-2 \\\\ -t & =-22 \\\\ -1\\cdot t & =-22 \\\\ \\frac{-1\\cdot t}{-1} & =\\frac{-22}{-1} \\\\ t & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-44205/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59230000-ef0e-56c0-ab06-17676d817d5a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-44205"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14365/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "be6ccc92-c990-5058-ada1-3e61078deb9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14365/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "aec5bcbf-664d-5504-9bb2-d2b854602e61" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-14365/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-14365/answer/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14365" :question/id #uuid "5d86f908-7737-5796-8d80-bd80a4b73f99" :question/math-academy-id "q-14365" :question/problem "If the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(7,-4)$ and $(a,b)$ is $(2,1),$\nthen $a+b=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-14365/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b6b70eb-df57-5d17-a753-645ccdf94945"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14365"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99720/answer/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "fddb9305-d180-552f-8764-a6de81b573b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99720/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "538406e0-44fb-5ffa-a87e-0c0b8a80f33e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-99720/answer/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer-field/choices ["q-99720/answer/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99720" :question/id #uuid "4c27c663-c4ba-5f94-a868-bd140e031303" :question/math-academy-id "q-99720" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}=\\frac{1}{{10}^{1/4}}={10}^{-1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99720/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67d294d0-542a-5332-b837-256792817dde"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99720"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130569/answer/59cabed12271767d3647412725d382bc72464274dc4ac46b4e7903c969156a02" :answer/id #uuid "9b977c7e-eb1a-5dec-87f2-788ad6289b79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130569/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7960ed4-5ae5-5275-b08e-12ab6f8ca413" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-130569/answer/59cabed12271767d3647412725d382bc72464274dc4ac46b4e7903c969156a02" :answer-field/choices ["q-130569/answer/59cabed12271767d3647412725d382bc72464274dc4ac46b4e7903c969156a02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130569" :question/id #uuid "13175d1d-ea3b-53e4-8a6a-5d9e08d8c7ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-130569" :question/problem "Evaluate $f(2)$, where $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =\\sqrt{2+7}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}+16 \\\\  & =3+16 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-130569/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130569"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-68957/answer/fc9c98e7e7ac444a6fdca748c0cc789b70519e05293764e2d8339ce79227882f" :answer/id #uuid "05b55f04-7cd3-5516-801f-54d823312188" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-68957/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "920bc779-3731-5cf3-8bd3-d6fec7f0b5ea" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-68957/answer/fc9c98e7e7ac444a6fdca748c0cc789b70519e05293764e2d8339ce79227882f" :answer-field/choices ["q-68957/answer/fc9c98e7e7ac444a6fdca748c0cc789b70519e05293764e2d8339ce79227882f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-68957" :question/id #uuid "4f339d72-643b-5603-8c4c-84752670bfae" :question/math-academy-id "q-68957" :question/problem "${(\\frac{6}{7})}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}={6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3-8}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-68957/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd2dc211-25cb-5975-a665-f1f6ffd52488"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-68957"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70116/answer/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "35b1c28b-4cd8-5ae3-ae71-00d05daa2f29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70116/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc207bd0-5db3-5bf4-999b-8a51dc1a7a3b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-70116/answer/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-70116/answer/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70116" :question/id #uuid "275243bf-578c-5ea8-b1eb-e4e03906be2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-70116" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{2(x-3)}{4-x}$ for $x=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We replace $x$ with $2$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-3)}{4-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(2-3)}{4-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(-1)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}(-1)}{\\cancel{2}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-70116/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75725a2d-2a8d-563f-99f1-8d071c2ff0c9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70116"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-216064/a/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "38d448d8-75d3-5040-a188-af3eb10d7682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-216064/a" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7df017-81ad-5529-9346-295e76e57b85" :answer-field/key "a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-216064/a/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-216064/a/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-216064/b/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "cfaad01c-ff25-516e-9912-c42b36d3564b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-216064/b" :answer-field/id #uuid "25bdc574-1350-5311-8164-7343ef90bab7" :answer-field/key "b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-216064/b/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-216064/b/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-216064" :question/id #uuid "50eece3f-06ca-554e-b56c-6eb5ad4a01ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-216064" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}a-\\frac{1}{2}b=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}a+\\frac{1}{3}b=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$a$: {{a}}\n\n$b$: {{b}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $15,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}a-\\frac{1}{2}b=1 & |\\times 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}a+\\frac{1}{3}b=3 & |\\times 15\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}a-2b=4 \\\\ 3a+5b=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3,$ then we will get a $-3a$ term that can cancel with the $3a$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a-2b=4 & |\\,\\times (-3) \\\\ 3a+5b=45 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3a+6b=-12 \\\\ 3a+5b=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $a$-variable, and allows us to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-3a}+6b & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}+5b & =45 \\\\ (6b+5b) & =(-12+45) \\\\ 11b & =33 \\\\ b & =\\frac{33}{11} \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $b=3$ into the first equation to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-2b & =4 \\\\ a-2(3) & =4 \\\\ a-6 & =4 \\\\ a & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $a=10,\\,b=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-216064/a" "q-216064/b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-216064"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-44298/coordinate-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "2c4ee81a-ccfb-5af4-9d7f-6b289d2cd64d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44298/coordinate-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "da5b7cc0-e4c3-528c-aa88-a10de0acf241" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-44298/coordinate-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer-field/choices ["q-44298/coordinate-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-44298/coordinate-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "ea076972-7904-5cb7-87e8-ab329a78a15c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44298/coordinate-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "59769b53-693c-54d6-bb7a-05801ddb3206" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-44298/coordinate-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-44298/coordinate-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-44298" :question/id #uuid "8c4e56e6-52fc-51ba-afd3-0b7b7237eab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-44298" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $y=15x+5.$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "At the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15x+5 \\\\ 0 & =15x+5 \\\\ -15x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{-15x}{(-15)} & =\\frac{5}{(-15)} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(-\\frac{1}{3},0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-44298/coordinate-1" "q-44298/coordinate-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63461668-4f76-52a4-8115-1ab135eef9e3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-44298"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130520/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "7eddc1dd-c230-5bc7-af48-2e6fe476b329" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130520/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "438f7e7f-aba9-5f26-8a88-a8e64341593f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-130520/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-130520/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130520" :question/id #uuid "7c33c986-dc79-5316-96bc-a5c56e151eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-130520" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b9c13f97b40699c11e5b3f4f1c77457177cf83418ce08d7a30420117b816f687.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is given by the graph above. Calculate $f(-1)+f(3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-1,$ we have $y=0.$ So, $f(-1)=0.$\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(3)=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1)+f(3)=0+(-2)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130520/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f0567d5-4622-5c34-a448-0bc043fec12f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130520"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-108192/answer/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "2f18187b-0b51-5ce0-872d-df9e45afe42e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108192/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2df93ae-3252-5ea5-9051-ef23a01b6c04" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-108192/answer/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-108192/answer/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-108192" :question/id #uuid "b7eae905-c57c-5087-add9-0cd565ddeaa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-108192" :question/problem "Stephanie is a geologist who began studying land subsidence due to a geological fault. The table below shows the land subsidence  $C,$ in centimeters, $n$ weeks after the study began. How many centimeters of subsidence were there at the beginning of the study?\n\n| $n$ (weeks) | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C$ (centimeters) | $11$ | $13.5$ | $16$ |\n\nYou may assume that the relationship between $n$ and $C$ is linear.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the number of centimeters increases by $2.5$ every $5$ weeks. In other words, the number of centimeters increases by\n\n$$\n\\frac{2.5}{5}=0.5\n$$\n\ncentimeters per week. Thus, if $A$ is the amount of subsidence at the beginning of the study, then we can model the total subsidence $C$ after $n$ weeks using the equation\n\n$$\nC=A+0.5n.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute one of the pairs of values from the table into this equation and solve for $A.$ After $n=10$ weeks the subsidence was $C=11$ centimeters. We substitute these values into the equation and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =A+0.5n \\\\ 11 & =A+0.5(10) \\\\ 11 & =A+5 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, there were $6$ centimeters of subsidence when the study began." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-108192/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "714a3b9b-e0d6-5d88-8c06-907cec122356"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108192"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66772/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "6130991a-8ab3-5716-b016-0c243c85f322" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66772/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "51107b99-a0e6-5ec3-9377-1d83c4b7a6fa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-66772/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-66772/answer/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66772" :question/id #uuid "77269f1f-6854-5aa0-b092-d17b19bb30c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-66772" :question/problem "$6{log}_{5}(100)-6{log}_{5}(4)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}(a)-{log}_{n}(b)={log}_{n}(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{5}(100)-6{log}_{5}(4) & =6({log}_{5}(100)-{log}_{5}(4)) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}(\\frac{100}{4}) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${5}^{2}=25$, then ${log}_{5}(25)={log}_{5}({5}^{2})=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n6{log}_{5}(25)=6\\cdot 2=12.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-66772/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da4785a-0124-517b-9dba-ba012853b83a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66772"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "60f285b6-518e-544a-83b3-61a07eb08a67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c2c32971-4119-5de3-be91-98e96faa25f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "2d51af7a-49e9-5c4e-b237-6a5f7b502cfb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "0909f57e-a765-5887-be02-0912931e4062" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "9eb50253-a975-5059-90ce-f2edaee71701" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "597a71cf-9864-5336-b079-cb365d18d538" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "1198c783-6d09-59c8-83de-ea02f8aaafbf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "a50c314e-f8f7-5634-8543-bfcb643fd0f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f244f4e1-13b7-5e68-bfa8-3655a8868455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-134815/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-134815/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-134815/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-134815/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-134815/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-134815/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-134815/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-134815/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-134815/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-134815" :question/id #uuid "d5d30017-4bac-5ef9-907f-2a33c3ed66b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-134815" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\nII. - The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\nIII. - This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-134815/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa97e57a-70d8-5708-8b7d-fa05604a3f88"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-134815"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "2836c62e-4424-585e-b336-1f4274344dea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "dcb2485d-327c-5235-8e3e-ceed6d3a2ef4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e3842f0e-4a4c-51b1-8fd8-b5c2cbfcf200" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b5842364-149e-5b82-ab88-a434e6e3a47a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "67487606-7811-5761-a83c-868f3a1183f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "611e0a96-0804-5d12-9945-f9de947f2ae4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "6e9ab2bb-e1b9-5170-9c76-b31d705dee86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "42d6f634-f8fc-582e-9020-d11ee4f8e8c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "07029c89-c1ae-54b6-a2f2-8eca277ca242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106090/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-106090/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-106090/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-106090/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-106090/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-106090/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-106090/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-106090/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-106090/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106090" :question/id #uuid "2b16aa53-4832-5f75-8c64-41571c8255c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-106090" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f367a4318a1b9d3c2e582f515a2c57058067ed4546f588dc30282f7dd34aac89.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-106090/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "851c841f-6e27-5f81-8a0d-0f2439ae3e61"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106090"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1fab48ab-c6dd-5c37-b4c3-b974a79cd885" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6564a1f4-1e4f-5275-86da-a58ccba15532" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1a378210-8ecf-5ba8-af29-1ab0d287301c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e2129c7c-0469-5c30-af1b-889b4fad5941" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "8e191c58-93fc-5a21-8269-2e823b43a26e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "48683ef0-cb9a-5a42-a5dc-8c5f4b4180fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "c3adeb09-5140-5fca-9700-c994dedc9c7e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "05eb3693-4523-5ff9-ae6d-14f814a75511" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2ed87d-8da5-5cf0-924f-c476dada74aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-102961/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-102961/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-102961/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-102961/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-102961/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-102961/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-102961/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-102961/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-102961/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-102961" :question/id #uuid "02ff78bf-c744-52ed-b02f-687ebfd84d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-102961" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. - The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\nII. - $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\nIII. - The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false since $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false since $f(-1)=\\sqrt[3]{-1}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-102961/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3135b492-14a6-5ccf-9b83-deecba63f7f4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-102961"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99877/answer/5d022a88a643e97abec4d01dfce48411101475c0705a98f29f3a8e3560fb0616" :answer/id #uuid "c7a975cc-631d-5d9f-a650-71675ddb4917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99877/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "26a5bf6a-c62a-5cc2-9f6b-4f19779fd5b4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-99877/answer/5d022a88a643e97abec4d01dfce48411101475c0705a98f29f3a8e3560fb0616" :answer-field/choices ["q-99877/answer/5d022a88a643e97abec4d01dfce48411101475c0705a98f29f3a8e3560fb0616"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99877" :question/id #uuid "ea0c4278-e87d-566a-b51b-71d40367ec17" :question/math-academy-id "q-99877" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4317726b2c9fbe22707377e1c713e8e8271ae95f0d818ac658da02cc81d64202.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))={lim}_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.$ \nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{3}-5(2) \\\\  & =0-10 \\\\  & =-10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99877/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38e6d549-0d5a-56e3-88b0-0b22dceb2d0d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99877"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "df46b48d-72ed-5615-8ae3-3775a22c0eea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "ebe692cb-a210-5dd0-a29a-6c7703079da9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "7c79bc7e-c0a1-5ada-92ce-10780a2dd723" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "28495b6b-7018-59ae-a633-b97302ca2b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c628cb53-41cf-5b62-9e95-80cf1a678558" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "2d84c27a-5dc9-538b-a29d-15e59042e1bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "8adb9e03-b9ff-5552-9ba6-2dd348547485" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "f7b315a6-0eca-51f5-95ae-171c360d6e22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f669dac0-ac79-533e-b8d1-4325879d1d5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78133/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-78133/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-78133/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-78133/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-78133/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-78133/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-78133/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-78133/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-78133/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78133" :question/id #uuid "3ea098b9-ba41-5103-943b-e3ce0105dfe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-78133" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/43544a1ba403055094cae54fed9772df353d3697f50888728ebee61e77b729da.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-7$\nII. - $x=-4$\nIII. - $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-7.$ Because there is a jump at $x=-7,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-7$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a break at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78133/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78133"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-6979/y/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "b08d7f70-e837-52b5-a04f-b94a8a4abbb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6979/y" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff98420d-303a-5998-a7ab-08711c468a4e" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-6979/y/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-6979/y/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-6979" :question/id #uuid "f8e64f3e-acff-51b4-ab2e-e9c313440acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-6979" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x}{1-{x}^{3}}.$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x}{1-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(3+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{3+0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-6979/y"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-6979"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-92957/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "4e352997-2274-5862-96fc-f97ff0c21c4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-92957/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "a07160fd-6040-5369-9aa1-33db8f39241d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-92957/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-92957/answer/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-92957" :question/id #uuid "5ed91a52-4c22-5f53-9a5a-98369b57e065" :question/math-academy-id "q-92957" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=4({5}^{-x}).$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4af39ad263d5fdeb3bf0a48e7306b24863fe464619218f84b4f053fdf6af3090.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the $x$-axis as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-92957/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-92957"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-43632/answer/138a325da7ef7d5536eadf4e663d6f6fef2f20544b566f5b7cf32ed784c83ee6" :answer/id #uuid "8584c7f4-aee0-51c5-8848-9809d4b3fba5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-43632/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "b83c8ac8-1b0a-5849-87e0-b94e6262669e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-43632/answer/138a325da7ef7d5536eadf4e663d6f6fef2f20544b566f5b7cf32ed784c83ee6" :answer-field/choices ["q-43632/answer/138a325da7ef7d5536eadf4e663d6f6fef2f20544b566f5b7cf32ed784c83ee6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-43632" :question/id #uuid "975f0bb3-e993-5f9b-a3ec-3b810e3cbf9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43632" :question/problem "What is the prime decomposition of  $126?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the prime factorization of a number, we can use a factor tree.\n\nFirst, we need to express $126$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $126$ is $2,$ and $126=2\\cdot 63.$ We write these factors below $126$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b4f520ebadd00c650f5172e14943f67e67bc63f8e38a1ecd3fe86a7e7ab74ad4.png)\n\nWe draw a circle around $2$ because it's prime. \n\nNext, we need to express $63$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $63$ is $3,$ and $63=3\\cdot 21.$ We write these factors below $63$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\nWe carry on like this, stopping only when all of the endpoints are prime. By doing this, we get the following: \n\nSo finally, our prime factorization is\n\n$$\n126=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot 7=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-43632/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e7e1deb-efdb-5549-8e83-ffcba093d481"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-43632"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-43634/answer/4ea30393cf763dd4925fd1ba691583e66adefa02ab932427800fc23bf7f7fcba" :answer/id #uuid "21381e4c-dbdb-5dbf-9740-7260353e0ab7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-43634/answer" :answer-field/id #uuid "97b3c1fb-965d-5446-b945-9970eb8d409a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-43634/answer/4ea30393cf763dd4925fd1ba691583e66adefa02ab932427800fc23bf7f7fcba" :answer-field/choices ["q-43634/answer/4ea30393cf763dd4925fd1ba691583e66adefa02ab932427800fc23bf7f7fcba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-43634" :question/id #uuid "6e736a28-8e89-585c-8f2b-ce912c35bf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-43634" :question/problem "What is the prime decomposition of $315?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the prime factorization of a number, we can use a factor tree.\n\nFirst, we need to express $315$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $315$ is $3,$ and $315=3\\cdot 105.$ We write these factors below $315$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/35f5150e72285c0c706af351aa9827bf7010e22fa0dd49a07569466006c2bff1.png)\n\nWe draw a circle around $3$ because it's prime. \n\nNext, we need to express $105$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $105$ is $3,$ and $105=3\\cdot 35.$ We write these factors below $105$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\nWe carry on like this, stopping only when all of the endpoints are prime. By doing this, we get the following:\n\nSo finally, our prime factorization is \n\n$$\n315=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 5\\cdot 7={3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-43634/answer"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e7e1deb-efdb-5549-8e83-ffcba093d481"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-43634"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f10e9224-203c-5056-a43b-1139e16b53e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5dde2dc4-e38c-5c9e-836a-729f2fd9d7be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "eddc8efc-da25-514e-ae6d-122bd4f49d5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "935540f8-c858-5bef-8d72-cd50516436da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "58668d09-0f3a-5dc7-9e04-380e43c43f7b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d3c896c4-9756-5b21-afd7-840dfc0de007" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "2ccadedc-ac9d-501f-ac6f-af1d32f00024" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "5aee3821-2280-5178-9380-34cbe7e976d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4271acfa-9f19-528d-b7fe-83b00c07c70b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-134130/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-134130/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-134130/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-134130/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-134130/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-134130/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-134130/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-134130/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-134130/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-134130" :question/id #uuid "e2ae6b9a-e5a8-56dc-8196-49d72b8c47d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-134130" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\nII. - There are two players aged $21.$\nIII. - None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10,$ namely, $(21,10).$ This means only one of the players has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/209759f5d70fd8587740d1da840f9da838a3c9a055a0d1e962f76c1cd6341fd3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $21,$ namely, $(21,10)$ and $(21,12).$ This means that two players are $21$ years old.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one player has fewer than $6$ years of experience. The dot below the horizontal line represents this player.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-134130/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-134130"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124498/selection/93735c42909c29cb8b75173f69468ea50351294813e53008252c0cf2395b529e" :answer/id #uuid "4d5bf00b-17e6-5bda-9736-dc067b0bef91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124498/selection/06d0beaf97c38a4cf6afec9df1f92a49e51be184562d83f7bd6a8bf64e4b8d5f" :answer/id #uuid "ec1a423d-00ae-5c18-b176-f6564918ba0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124498/selection/5bfd60fc754c8b052587279b2707468d212bd3c25139044b47761a99e7d9e646" :answer/id #uuid "5fbfd842-2bd3-5d73-a317-4d7d71c5608d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+1)!" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124498/selection/e9c27a67439004c83bac50a4f47178dcee463866cb4ff0e1e7b4ae2d6ee17b1c" :answer/id #uuid "dd27a54b-d59e-5618-9e13-1b28c5c7a7af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(n+2)!" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124498/selection/fd227ee1af32ef56f91f2648c1ddfe625b22304367b0e2c8b31ccfa608ec5041" :answer/id #uuid "f8153453-717a-5fa9-bdcd-0cb27d0e8fa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(n+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124498/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b79e6af0-433a-53d2-9e69-bef7522f0bfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124498/selection/06d0beaf97c38a4cf6afec9df1f92a49e51be184562d83f7bd6a8bf64e4b8d5f" :answer-field/choices ["q-124498/selection/93735c42909c29cb8b75173f69468ea50351294813e53008252c0cf2395b529e" "q-124498/selection/06d0beaf97c38a4cf6afec9df1f92a49e51be184562d83f7bd6a8bf64e4b8d5f" "q-124498/selection/5bfd60fc754c8b052587279b2707468d212bd3c25139044b47761a99e7d9e646" "q-124498/selection/e9c27a67439004c83bac50a4f47178dcee463866cb4ff0e1e7b4ae2d6ee17b1c" "q-124498/selection/fd227ee1af32ef56f91f2648c1ddfe625b22304367b0e2c8b31ccfa608ec5041"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124498" :question/id #uuid "71c74243-2d3a-5a86-b262-41a397c01a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124498" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n+3)!$ for any $n\\ge -1?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+3)!=(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+3)! & =(n+3)\\cdot \\overset{(n+2)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124498/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124498"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124502/selection/c6d5a4e01a2cb5035b548cdfe4bddce511853f8d4eebddf179627bd05470acaf" :answer/id #uuid "945bfc0d-31e2-5ee2-b31f-9eda3f097d12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)" :db